, for the first time, agreed to make e-books available to public library users.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/opinion/e-books-libraries-and-democracy.html', 'snippet': 'All of the Big Six publishers have, for the first time, agreed to make e-books available to public library users.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WRESTLING with my newspaper on the subway recently, I noticed the woman next to me reading a book on her smartphone. “That has to hurt your eyes,” I commented. \xa0Not missing a beat, she replied, in true New York style, “My font is bigger than yours.” She was right. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/opinion/0501OPEDtopos/0501OPEDtopos-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'E-Books and Democracy', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'E-Books and Democracy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marx, Anthony W', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hachette Book Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HarperCollins Publishers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Macmillan Publishers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Penguin Group', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Random House', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Simon & Schuster Inc', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T04:31:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony W. Marx', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Marx', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c7a3fe5-7892-592d-8004-8f005cc70cf8', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c7a3fe5-7892-592d-8004-8f005cc70cf8'}, {'abstract': 'Naoki Inose, the governor of Tokyo, apologized for comments he made recently about Istanbul, a rival city hoping to host the Olympics in 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/sports/olympics/tokyo-official-apologizes-for-remarks-about-olympics-rival.html', 'snippet': 'Naoki Inose, the governor of Tokyo, apologized for comments he made recently about Istanbul, a rival city hoping to host the Olympics in 2020.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Naoki Inose, the governor of Tokyo, apologized for comments he made recently about Istanbul, a rival city hoping to host the Olympics in 2020. Inose, who is also the chairman of Tokyo’s bid committee, said in a recent interview with The New York Times that Istanbul may be less equipped than Tokyo to host the Games. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tokyo Official Apologizes for Remarks About Olympics Rival', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tokyo Official Apologizes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Olympic Committee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istanbul (Turkey)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Inose, Naoki', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2020)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T04:59:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a935a35-5ca4-5377-ab7d-21c4f6d7bf70', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a935a35-5ca4-5377-ab7d-21c4f6d7bf70'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:30 P.M. (Sundance) NURSE BETTY (2000) Renée Zellweger, above, with Chris Rock, left, and Morgan Freeman, is Betty, a small-town waitress who suffers a nervous breakdown after she witnesses the murder of her husband (Aaron Eckhart) at the hands of two hit men (Mr. Freeman and Mr. Rock). Instead of facing reality, Betty escapes into the idealized fantasy of her favorite soap opera, “A Reason to Love,” which is set in a hospital. She travels to Los Angeles to become a nurse and tries to reunite with her imagined old flame, the fictional Dr. David Ravell (Greg Kinnear). Unfortunately, the hit men are in hot pursuit. Writing in The New York Times, Stephen Holden said that this dark comedy “takes the most hackneyed dramatic ploy of soap opera, a case of amnesia, and spins it into an irresistibly heady mixture of sadness and hilarity.” He added, “At its giddiest peaks the movie, directed by Neil LaBute, produces that shivery feeling when your heart melts, but at the same moment you want to burst out laughing.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T05:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/7644a25e-3976-5152-bd23-d504ad4165cd', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7644a25e-3976-5152-bd23-d504ad4165cd'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/news-quiz-may-1-2013/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | May 1, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T05:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taylor Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taylor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd30afa6-9533-51ad-ba85-b120f2ac49fc', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd30afa6-9533-51ad-ba85-b120f2ac49fc'}, {'abstract': 'After more than two decades of pursuing the case in court and several acquittals, lawyer for victim of riots says he won’t quit.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/lawyer-to-contest-congress-leader-sajjan-kumars-acquittal-in-1984-riots-case/', 'snippet': 'After more than two decades of pursuing the case in court and several acquittals, lawyer for victim of riots says he won’t quit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — A Delhi court acquitted the Indian Congress Party leader Sajjan Kumar Tuesday for the murder of five men during the 1984 anti-Sikh riots, but the lawyer for one of the victims said he is already planning to appeal the decision to the Delhi High Court.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/world/asia/30-anti-sikh