dn’t exactly choose to create adoptive “twins.” But we didn’t say no.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Taking Sides', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Wanted ‘Twins’', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADOPTIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Births', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Twins', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T13:52:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e77e5c-8af1-5e6c-9840-db5823db0353', 'word_count': 1070, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89e77e5c-8af1-5e6c-9840-db5823db0353'}, {'abstract': 'From the moment the ultrasound revealed two blobs in my uterus and not one, I have felt compromised.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/i-didnt-want-twins/', 'snippet': 'From the moment the ultrasound revealed two blobs in my uterus and not one, I have felt compromised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Taking Sides', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Didn’t Want Twins', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Births', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Twins', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T13:52:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Apfel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Apfel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07b7981e-f708-5e9b-af6e-e026013b3fc3', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07b7981e-f708-5e9b-af6e-e026013b3fc3'}, {'abstract': 'Contestants for the Peace First Prize, some as young as 8 years old, have devised innovative ways to fight problems like bullying and poverty in their communities.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/young-peacemakers/', 'snippet': 'Contestants for the Peace First Prize, some as young as 8 years old, have devised innovative ways to fight problems like bullying and poverty in their communities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I intended to write this column next October, but why wait? As President Obama observed last week, we’ve been through a tough time. And as the Boston Marathon bombing continues to be headline news — and gut-wrenching images continue to circulate — we need to keep in mind what behavioral scientists call the “availability heuristic” — our tendency to mistakenly estimate the probability of events based on how readily examples come to mind. A story that leaves a painful and vivid impression like this one can distort our sense of reality for a long time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Movers, With a Passion for Change', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bullies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T14:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d82b081c-ab77-56db-b7e4-2346fe5c004f', 'word_count': 1725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d82b081c-ab77-56db-b7e4-2346fe5c004f'}, {'abstract': 'Is it once again time to revise our opinion of Ulysses S. Grant?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/americas-most-reconsidered-general/', 'snippet': 'Is it once again time to revise our opinion of Ulysses S. Grant?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reputation of Ulysses S. Grant has oscillated wildly through history, almost since the day of his birth, April 27, 1822. A West Point graduate with a middling military career and a series of failed business ventures. From a midlevel command at the start of the Civil War to the very top of the military leadership at its end, followed by a two-term presidency. But from almost the moment he took office, his reputation began to steadily decline. As late as the 1950s his military success was commonly attributed to superior numbers applied with brute force. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Most Reconsidered General', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Ulysses S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chattanooga (Tenn)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vicksburg (Miss)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T14:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Leigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98ab6e92-1bfc-5213-8099-1533609e1c29', 'word_count': 1549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98ab6e92-1bfc-5213-8099-1533609e1c29'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Grieder’s new book analyzes the Lone Star state’s past, present and future.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/big-hot-cheap-and-right-erica-grieder-talks-about-texas/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Grieder’s new book analyzes the Lone Star state’s past, present and future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her new book, “Big, Hot, Cheap and Right: What America Can Learn From the Strange Genius of Texas,” Erica Grieder, an editor at Texas Monthly magazine, writes about the forces that shaped the state’s attitudes as well as its current economic growth in the face of widespread troubles in the rest of the country. In a recent e-mail interview, Ms. Grieder discussed the state’s economic “miracle,” whether the future there bodes well for Democrats and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/books/30grieder-2/30grieder-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/30/books/30grieder-2/30grieder-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/30/books/30grieder-2/30grieder-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '