'value': 'Skelos, Dean G', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-01T20:50:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21fbd0fe-b69b-5b55-9865-0eb1b97a108c', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21fbd0fe-b69b-5b55-9865-0eb1b97a108c'}, {'abstract': 'Bee pollen, venom and royal jelly are being used along with honey, and increasingly in creams and serums for the face.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/fashion/cosmetics-and-creams-from-the-hive.html', 'snippet': 'Bee pollen, venom and royal jelly are being used along with honey, and increasingly in creams and serums for the face.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The beauty buzz is all about bees these days. Honey has long been a staple in cosmetics, with Perlier and Laura Mercier making well-loved bath items that contain it. But now other products of the hive, like pollen, venom and royal jelly, are being used as well, and increasingly in creams and serums for the face. Their salubrious properties supposedly include increasing blood circulation, as well as plumping and hydrating the skin. If all else fails, at least you’ll smell sweet.  ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/02/fashion/02SPOTS_SPAN/02SPOTS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/02/fashion/02SPOTS_SPAN/02SPOTS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 501}}, {'rank': 0, '