 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3d2dc1-b766-562e-a349-0d5d6b30d128', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3d2dc1-b766-562e-a349-0d5d6b30d128'}, {'abstract': 'In what could be a prelude to a larger shakeup in player personnel, John Idzik made his first two changes to the Jets’ scouting department.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/sports/football/jets-make-changes-in-scouting.html', 'snippet': 'In what could be a prelude to a larger shakeup in player personnel, John Idzik made his first two changes to the Jets’ scouting department.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what could be a prelude to a larger shakeup in player personnel, John Idzik made his first two changes to the Jets’ scouting department. The team will not retain Michael Davis, the assistant director of college scouting, and an area scout, Joe Bommarito, who was primarily responsible for the West Coast. Idzik had retained the scouts he inherited after being hired in January to preserve continuity as the Jets prepared for last week’s draft. But with the draft over, it is likely that Idzik will revamp the Jets’ player personnel department and select his own people. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets Make Changes in Scouting', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Pro Football', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jets Make Changes in Scouting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Idzik, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bommarito, Joe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T05:20:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f809d93-9920-5d8d-8d52-c54afcfb5d60', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f809d93-9920-5d8d-8d52-c54afcfb5d60'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/news-quiz-may-2-2013/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | May 2, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T05:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taylor Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taylor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/836f79e7-a4fa-5d65-b695-c5f455739346', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/836f79e7-a4fa-5d65-b695-c5f455739346'}, {'abstract': 'The family of Chen Guangcheng, the blind Chinese dissident whose daring escape from house arrest embarrassed China, says his nephew  is not getting treatment for appendicitis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/world/asia/china-accused-of-denying-care-to-relative-of-chen-guangcheng.html', 'snippet': 'The family of Chen Guangcheng, the blind Chinese dissident whose daring escape from house arrest embarrassed China, says his nephew  is not getting treatment for appendicitis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Human rights advocates have accused the authorities in eastern China of denying urgent medical care to the jailed nephew of the blind dissident Chen Guangcheng, a move they say is aimed at punishing Mr. Chen for his continued antigovernment activism abroad after his daring escape to the United States Embassy in Beijing last year.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Accused of Denying Care to Dissident’s Imprisoned Nephew', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Dissident’s Nephew Is Said To Be Denied Prison Medical Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Guangcheng', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Kegui', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T05:38:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd9caf72-4b51-5f19-a8e4-139ed56250e9', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd9caf72-4b51-5f19-a8e4-139ed56250e9'}, {'abstract': 'Strikes and anger on International Labor Day; abortion law proposed in Ireland; a new Russian opera house; and a rugby team’s challenge.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/iht-quick-read-may-2/', 'snippet': 'Strikes and anger on International Labor Day; abortion law proposed in Ireland; a new Russian opera house; and a rugby team’s challenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWS As workers around the world observed the international Labor Day holiday with demonstrations and rallies, thousands of Greeks walked off their jobs on Wednesday in the second general strike against government austerity measures this year, shutting down tax offices, leaving state hospitals to operate with emergency employees and disrupting public transportation. The Greek protest came as workers in Asia, including Bangladeshis infuriated by the lethal collapse of a garment factory, demonstrated in cities including the capitals of Cambodia, Indonesia and the Philippines. In Istanbul, riot police officers sprayed throngs of people with water and tear gas as they gathered for a rally, defying an official ban. Niki Kitsantonis and Alan Cowell report. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/arts/artsspecial/02OPERA-QR/20130502OPERA-slide-H8OU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/01/arts/artsspecial/02OPERA-QR/20130502OPERA-slide-H8OU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/01/arts/artsspecial/02OPERA-QR/20130502OPERA-slide-H8OU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'su