: 'Use of Mortgage Interest Deduction Depends on Where You Live', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Geography Of a Deduction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Charitable Trusts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T15:45:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0f9d816-f5b3-55c9-b853-9cd01d98571a', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0f9d816-f5b3-55c9-b853-9cd01d98571a'}, {'abstract': 'The firm, Bowery Capital, plans to invest in business-oriented start-ups with a marketing or technology focus. It is led by Mike Brown Jr., who previously co-founded AOL Ventures.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/new-venture-firm-opens-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'The firm, Bowery Capital, plans to invest in business-oriented start-ups with a marketing or technology focus. It is led by Mike Brown Jr., who previously co-founded AOL Ventures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The field of enterprise technology has been particularly hot among investors recently, especially after a string of well-received public offerings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Venture Firm Opens in New York', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Codecademy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T15:46:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96fd9078-a5c4-5256-8a0b-74e224bae9ca', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96fd9078-a5c4-5256-8a0b-74e224bae9ca'}, {'abstract': 'I do not think that study means what you think it means.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/medicaid-nonsense/', 'snippet': 'I do not think that study means what you think it means.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Busy day, no additional blogging until much later. But you should be reading The Incidental Economist on the Oregon Medicaid study that’s creating a lot of fuss today. Basically, budget woes forced Oregon to allocate Medicaid access by lottery, giving a rare randomized experiment. Those who got Medicaid suffered much less financial distress and less depression; they received more preventive care; but on some (not all dimensions) their health wasn’t significantly better than those who lost out in the lottery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Medicaid Nonsense', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T15:50:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c842a30-dfd4-5f6a-9b5a-5c96d280920e', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c842a30-dfd4-5f6a-9b5a-5c96d280920e'}, {'abstract': 'Automotive voice command systems can be infuriating when they misinterpret your commands. General Motors seems to have worked out the bugs with the 2014 Chevy Impala.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/talking-to-the-2014-chevrolet-impala-g-m-s-improved-mylink/', 'snippet': 'Automotive voice command systems can be infuriating when they misinterpret your commands. General Motors seems to have worked out the bugs with the 2014 Chevy Impala.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talking to your car can be like trying to reason with a recalcitrant toddler. It often petulantly refuses to obey your commands or, worse, does the opposite of what you want. But in-vehicle voice recognition systems are gradually improving, as demonstrated by the new version of Chevrolet’s MyLink system.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking to the 2014 Chevrolet Impala: G.M.’s Improved MyLink', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For 2014 Chevy Impala, A Rebooted MyLink', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T16:11:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By John R. Quain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Quain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', '