sion to demolish its new acquisition, the 2001 American Folk Art Museum, at 45 West 53rd Street, designed by Tod Williams and Billie Tsien. Its peculiar but much-admired facade of roughened bronze captures the dark side of high-style modernism, the spaceshiplike surface reminiscent of the planet-destroying Death Star in the “Star Wars” series.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 306}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 522, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1044, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/realestate/05STREETS_SPAN/05STREETS_SPAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With a Museum for a Neighbor ...', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes | MoMA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With a Museum for a Neighbor ...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FOLK ART MUSEUM', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T22:50:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/883fdeba-73ed-59fd-81e2-e1a5ba0ff5c5', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/883fdeba-73ed-59fd-81e2-e1a5ba0ff5c5'}, {'abstract': 'Compensation growth has been relatively flat across the board in recent years. The biggest raises have been in transportation and higher education.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/02/where-the-raises-are-trucking-and-academia/', 'snippet': 'Compensation growth has been relatively flat across the board in recent years. The biggest raises have been in transportation and higher education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I spoke today with Diane Swonk, chief economist at Mesirow Financial, who mentioned that she has been keeping an eye on which industries and occupations are giving raises. Unfortunately, not many fall under that category.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where the Raises Are: Trucking and Academia', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trucks and Trucking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T22:55:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5865f082-5565-5c65-9fb5-1118d3a26b25', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5865f082-5565-5c65-9fb5-1118d3a26b25'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel Algrant’s “Greetings From Tim Buckley” is a biopic about two musicians who died young: the folk singer Tim Buckley and his folk-rocker son, Jeff Buckley.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/movies/greetings-from-tim-buckley-with-penn-badgley.html', 'snippet': 'Daniel Algrant’s “Greetings From Tim Buckley” is a biopic about two musicians who died young: the folk singer Tim Buckley and his folk-rocker son, Jeff Buckley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Biopics come in a number of flavors, though most turn out either to be hagiographies that clean up the little and big messes of famous lives, burnishing legends for maximum nostalgia, or analytical postmortems that try to put their subjects into play with history and the world. “Greetings From Tim Buckley” takes a look at two musicians who died young and were largely strangers, Tim Buckley and his son Jeff Buckley, and goes right for worship: it’s sweet, sentimental, almost inevitably touching if not especially persuasive, brushing against the thorns in each man’s life without drawing blood. Mostly it plays out as a virtual reunion tour and fantasy father-and-son encounter session.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blood Ties That Don’t Quite Bind', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood Ties That Don’t Quite Bind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Tim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Badgley, Penn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Greetings from Tim Buckley (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-02T22:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/daa323ef-ef74-5cb6-adaa-68b961978c98', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daa323ef-ef74-5cb6-adaa-68b961978c98'}, {'abstract': 'Bolivia’s most populous city is at the center of the country’s growing drug trade and an alarming crime wave.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/05/02/world/americas/20130502BOLIVIA.html', 'snippet': 'Bolivia’s most populous city is at the center of the country’s growing drug trade and an alarming crime wave.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bolivia’s most populous city is at the center of the country’s growing drug trade and an alarmin