original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/154b2584-6bea-5c7c-9026-4d378a5ef41c', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/154b2584-6bea-5c7c-9026-4d378a5ef41c'}, {'abstract': 'Tiger scat offers DNA traces that can help conserve Nepal’s struggling tiger population.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/dna-from-tiger-scat-aids-conservation-efforts-in-nepal/', 'snippet': 'Tiger scat offers DNA traces that can help conserve Nepal’s struggling tiger population.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve drawn attention periodically to the output of\xa0Kashish Das Shrestha, a talented photographer and writer who splits his time between New York City and Nepal. He’s filed an interesting new piece on the Nepal Tiger Genome Project, developed with funding from the U.S. Agency for International Development, which is using genetic tools to help conserve Nepal’s hemmed-in tiger population. Here’s my Twitter/Tumblr summary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'DNA from Tiger Scat Aids Conservation Efforts in Nepal', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tigers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wildlife Sanctuaries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Agency for International Development', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T13:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39c7608d-8b0f-5909-94c0-e1dc9c7b0ae4', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39c7608d-8b0f-5909-94c0-e1dc9c7b0ae4'}, {'abstract': 'A company in Dubai bought large amounts of computer equipment from Dell that ended up in Syria despite sanctions, documents show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/technology/dell-products-make-their-way-circuitously-to-syria.html', 'snippet': 'A company in Dubai bought large amounts of computer equipment from Dell that ended up in Syria despite sanctions, documents show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Large amounts of computer equipment from Dell have been sold to the Syrian government through a Dubai-based distributor despite strict trade sanctions intended to ban the selling of technology to the regime, according to documents obtained by The New York Times.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/subDell/subDell-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Outwitting Sanctions, Syria Buys Dell PCs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outwitting Sanctions, Syria Buys Dell PCs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dell Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BDL Gulf', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anas Hasoon Trading', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T13:23:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e68ccd1-93b5-56db-b13d-96c02ab9b429', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e68ccd1-93b5-56db-b13d-96c02ab9b429'}, {'abstract': 'D.I.Y. justice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/magazine/to-catch-an-almost-thief.html', 'snippet': 'D.I.Y. justice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I work as an accounting assistant. Our bookkeeper stole a presigned check and issued it to herself for $80,000. When she tried to deposit it, her bank did due diligence and called our company. The bookkeeper was fired immediately. When my boss and I reported this incident to the police, the officers said that — since the crime was not actually committed — we could not file a report. They suggested reporting it to the local district attorney. But my boss has been reluctant to take that next step. Six months later, he makes excuses whenever I ask him why he hasn’t gone to the D.A. I’m outraged that the bookkeeper was let go without paying any price for her actions. Should I report the crime? K. W., NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/