'The 4.21.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 4.21.13 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T13:32:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/97075d88-ae4e-5bae-97a9-160026b371fe', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97075d88-ae4e-5bae-97a9-160026b371fe'}, {'abstract': 'What the dueling judges of “American Idol” teach us about talent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/magazine/mariah-carey-nicki-minaj-and-the-battle-for-the-soul-of-pop.html', 'snippet': 'What the dueling judges of “American Idol” teach us about talent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America loves a cat fight — but characterizing the spectacular ongoing clash between the “American Idol” judges Nicki Minaj and Mariah Carey as such is like calling Godzilla’s battle with Megalon a slap fight. Anyone who has watched “American Idol” for more than a few seconds this season knows that their feud is no ratings-boosting prank; contempt of this magnitude can’t be faked. When Minaj raves over a finalist, Carey winces; when Carey coos, Minaj rolls her eyes. The two divas dared to address each other directly a few weeks ago, but their talk quickly devolved into interruptions and condescension. Producers may have recruited these two in the hope that an occasional good-natured spat would reignite the spark that went out when Simon Cowell exited the show three years ago. As it turns out, Minaj and Carey trade harmless barbs about as well as North and South Korea.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/magazine/05riff/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 85, 'width': 600, 'su