l-world experience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing Whack-a-Mole With Expansionary Austerity', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T14:06:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c2b5a16-c038-5d41-94f4-7f930af0d7c2', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c2b5a16-c038-5d41-94f4-7f930af0d7c2'}, {'abstract': 'Partisans on both sides focused on the negative in the better-than-expected jobs report, with the left blaming sequestration and the right citing regulation.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/bipartisan-agreement-blame-washington/', 'snippet': 'Partisans on both sides focused on the negative in the better-than-expected jobs report, with the left blaming sequestration and the right citing regulation.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:41 a.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bipartisan Agreement: Blame Washington', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T14:07:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wheaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b35af5ce-a1b2-57fa-af20-bf41482d902a', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b35af5ce-a1b2-57fa-af20-bf41482d902a'}, {'abstract': 'How I ended up in the bathtub with my jeans on, thinking I’d found the secret to life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/magazine/my-desperate-stupid-emotional-hunt-for-the-perfect-pants.html', 'snippet': 'How I ended up in the bathtub with my jeans on, thinking I’d found the secret to life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a kid and my mom bought me Levi’s, they were stiff and uncomfortable for weeks. Then over time and multiple washings, they’d fade the way you wanted them to and start to contour themselves to your body. They became more than your pants. They were your skin. They grew with you. They saw what you saw, absorbed your pain, your mistakes, your spills and slides. They scarred and ripped with you. They seemed to last forever. Some part of me can’t understand why I ever got rid of that pair of Levi’s that I had in seventh grade. How did I lose track of those pants?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 745}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur