it greenhouse gas emissions is “unburnable carbon” — an effort to define and then wall off the portion of the world’s still-vast reserves of coal, oil or natural gas that might, if combusted, cause unacceptably costly or dangerous climate change. The effort builds, to a large extent, on studies aiming to create a “carbon budget” for the world’s nations — divvying up the amount of emissions (and thus fuels) below that threshold. The most notable paper, published in Nature, was\xa0“Greenhouse-Gas Emission Targets for Limiting Global Warming to 2\u2009°C.” (2\u2009°C being 2 degrees Celsius). (Earlier this year, Katherine Bagley wrote a nice piece on the influence of that paper.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 531, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 531, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/blogs/dotunburnable/dotunburnable-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On ‘Unburnable Carbon’ and the Specter of a ‘Carbon Bubble’', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Capture and Sequestration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKibben, Bill', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ecuador', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:13:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d541ca45-e87b-5ceb-84be-5e716527efe1', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d541ca45-e87b-5ceb-84be-5e716527efe1'}, {'abstract': 'Vollkswagen introduces a more powerful turbo, and General Motors announces the official pace car for this year’s Indianapolis 500.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/wheelies-the-juiced-beetle-edition/', 'snippet': 'Vollkswagen introduces a more powerful turbo, and General Motors announces the official pace car for this year’s Indianapolis 500.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The Juiced Beetle Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aluminum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indianapolis 500 (Auto Race)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Cars (Compact, Subcompact and Microcars)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haslam, Jimmy III', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALCOA INC', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pilot Travel Centers LLC', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:15:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d8d5118-d17b-505b-950a-40158e919243', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d8d5118-d17b-505b-950a-40158e919243'}, {'abstract': 'David Rohde shows the consequences of raising U.S.A.I.D. budgets and cutting its staff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/beyond-war-by-david-rohde.html', 'snippet': 'David Rohde shows the consequences of raising U.S.A.I.D. budgets and cutting its staff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Luckily, David Rohde’s new book, “Beyond War: Reimagining American Influence in a New Middle East,” doesn’t live up to its name. “American influence” is too amorphous, and the “new Middle East” too broad, to be convincingly “reimagined” in 221 pages. To his credit, Rohde, a columnist for Reuters and The Atlantic, barely tries. What he does instead is expose the deep contradiction between Washington’s long experiment in slashing the civilian instruments of American power and its post-9/11 attempt to use those same instruments to remake the greater Middle East.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1325, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05BEINART/05BEINART-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Send In the Contractors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Send In the Contractors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rohde, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Agency for International Development', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:23:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Beinart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beinart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f23f96cf-e662-54a3-ae36-cefe77e80305', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f23f96cf-e662-54a3-ae36-cefe77e80305'}, {'abstract': 'This biography of William Colby, onetime director of the Central Intelligence Agency, reveals a spy who could blend in anywhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/shadow-warrior-by-randall-b-woods.html', 'snippet': 'This biography of William Colby, onetime director of the Central Intelligence Agency, reveals a spy who could blend in anywhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the Vietnam War, Bill Colby of the Central Intelligence Agency ran the Phoenix program, which set out to “neutralize” the Viet Cong by capturing or killing them. In 1972, when Colby came home to a nation that had turned against the war, his face began appearing around Washington on “Wanted” posters. He was jeered on the street and peppered with death threats. Every day at 5 a.m., he was awakened at home by the same crank caller, accosting him as a murderer and a war criminal. Colby did not bother to get his home number changed. Instead, he began to use the predawn call as an alarm clock.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 520}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05THOMAS/05THOMAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gray Man', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gray Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Evan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Randall B', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colby, William E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:23:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evan Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5be202c0-9180-5466-b6bd-1c1011894b47', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5be202c0-9180-5466-b6bd-1c1011894b47'}, {'abstract': 'Claire Messud’s latest novel depicts a thwarted artist bewitched by a boy and his parents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/the-woman-upstairs-by-claire-messud.html', 'snippet': 'Claire Messud’s latest novel depicts a thwarted artist bewitched by a boy and his parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading the title of Claire Messud’s latest novel, anyone of a literary turn of mind will immediately think of the madwoman in the attic, the 19th century’s best-known “woman upstairs.” In “Jane Eyre,” Bertha Mason was the first wife of the master of Thornfield Hall, who shut her away and, in so doing, opened the door to more than a hundred years of impassioned feminist criticism. This connection is entirely intentional, as Messud quickly makes plain. “We’re not the madwomen in the attic,” argues her “reliable,” “organized” protagonist, a teacher named Nora Eldridge, referring to unmarried women like herself, “numerous” in their 20s and 30s, “positively legion” in their 40s and 50s. “We’re the quiet woman at the end of the third-floor hallway, whose trash is always tidy, who smiles brightly in the stairwell.” Outwardly they may seem “benignant” (to use a Brontëan word), but inwardly, Nora declares, they seethe. “People don’t want to worry about the Woman Upstairs,” she reflects. “Not a soul registers that we are furious. We’re completely invisible.” In time, she will resolve to “use that invisibility, to make it burn.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 611}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1005, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2010, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Schillinger/0505-bks-Schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Significant Others', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Significant Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Messud, Claire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:23:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b75a00bc-66e1-53d9-b645-7e321ba22be3', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b75a00bc-66e1-53d9-b645-7e321ba22be3'}, {'abstract': 'The centenary of H. G. Wells’s book that started a hobby and continues to inspire war-game designers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/basic-training.html', 'snippet': 'The centenary of H. G. Wells’s book that started a hobby and continues to inspire war-game designers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you happened to visit the Royal Military Academy in Sandhurst, England, in April, you may have spotted neat ranks of red- and blue-coated soldiers converging on the front lines of a grassy battlefield — a miniature battlefield, that is, peppered with soldiers just over two inches tall. Their commanding officers were gathered to celebrate the centenary of the book that gave birth to their hobby and, in so doing, spawned a family tree that has since branched out to include tabletop role-playing pursuits and big-budget video-game extravaganzas.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 753, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 753, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBWALLACE1/05SUBWALLACE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Basic Training', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Basic Training', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wells, H G', 'rank': 3, 'major': '