rganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4fffe68-7b39-5a07-8275-c1e7a22e4763', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4fffe68-7b39-5a07-8275-c1e7a22e4763'}, {'abstract': 'Ramona Ausubel’s fantastical story collection traces a cycle of transformation: from love to conception to gestation to birth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/a-guide-to-being-born-by-ramona-ausubel.html', 'snippet': 'Ramona Ausubel’s fantastical story collection traces a cycle of transformation: from love to conception to gestation to birth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good owner’s manual takes the unexpected into account; the more extreme the eventuality the better. In the same spirit, “A Guide to Being Born,” by Ramona Ausubel, the author of “No One Is Here Except All of Us,” deals with the desolation and surreptitious thrills that come to us through ownership of a body and its accompanying life. Or ownership of a life and its accompanying body: these tales allow the body to adjust as needed. In “Chest of Drawers,” a prospective father feels acutely underinvolved in preparations for the birth of his daughter, and so he develops chest cavities that can be pulled open and pushed shut, filling them with tiny babies from the toy shop. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05OYEYEMI/05OYEYEMI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 955, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05OYEYEMI/05OYEYEMI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 955}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05OYEYEMI/05OYEYEMI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 644, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 