ragraph': 'Helen Oyeyemi, who reviews Ramona Ausubel’s short-\xadstory collection, “A Guide to Being Born,” this week, can’t remember when she started writing. It was surely long before her first novel, “The Icarus Girl,” was published in 2005, when Oyeyemi was still a 20-year-old student at Cambridge University, and she has published three more in the eight years since.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 963, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 963}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 638, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1276, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Upfront/0505-bks-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oyeyemi, Helen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:30:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6759f32-6f19-519f-8434-79d8ed525419', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6759f32-6f19-519f-8434-79d8ed525419'}, {'abstract': 'An extensive exhibition at Mana Contemporary in Jersey City provides a larger perspective behind the Japanese photographer’s erotic works.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/on-view-nobuyoshi-araki/', 'snippet': 'An extensive exhibition at Mana Contemporary in Jersey City provides a larger perspective behind the Japanese photographer’s erotic works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobuyoshi Araki is one of the world’s most prolific photographers. In his 73 years, he has compulsively documented daily life in his native Japan and produced more than 450 books and collector’s editions of film-only prints (a feat made more impressive today by the comparative ease of digital photography). His work appears in permanent collections at the Tate Modern and the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art and has been exhibited extensively throughout Europe and Asia. But in the United States, Araki is far less well known, and there’s no real mystery why: a substantial portion of his prints feature naked women who, at times, are tightly bound using a method of erotic, Japanese rope-tying called kinbaku-bi.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Language of Nobuyoshi Araki', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARAKI, NOBUYOSHI', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Rinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d7f8c95-228e-510d-b21e-f76ec16d11d5', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d7f8c95-228e-510d-b21e-f76ec16d11d5'}, {'abstract': '“Lucky Guy,” starring Tom Hanks, has recouped its capitalization costs after eight weeks of performances.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/lucky-guy-pays-off-for-investors/', 'snippet': '“Lucky Guy,” starring Tom Hanks, has recouped its capitalization costs after eight weeks of performances.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Lucky Guy,” the Broadway play by Nora Ephron, which stars Tom Hanks as the newspaper columnist Mike McAlary, has recouped its $3.6 million capitalization after only eight weeks’ worth of performances, the show’s producers said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Lucky Guy’ Pays Off for Investors', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanks, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Broadway (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:32:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64fdcb96-6575-5a9a-8ef8-41da7d1df0c8', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64fdcb96-6575-5a9a-8ef8-41da7d1df0c8'}, {'abstract': 'Computers and the Internet can open up new worlds for the elderly.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/helping-seniors-learn-new-technology/', 'snippet': 'Computers and the Internet can open up new worlds for the elderly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several years ago Garrison Phillips, a retired actor, bought himself a Dell computer. He soon recognized that he didn’t know what to do with it. “I needed instruction,” he told me in an interview conducted — perhaps surprisingly — by e-mail. “The technology of the Internet, cell and iPhones was like a foreign language.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Helping Seniors Learn New Technology', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:33:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/316b16c7-2742-500d-bd83-646f1f069105', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/316b16c7-2742-500d-bd83-646f1f069105'}, {'abstract': 'Net income dropped 85 percent, to $4.7 million, largely on costs attributable to buyouts, severance and restructuring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/business/media/washington-post-profits-drop-sharply.html', 'snippet': 'Net income dropped 85 percent, to $4.7 million, largely on costs attributable to buyouts, severance and restructuring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Washington Post Company on Friday reported a sharp decline in profit in the first quarter of the year, largely from losses in its newspaper and education divisions.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/business/Post/Post-thumbL