since she began writing her atmospheric mysteries about Thomas Pitt, a detective in late-Victorian London, and his highborn wife, Charlotte. But, strange as it may seem for such an unflinching observer of the criminal justice system, until now Perry hadn’t trained her exacting eye on the subject of rape. MIDNIGHT AT MARBLE ARCH (Ballantine, $27) makes up for that lapse with a vengeance. Without losing her grip on the refined language appropriate to the day, Perry has written a sweeping and scandalous exposé of sexual brutality amid the upper classes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 931, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 931}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 1180, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-CRIME/0505-bks-CRIME-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Death and the Maidens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death and the Maidens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Anne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Talton, Jon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morrell, David', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chesterton, R B', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/410d4384-4103-5658-af43-1f2551d183ee', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/410d4384-4103-5658-af43-1f2551d183ee'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Dimock’s narrator, haunted by the treatment of political detainees, writes to a former Bush administration official.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/george-anderson-by-peter-dimock.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Dimock’s narrator, haunted by the treatment of political detainees, writes to a former Bush administration official.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can fiction make us better people? Harold Bloom’s “Invention of the Human” posits that Shakespeare’s plays forever altered human consciousness. Blakey Vermeule, the author of “Why Do We Care About Literary Characters?,” answers her title’s question this way: “We use them to sort out basic moral problems or to practice new emotional situations. . . . We use them in place of statistics as tools.” Reading Peter Dimock’s second novel, “George Anderson: Notes for a Love Song in Imperial Time,” didn’t automatically make me a better person — but it did make me aware that, as an American, I should try to be one, even if my attempts are probably doomed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 587}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1839, 'width': 1880, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Julavits/0505-bks-Julavits-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dark Side', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dark Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Julavits, Heidi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimock, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heidi Julavits', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heidi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Julavits', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d73cc15-0dcf-5e52-9228-82212d539657', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d73cc15-0dcf-5e52-9228-82212d539657'}, {'abstract': 'A mysterious Fabergé egg encodes secrets at the heart of Danielle Trussoni’s second tale of evil fallen angels..', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/angelopolis-by-danielle-trussoni.html', 'snippet': 'A mysterious Fabergé egg encodes secrets at the heart of Danielle Trussoni’s second tale of evil fallen angels..', 'lead_paragraph': 'Angels do in fact walk among us, and they are not at all to be trifled with. Or at least that’s the alarming state of affairs at the heart of Danielle Trussoni’s 2010 best-selling novel, “Angelology.” Called Nephilim, these angels — or, more properly, angel-human hybrids — are the descendants of traitorous, fallen rebel angels. No haloed guardians, these: they want nothing less than dominion over mankind. Set against them are the angelologists, a secret global group dedicated to thwarting the Nephilim’s unholy ambitions. Readers flocked to Trussoni’s first novel (she’s also the author of a 2006 memoir, “Falling Through the Earth”) for its mix of fantasy and religious conspiracy, as well as for the improbable romance between its characters: the art historian Verlaine and Evangeline, an innocent young nun.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 645}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 953, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1906, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/0505-bks-Wecker/0505-bks-Wecker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cracked', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cracked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trussoni, Danielle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:43:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Wecker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wecker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff24c00-1bf8-5ba1-aa43-708782380720', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff24c00-1bf8-5ba1-aa43-708782380720'}, {'abstract': 'In early-20th-century America, two powerful men were engaged in a contest to possess the world’s most remarkable watch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/books/review/a-grand-complication-by-stacy-perman.html', 'snippet': 'In early-20th-century America, two powerful men were engaged in a contest to possess the world’s most remarkable watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because our species evolved in stressful environments, the drive to grapple with scarcity is deeply embedded in our psychology. Severe and chronic shortages can of course wear people down. But just as the development of a sound immune system requires exposure to lots of microbes, sound mental health seems to require the challenges presented by moderate amounts of privation. An old cartoon by Gahan Wilson captures the idea. Two middle-aged angels, newly arrived in heaven, are playing harps while perched on a cloud. One, his face a mask of dismay, asks the other, “You mean this goes on forever?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 841}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1251, 'width': 893, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/books/review/05SUBFRANK/05SUBFRANK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Face Value', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Face Value', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Robert H', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Watches and Clocks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perman, Stacy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T15:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'ar