ole': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b685e04-6c3b-5959-990d-81f8377838d4', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b685e04-6c3b-5959-990d-81f8377838d4'}, {'abstract': 'The question is not whether Chicago can come up with a new offensive or defensive scheme. It is whether the Bulls will have enough healthy players to overcome a flawed Nets team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/sports/basketball/bulls-are-low-on-bodies-and-perhaps-out-of-ideas.html', 'snippet': 'The question is not whether Chicago can come up with a new offensive or defensive scheme. It is whether the Bulls will have enough healthy players to overcome a flawed Nets team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bulls guard Nate Robinson was vomiting into a\xa0trash can\xa0in the\xa0third quarter of Game 6 against the Nets\xa0on Thursday night, but at least he was able to take the court. Forward Luol Deng, who missed the game, said on his Twitter account that he had a spinal tap Thursday to check for meningitis. Point guard Kirk Hinrich remained out with a bruised left calf. Taj Gibson and Joakim Noah played through debilitating leg injuries. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 889, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 889}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1812, 'width': 1223, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/sports/04dribble3/04dribble3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bulls Out of Gas and Maybe Ideas', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bulls Out of Gas and Maybe Ideas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bulls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Nets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T18:40:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beckley Mason', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beckley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mason', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f069e99-167a-5200-8028-5b8a2f96f293', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f069e99-167a-5200-8028-5b8a2f96f293'}, {'abstract': 'The iconic sculptor’s new show plays with the distinction between indoors and outdoors.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/on-view-betty-woodman/', 'snippet': 'The iconic sculptor’s new show plays with the distinction between indoors and outdoors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sculptor Betty Woodman is known for her exuberant riffs on the vase — boldly colored and patterned ceramic pieces which she has said “could be functional, but I really want them to be considered works of art.” Woodman’s new exhibition, “Windows, Carpets and Other Paintings,” which opens May 7 at Salon 94 Freemans in New York City, includes more remixes of the vessel form, but also works that examine the relationship between indoors and outdoors. In “Of Botticelli,” a site-specific mural that fills an entire wall of the gallery, ceramic fragments represent columns wrapped in vines and crowned by vases, hinting at an unseen garden view. Other pieces, which combine ceramics with painting on canvas, hang on the walls and are framed to look like windows. Still others sit on the floor like carpets. And in “Wallpaper,” Woodman recycles 90 cast-off bits of clay — pieces cut away during the making of her ceramics — by glazing them and arranging them on a wall, where they will be free to visitors for the taking.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Betty Woodman’s Playful Ceramics', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodman, Betty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T18:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/487f3804-3fe3-5842-9a8a-fa41d9abe497', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/487f3804-3fe3-5842-9a8