'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/097361aa-369e-5319-a11f-6a09e404441f', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/097361aa-369e-5319-a11f-6a09e404441f'}, {'abstract': 'In “All of a Sudden,” at Abrons Arts Center, Jack Ferver refers to both Elizabeth Taylor and Elizabeth Berkley in his usual relentlessly campy, self-reflexive style.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/arts/dance/all-of-a-sudden-by-jack-ferver-at-abrons-arts-center.html', 'snippet': 'In “All of a Sudden,” at Abrons Arts Center, Jack Ferver refers to both Elizabeth Taylor and Elizabeth Berkley in his usual relentlessly campy, self-reflexive style.', 'lead_paragraph': '“All of a Sudden,” the title of the new Jack Ferver show that opened at Abrons Arts Center on Thursday, is a phrase you might use in describing a signature dynamic of any Jack Ferver production. One minute, everything is campy, outrageous, ridiculous, fake, not to be taken seriously. And then — all of a sudden — someone is slapped or spit upon, or real tears seem to flow, and the pain beneath is exposed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04FERVER/04FERVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04FERVER/04FERVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 622}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04FERVER/04FERVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 988, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04FERVER/04FERVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1976, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 's