{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Golan Heights', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T22:11:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6316356-2508-58bc-9ac8-40f9cf81fa3d', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6316356-2508-58bc-9ac8-40f9cf81fa3d'}, {'abstract': 'Performing at Carnegie Hall, the American Symphony Orchestra presented a program of little-known works by Hungarian composers whose careers were roiled by the rise of the Nazis and World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/arts/music/american-symphony-orchestra-presents-hungary-torn.html', 'snippet': 'Performing at Carnegie Hall, the American Symphony Orchestra presented a program of little-known works by Hungarian composers whose careers were roiled by the rise of the Nazis and World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time has shown that no subject or context is too complex or provocative for the conductor Leon Botstein to investigate in his programming for the American Symphony Orchestra, nor any topic so clear-cut that he will not muddy the waters by incorporating elements likely to prove provocative. “Hungary Torn,” presented on Thursday night at Carnegie Hall, offered a textbook example of Mr. Botstein’s professorial zeal for dislodging convention.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1196, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04ORCHESTRA/04ORCHESTRA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Dark Time in Hungary, Remembered', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Dark Time In Hungary, Remembered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SYMPHONY ORCHESTRA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botstein, Leon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T22:12:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d31a4ed0-1fe1-58cd-b7be-ab16ffa9e7e0', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d31a4ed0-1fe1-58cd-b7be-ab16ffa9e7e0'}, {'abstract': 'The larger-than-life German artist, famed for his apocalyptic postwar landscapes, softens up a bit in “Morgenthau Plan,” a new show at the Gagosian Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/03/artifacts-the-dinosaur-in-anselm-kiefer/', 'snippet': 'The larger-than-life German artist, famed for his apocalyptic postwar landscapes, softens up a bit in “Morgenthau Plan,” a new show at the Gagosian Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In person, Anselm Kiefer is much more self-mocking and relaxed than his enormous, heart-of-darkness German history paintings and titanic lead or concrete sculptures might suggest. On Thursday morning, clad in white yoga pants, a dark shirt and thin black flip-flops, the 68-year-old artist showed a light spirit as he walked through “Morgenthau Plan,” his exhibition opening tonight at the Gagosian Gallery on West 21st Street. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts | The Dinosaur in Anselm Kiefer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kiefer, Anselm', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gagosian Gallery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-03T22:13:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Yablonsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yablonsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a275ed8-9710-5064-a700-a2f590f1965c', 'word_count': 1314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a275ed8-9710-5064-a700-a2f590f1965c'}, {'abstract': 'At Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening, the pianist Richard Goode performed three sonatas that a resigned, accepting Beethoven wrote late in his career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/04/arts/music/richard-goode-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'At Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening, the pianist Richard Goode performed three sonatas that a resigned, accepting Beethoven wrote late in his career.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Resignation, what a wretched resource! Yet it is all that is left to me,” Beethoven wrote in 1801, despairing at his encroaching deafness and other physical ailments. A sense of resignation and acceptance pervades the triptych of sonatas that he completed in 1822, given majestic, profound readings by the pianist Richard Goode on Wednesday evening at Carnegie Hall. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04GOODE/04GOODE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04GOODE/04GOODE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04GOODE/04GOODE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/04/arts/04GOODE/04GOODE-superJumbo