_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Job Market', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eilene Zimmerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eilene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/14ea89bc-4b89-5c52-9bcb-d163c6dfdd2f', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14ea89bc-4b89-5c52-9bcb-d163c6dfdd2f'}, {'abstract': 'The C.E.O. of L.L. Bean has worked at the company for 30 years. “Every time I felt I was at the peak of the learning curve,” he says, “the company gave me more to do.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/05/jobs/ll-beans-chief-bringing-a-family-perspective.html', 'snippet': 'The C.E.O. of L.L. Bean has worked at the company for 30 years. “Every time I felt I was at the peak of the learning curve,” he says, “the company gave me more to do.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’M the second-youngest of six brothers. My dad owned a packaging company in Stratford, Conn., and most of my brothers worked there at some point, along with our aunts and uncles. I never did. Dad was selling the business by the time I reached my midteens, and he wanted me to get some experience elsewhere. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/business/05-BOSS/05-BOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/05/business/05-BOSS/05-BOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 778}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/05/business/05-BOSS/05-BOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None,