nk': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-06T01:05:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbfc4238-57d1-5227-b5b1-26374bf72aa7', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbfc4238-57d1-5227-b5b1-26374bf72aa7'}, {'abstract': 'A growing number of states and cities are working to keep ex-offenders with minor convictions from being shut out of the job market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/06/opinion/job-fairness-for-ex-offenders.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of states and cities are working to keep ex-offenders with minor convictions from being shut out of the job market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sixty-five million Americans have criminal records that might cause them to be denied jobs, even for arrests or minor convictions that occurred in the distant past. Last year, the federal Equal Employment Opportunity Commission reaffirmed a longstanding ruling that it was illegal to screen out employees unless the offense was directly related to the job. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Unfair Barrier to Employment', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Unfair Barrier to Employment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ex-Convicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-06T01:05:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fa9124d-1a31-5e22-8400-0aa1b26d39f3', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fa9124d-1a31-5e22-8400-0aa1b26d39f3'}, {'abstract': 'California’s new law is a sign that enlightened lawmaking is possible in American politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/06/opinion/real-gun-control-from-sacramento.html', 'snippet': 'California’s new law is a sign that enlightened lawmaking is possible in American politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A continent removed from Washington’s shameful resistance to new gun controls, California has just enacted a law that will speed up the confiscation of firearms from an estimated 20,000 people who bought them legally but were later disqualified because of a conviction for a violent crime, a finding of mental illness or a restraining order for domestic violence. The law, signed Wednesday by Gov. Jerry Brown after passage by the Democrat-controlled Legislature, is a sign that enlightened lawmaking unhindered by gun lobby scare tactics and Capitol Hill filibustering is possible in American politics. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Real Gun Control From Sacramento', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Real Gun Control From Sacramento', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}]