, 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea2081d0-a68a-531e-b09e-dc03d086ea25', 'word_count': 1503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea2081d0-a68a-531e-b09e-dc03d086ea25'}, {'abstract': '“The Girl I Left Behind Me” conjures a vanished novelty of the theater, the cross-dressing female performers who once fascinated large audiences in England and America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/06/theater/reviews/the-girl-i-left-behind-me-at-59e59-theaters.html', 'snippet': '“The Girl I Left Behind Me” conjures a vanished novelty of the theater, the cross-dressing female performers who once fascinated large audiences in England and America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Strange as it may seem in these days of increasingly blurred gender norms, watching a woman dressed as a man singing songs of love for women may actually be a more exotic entertainment today than it was a century ago. In “The Girl I Left Behind Me,” a lovely show that opened on Sunday night at the 59E59 Theaters, the gorgeously gifted singer Jessica Walker and the writer Neil Bartlett conjure a vanished novelty of the theater, the cross-dressing female performers who once fascinated large audiences in England and America in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/arts/girl/girl-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 832, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/06/arts/girl/girl-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 832}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i