 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a woodcut map by Heinrich Bünting dating from 1581 that shows the world as a clover, with Europe, Asia and Africa as the leaves, and Jerusalem at the center, confirming its position as the emotional and spiritual nexus of the great monotheistic cultures. On Wednesday evening the Cathedral Church of St. John the Divine hosted a concert by its Cathedral Choir and the outstanding instrumental ensemble Rose of the Compass, offering the musical equivalent of the clover map. The fascinating and well-researched program encompassed Coptic and Armenian chant, Sephardic songs, Sufi melodies and Thomas Tallis’s “Lamentations of Jeremiah” on the historic destruction of Jerusalem. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07great-1/07great-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thu