y don’t speak English.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is pretty great.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zero to Hacking in Five Months', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-06T20:01:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andre Behrens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andre', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Behrens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7283111a-00d8-5301-8161-d7eedbb1affa', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7283111a-00d8-5301-8161-d7eedbb1affa'}, {'abstract': 'Like the heroine of her well-received new novel, “The Flamethrowers,” Rachel Kushner is drawn to worlds she does not completely understand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/books/rachel-kushner-author-of-the-flamethrowers.html', 'snippet': 'Like the heroine of her well-received new novel, “The Flamethrowers,” Rachel Kushner is drawn to worlds she does not completely understand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — On a recent morning the novelist Rachel Kushner stood in the parking garage of the Petersen Automotive Museum, where the Green Monster, a lean, turbo-jet-powered vehicle used to set land speed records, was displayed in a roped-off corner. In her new, rapturously reviewed novel, “The Flamethrowers,” set in the 1970s, the narrator finds herself driving just such a vehicle on the Bonneville Salt Flats in Utah, where those records are set.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/07/arts/07kushner/07kushner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'arti