cy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clancy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27138262-f1e0-5df7-ae7b-005aca70e0b9', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27138262-f1e0-5df7-ae7b-005aca70e0b9'}, {'abstract': 'Warren E. Buffett rejects the notion that Berkshire Hathaway will go the way of Teledyne. | Bank of America has reached a $1.7 billion agreement with the bond insurer MBIA. | A crowd of young men gathered on Monday in Union Square in Manhattan to buy and sell bitcoins. | The most recent episode of “Mad Men” provided a lesson in ad agency deal-making, circa 1968.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/pondering-a-berkshire-without-buffett/', 'snippet': 'Warren E. Buffett rejects the notion that Berkshire Hathaway will go the way of Teledyne. | Bank of America has reached a $1.7 billion agreement with the bond insurer MBIA. | A crowd of young men gathered on Monday in Union Square in Manhattan to ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Berkshire Hathaway annual meeting, Warren E. Buffett rejected the notion that the company would go the way of Teledyne, a conglomerate that was broken up after its leader, Henry E. Singleton, stepped down as chief executive. Mr. Singleton, who might be called the Buffett of his time, decided Teledyne had become too big and unwieldy for a single manager to effectively oversee and expand, Andrew Ross Sorkin writes in the DealBook column. A breakup of Berkshire, “I’m convinced would create a poorer result,” Mr. Buffett said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pondering a Berkshire Without Buffett', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T11:53:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c808097d-8952-5c5c-b7d0-40b94dcd9635', 'word_count': 1918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c808097d-8952-5c5c-b7d0-40b94dcd9635'}, {'abstract': 'The company announced on Tuesday that it would offer half of the 31-story tower for sale or lease.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/hold-until-8-am-tuesday-verizon-moving-1100-workers-from-lower-manhattan-to-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'The company announced on Tuesday that it would offer half of the 31-story tower for sale or lease.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Verizon announced on Tuesday that it would move 1,100 employees out of its headquarters and switching center at 140 West Street in Lower Manhattan and offer half of the 31-story tower for sale or lease.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/blogs/20130507Verizon1/20130507Verizon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/06/blogs/20130507Verizon1/20130507Verizon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/blogs/20130507Verizon1/20130507Verizon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/blogs/20130507Verizon1/20130507Verizon1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1275, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capti