dline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T16:58:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e38682d-4c32-5fb4-8d53-c81b502b47a5', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e38682d-4c32-5fb4-8d53-c81b502b47a5'}, {'abstract': 'Am I compromising my own financial future for the sake of starry-eyed idealism? Would my approach work in your business?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/why-i-would-rather-pay-my-employees-too-much/', 'snippet': 'Am I compromising my own financial future for the sake of starry-eyed idealism? Would my approach work in your business?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thank you to everyone who commented on my last post, “Why I Pay What I Pay,” about the performance I expect from workers at different wage rates. I was surprised by the number of comments, but I guess that any discussion of pay is going to push someone’s button. That said, I’d like to respond to a couple of points that were raised and also make a few new ones.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why I Would Rather Pay My Employees Too Much', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T17:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1b1a3d1-a5df-56e3-bbe9-7484b001a7b5', 'word_count': 1496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1b1a3d1-a5df-56e3-bbe9-7484b001a7b5'}, {'abstract': 'The low-cost British airline EasyJet has replaced its airport check-in desks with bag-drop desks, following its competitor Ryanair.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/limited-options-for-airline-check-in/', 'snippet': 'The low-cost British airline EasyJet has replaced its airport check-in desks with bag-drop desks, following its competitor Ryanair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back when air travel was new and glamorous, the check-in desk, where an elegant air hostess would greet you, was a delightful way to start your journey. Nowadays, it is more often the site of overwhelmed agents contending with long lines of frustrated travelers.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Limited Options for Airline Check-In', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trending: Limited Options for Airline Check-In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'easyJet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ryanair Holdings PLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T17:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Brennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4fad835-afb5-5166-9af8-9f67dd8ab8b1', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4fad835-afb5-5166-9af8-9f67dd8ab8b1'}, {'abstract': 'The Google Now app anticipates what information you might want and puts it on your phone screen before you ask for it, which can seem wonderfully prescient or intrusively creepy, but there is an easy adjustment if you don’t like the feeling that Google is watching your every move.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/how-to-tell-google-now-to-stop-peeking/', 'snippet': 'The Google Now app anticipates what information you might want and puts it on your phone screen before you ask for it, which can seem wonderfully prescient or intrusively creepy, but there is an easy adjustment if you don’t like the feeling that G...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It used to be that every new phone was heralded as “the iPhone killer.” Now the preoccupation is with “the Siri killer,” the app that will replace the sometimes frustrating Siri personal assistant on the iPhone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Tell Google Now to Stop Peeking', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T17:07:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/256a81f7-2c6c-50ac-aedb-4265df87748e', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/256a81f7-2c6c-50ac-aedb-4265df87748e'}, {'abstract': 'N.B.A. players mimic each others’ celebrations and wander over the line between respect and disrespect.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/in-the-n-b-a-celebrate-at-your-own-risk/', 'snippet': 'N.B.A. players mimic each others’ celebrations and wander over the line between respect and disrespect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Celebrations in the N.B.A. exist at the margins. They might not be as rigorously scrutinized as touchdown dances in the N.F.L., but there are consequences. When LeBron James stared down Jason Terry after knocking him to the floor on an alley-oop, he received a technical foul. When Kevin Durant topped off a slam in transition with a throat slash, the league fined him $25,000. But their transgressive nature is what gives these gestures their power. This goes double when they’re taken from an opponent, as when the Knicks’ Iman Shumpert stole Jason Terry’s flight plan and broke out Terry’s signature “JET” celebration after draining a 3-pointer in the Knicks’ Game 6 win over the Celtics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the N.B.A., Celebrate at Your Own Risk', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay-Z', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value':