'images/2013/05/06/learning/BowenLN/BowenLN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': "Guest Post | ‘Sliding Into Homelessness': A Student Essay and Teaching Resources", 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T19:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bed1462-4b61-5f92-b2cf-c63136002a0d', 'word_count': 2950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bed1462-4b61-5f92-b2cf-c63136002a0d'}, {'abstract': 'Try your hand at making a recipe for fried chicken, then take part in this month’s Recipe Lab video chat with Norman King, a lifelong Southerner and a food editor at Southern Living.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/the-art-of-frying/', 'snippet': 'Try your hand at making a recipe for fried chicken, then take part in this month’s Recipe Lab video chat with Norman King, a lifelong Southerner and a food editor at Southern Living.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recipe Lab, a monthly feature, invites you to cook with the Dining section. We’ll publish a standout recipe from a new cookbook for you to try; the next week, the book’s author will join Julia Moskin and readers in a live video chat about the recipe and cooking techniques. This month we’re making Southern fried chicken from “The Way to Fry,” by Norman King; you can watch the video chat here on Diner’s Journal on Wednesday, May 15, at 8 p.m. Eastern. If you’d like to appear in the video chat, more details are here.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Frying', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Memo to Modern Cooks: Fear Not the Fryer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T19:29:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca2e267d-90e7-50ac-af04-bce1301b2322', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca2e267d-90e7-50ac-af04-bce1301b2322'}, {'abstract': 'Eggplant’s tendency toward bitterness makes it a difficult pairing.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/sweet-and-spicy-or-assertive-wines-with-eggplant/', 'snippet': 'Eggplant’s tendency toward bitterness makes it a difficult pairing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eggplant is often described as a polarizing food, one that people either love or hate. I’m the counterexample. I like it well enough, unless it’s overcooked, which emphasizes the mushy texture and slight bitterness that seem to repel so many. That tendency toward bitterness, especially when combined with the spinach in Mary McCartney’s dish of eggplant wraps, makes it a difficult pairing. I think of reds first, but nothing too tannic, which would clash with the bitterness. The sweet and spicy fruit of a lighter-weight zinfandel would work well. So might a lively Sicilian red like a nero d’Avola or a cerasuolo di Vittoria. Assertive whites would work well, too, like a sauvignon blanc, even a flamboyantly styled example, or a good, dry chenin blanc, like a Vouvray or Savennières.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sweet and Spicy or Assertive Wines With Eggplant', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And To Drink...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggplants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T19:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3b773d6-a70a-568f-a2b1-a7d67acd9932', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3b773d6-a70a-568f-a2b1-a7d67acd9932'}, {'abstract': 'The author remembers her late father’s superior garlic, grown in the upper Hudson Valley.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/the-garden-he-left-behind/', 'snippet': 'The author remembers her late father’s superior garlic, grown in the upper Hudson Valley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My dad John and I started growing garlic about 20 years ago. I purchased my planting stock at a harvest festival, John through mail order. Eventually I came to admit his variety was much nicer than ours for both marketing and culinary purposes. He’d always announce sometime in April that it was up and about a foot high. Even though dad lived south of us in the upper Hudson Valley, my husband Jim and I assumed he was just embellishing, and we’d smirk to each other. He was always positive and optimistic, whether gardening, dealing with three different cancer episodes or rooting for Philadelphia sports teams. I finally grew enough garlic from his stock to completely replace my varieties. And yes, it is quick to emerge in the spring, gaining good height early. This year’s is already up. It looks good — a summer harbinger of the grilling season ahead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Garden He Left Behind', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Garlic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River Valley (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T19:32:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clotilde Hryshko', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clotilde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hryshko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f842d06-cd7c-5fb4-8731-9022683ec43e', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f842d06-cd7c-5fb4-8731-9022683ec43e'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the decline in weekly hours worked shows a big drop for retail, which may be influenced by the coming health mandate. Trucking and temp help services, however, have increased the length of the average worker’s weekly hours.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/shorter-hours-but-not-for-truckers-and-temps/', 'snippet': 'A look at the decline in weekly hours worked shows a big drop for retail, which may be influenced by the coming health mandate. Trucking and temp help services, however, have increased the length of the average worker’s weekly hours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the more disappointing data points in last Friday’s jobs report was the decline in average weekly hours worked, which fell to 34.4 hours in April from 34.6 hours in March. It’s not clear what was behind the decline, which occurred in multiple industries across the private sector. (The monthly jobs report does not include the length of the workweek for government workers.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shorter Hours, but Not for Truckers and Temps', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temporary Employment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trucks and Trucking', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-07T19:32:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26ea57d6-aca0-576b-971f-c40abb0e3fc5', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26ea57d6-aca0-576b-971f-c40abb0e3fc5'}, {'abstract': 'A federal judge ruled that A.I.G. could pursue a fraud claim of more than $7 billion for losses after the federal government rescued it in 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/business/ruling-clears-way-for-aig-suit-against-bank-of-america.html', 'snippet': 'A federal judge ruled that A.I.G. could pursue a fraud claim of more than $7 billion for losses after the federal government rescued it in 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A California judge has opened the door for the American International Group to pursue a fraud claim of more than $7 billion against Bank of America for losses it suffered on mortgage securities sold under duress after the federal government rescued A.I.G. in 2008. \xa0 ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'sou