ajor': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T00:59:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Rohde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0321a07-1c33-50df-84cf-735bd1ca6938', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0321a07-1c33-50df-84cf-735bd1ca6938'}, {'abstract': 'The wisdom, and pleasure, of getting children interested in cooking early on.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/07/kitchen-little/', 'snippet': 'The wisdom, and pleasure, of getting children interested in cooking early on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you argue, as I frequently do, that cooking has the potential to help us deal with many of our dietary problems, you often elicit a kind of “I don’t care, I hate cooking” response in addition to the expected “I don’t have time.” And that’s fine; cooking isn’t the only route to eating better, and besides, those who hate cooking, or can’t make the time for it, may be lucky enough to have someone else cook for them. As long as they wash the dishes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kitchen Little', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T01:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'ra