ted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f21e14dc-af99-56f2-8b60-0d1ecf73ae06', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f21e14dc-af99-56f2-8b60-0d1ecf73ae06'}, {'abstract': 'The workshop at Parsons the New School for Design was to be given by John Galliano, formerly the chief designer at Christian Dior, who was dismissed in 2011 after going on an anti-Semitic tirade in a bar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/nyregion/parsons-school-for-design-cancels-visit-by-galliano.html', 'snippet': 'The workshop at Parsons the New School for Design was to be given by John Galliano, formerly the chief designer at Christian Dior, who was dismissed in 2011 after going on an anti-Semitic tirade in a bar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A four-day workshop and discussion that the controversial fashion designer John Galliano was to lead at Parsons the New School for Design has been canceled, the Manhattan school announced on Tuesday evening. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Designer Won’t Hold Workshop at Parsons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Designer Won’t Hold Workshop At Parsons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galliano, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaminer, Ariel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parsons the New School for Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T04:39:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9a8838c-636a-576e-aecc-12ecc117b609', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9a8838c-636a-576e-aecc-12ecc117b609'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (TCM) DON’T SAY NO UNTIL I FINISH TALKING: THE STORY OF RICHARD D. ZANUCK (2013) Laurent Bouzereau chronicles the life of Richard D. Zanuck (1934-2012), the Oscar-winning producer, through recollections of his childhood on the 20th Century Fox lot as the son of the studio head Darryl F. Zanuck and his wife, the actress Virginia Fox. When Richard had the chance to produce his first film, “Compulsion,” starring Orson Welles, he said his father told him: “You know I had it a lot easier than you will have it. You have to overcome the circumstances of your birth. You carry baggage with you — I am the baggage you carry.” When Darryl Zanuck was installed as chairman of the studio in the 1960s, he asked his son for a list of possible names to be appointed president of the company. Richard, above, submitted a piece of paper that read, simply, “Me.” Films like “The Sound of Music” (1965), “Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid” (1969), “M*A*S*H” (1970) and “The French Connection” (1971) followed. But films like “Doctor Dolittle” (1967) and “Hello, Dolly” (1969) resulted in debt, and just before Christmas 1970, Richard was fired by his father. Shortly afterward his father also lost his job. This documentary, with Steven Spielberg as an executive producer, is followed at 9:45 by “Driving Miss Daisy” (1989), the Oscar best picture winner starring Jessica Tandy as a Southern widow and Morgan Freeman as her driver; it was the first movie produced by the Zanuck Company, which Richard and his wife, Lili Fini Zanuck, founded in 1988. “Cocoon” (1985), about a group of elderly people rejuvenated by aliens, follows at 1:15 a.m., and “Compulsion” (1959), Richard Fleischer’s take on the Leopold-Loeb case, at 3:30.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5feff57-d322-59d1-81a2-38c1d8c69b69', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5feff57-d322-59d1-81a2-38c1d8c69b69'}, {'abstract': 'North Korea finds itself losing critical support as the sources of foreign currency that maintain its military ambitions show signs of drying up.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/a-cash-chokehold-on-north-korea-gets-tighter/', 'snippet': 'North Korea finds itself losing critical support as the sources of foreign currency that maintain its military ambitions show signs of drying up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — The noose is tightening on North Korea. And for the leaders in Pyongyang, two developments in recent days suggest that the country’s ravenous thirst for foreign currency, which fuels its military ambitions, may be much harder to satisfy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/08/global-home/08rdv-kim-nkorea/08rdv-kim-nkorea-articleLarge.jpg',