 extramarital affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tuesday night’s special election in South Carolina’s First Congressional District was won by a Republican, Mark Sanford, the former South Carolina governor whose political career was imperiled in 2009 after he disclosed an extramarital affair. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sanford and the Electoral Effect of Sex Scandals', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, House of Representatives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert Busch, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanford, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T10:45:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/926409cb-7ac4-5358-b647-e4c6874ec538', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/926409cb-7ac4-5358-b647-e4c6874ec538'}, {'abstract': 'The presidents of the United States and Mexico met precisely not to talk about any contentious issues.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/how-obama-and-pena-nieto-evade-thorny-issues-between-the-united-states-and-mexico/', 'snippet': 'The presidents of the United States and Mexico met precisely not to talk about any contentious issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — Presidential visits are events designed for photo ops, controlled situations and speeches of little substance. They are the nice side of diplomatic relations;\xa0 the real negotiations happen before and after, behind closed doors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Speak Yet Say Nothing', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pena Nieto, Enrique', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T10:46:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlos Puig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Puig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41a37913-4207-5e79-bbcc-a019ac3b9264', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41a37913-4207-5e79-bbcc-a019ac3b9264'}, {'abstract': 'The Powerocks Tarot is about the same as a stack of credit cards and can charge an iPhone more than half way.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/the-tarot-sees-a-full-battery-in-your-future/', 'snippet': 'The Powerocks Tarot is about the same as a stack of credit cards and can charge an iPhone more than half way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Backup batteries usually trumpet their capacity, but capacity results in size: they are sometimes as large as a paperback book. The Powerocks Tarot, though, trumpets its size, because it’s just about the same as a stack of credit cards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/03/business/03gw-tarot/03gw-tarot-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tarot Sees a Full Battery in Your Future', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T10:49:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f626e83d-e8a9-55d1-bde0-ee96be38d24b', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f626e83d-e8a9-55d1-bde0-ee96be38d24b'}, {'abstract': 'How does BRAC, the world’s largest NGO, educate more than 1.25 million of the world’s poorest children for free — and do a better job than government?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/where-private-school-is-not-a-privilege/', 'snippet': 'How does BRAC, the world’s largest NGO, educate more than 1.25 million of the world’s poorest children for free — and do a better job than government?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the United States, private school is generally a privilege of the rich. But in poorer nations, particularly in Africa and South Asia, families of all social classes send their children to private school. The private schools within reach of the poor, however — usually a single classroom a woman runs in her house — are not a big improvement. But two school systems — Bridge International Academies in Kenya and BRAC in Bangladesh — are offering something different: they are making decent education accessible to the world’s poorest on a giant scale.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Private School Is Not a Privilege', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BRAC (Bangladesh)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bridge International Academies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangladesh', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T11:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47bd9f3d-ba13-55af-91ae-84eb21e34382', 'word_count': 1807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47bd9f3d-ba13-55af-91ae-84eb21e34382'}, {'abstract': 'I didn’t want to go into debt learning business principles so that I could one day hope to build Fashioning Change; I wanted to build Fashioning Change.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/how-we-keep-our-mentors-and-investors-on-board/', 'snippet': 'I didn’t want to go into debt learning business principles so that I could one day hope to build Fashioning Change; I wanted to build Fashioning Change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I came up with the idea for Fashioning Change, I had no idea what it meant to create a technology start-up. I just knew that I wanted to create something that would rival big fashion brands and e-commerce sites like Amazon, and I knew there was a lot I needed to learn. So I sought out resources to help me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How We Keep Our Mentors and Investors on Board', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mentors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ressi, Adeo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Founder Institute Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T11:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Herrera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d32c1a29-b593-56c8-8f1a-209b3431e24b', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d32c1a29-b593-56c8-8f1a-209b3431e24b'}, {'abstract': 'An endangered-species murder mystery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/magazine/who-would-kill-a-monk-seal.html', 'snippet': 'An endangered-species murder mystery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Hawaiian monk seal has wiry whiskers and the deep, round eyes of an apologetic child. The animals will eat a variety of fish and shellfish, or turn over rocks for eel and octopus, then haul out on the beach and lie there most of the day, digesting. On the south side of Kauai one afternoon, I saw one sneeze in its sleep: its convex body shuddered, then spilled again over the sand the way a raw, boneless chicken breast will settle on a cutting board. The seals can grow to seven feet long and weigh 450 pounds. They are adorable, but also a little gross: the Zach Galifianakises of marine mammals.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/magazine/12seals1/12seals1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Would Kill a Monk Seal?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Law & Order: Endangered-Species Unit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seals (Animals) and Sealing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T11:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Mooallem', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mooallem', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd28d24-c952-5617-9f34-60eefd638bc4', 'word_count': 7789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd28d24-c952-5617-9f34-60eefd638bc4'}, {'abstract': 'Once a year, someone would say, “Maybe we should do some training.”', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/if-practice-makes-perfect-why-dont-companies-practice-more/', 'snippet': 'Once a year, someone would say, “Maybe we should do some training.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his terrific book “Outliers,” Malcolm Gladwell writes about the importance of practice and, specifically, the necessity of completing 10,000 hours of practice in a particular field to become proficient. This makes sense to me. Everything I did before starting in business taught me that practice was essential to achieving success. In school, I had two or three exams each semester, but I went to class every day. Moreover, I had voluminous amounts of homework each night to refine my skills in a particular subject. In football, there were 10 games in a season, but nearly 100 practices.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If Practice Makes Perfect, Why Don’t Companies Practice More?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burkhart, Bryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T11:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bryan Burkhart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bryan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burkhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97ca99f3-00f2-532f-8d80-9603cf2a1d95', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97ca99f3-00f2-532f-8d80-9603cf2a1d95'}, {'abstract': 'Automated teller machines use software to read and process deposited checks.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/qa-scanning-checks-at-the-atm/', 'snippet': 'Automated teller machines use software to read and process deposited checks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q.& A.: Scanning Checks at the A.T.M.', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scanning Checks\r\nAt the A.T.M.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scanning Devices', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-08T11:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50681765-f54c-579a-8dc5-848dfeb5f3ba', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50681765-f54c-579a-8dc5-848dfeb5f3ba'}, {'abstract': 'The lightweight BoostBloc backup batteries from Etón provide power for mobile devices when electric outlets are lacking.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/backup-power-in-the-palm-of-your-hand/', 'snippet': 'The lightweight BoostBloc backup batteries from Etón provide power for mobile devices when electric outlets are lacking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Demand for electronic devices seems to be growing faster than the number of outlets available to charge them. Hoping to provide power to those in need, Etón has introduced a series of portable backup batteries called BoostBloc.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia'