:49+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Peter Bohler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bohler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/9753343d-7918-5a5e-84a6-ab90374302ca', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/9753343d-7918-5a5e-84a6-ab90374302ca'}, {'abstract': 'A forthcoming show at the Hammer Museum spotlights A. Quincy Jones’s innovations in indoor-outdoor design.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/08/now-showing-california-modern/', 'snippet': 'A forthcoming show at the Hammer Museum spotlights A. Quincy Jones’s innovations in indoor-outdoor design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Los Angeles architect A. Quincy Jones (1913-79) was equally at home designing middle-class housing developments as he was creating luxurious dwellings for celebrities like Gary Cooper and wealthy couples like Walter and Leonore Annenberg. This month, a retrospective examines his extraordinary body of work, which is as influential in its own way as that of better-known West Coast Modernists like Charles Eames and Richard Neutra. The show, at the Hammer Museum in Los Angeles, spotlights Jones’s innovations in indoor-outdoor design and includes photos and original blueprints of a number of his notable projects, like the colorful, airy Burbank headquarters of Warner Bros. Records (above) — an embodiment of the California dream if ever there was one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California Modern', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hammer Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 