largely thanks to a rise in the value of its investments.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/carlyles-earnings-barely-rise-in-1st-quarter/', 'snippet': 'The Carlyle Group’s earnings rose less than 1 percent in the first quarter, the firm reported on Thursday. The small gain came largely thanks to a rise in the value of its investments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The private equity giant Carlyle Group reported on Thursday that earnings rose less than 1 percent the first quarter, a gain that came largely thanks to a rise in the value of its investments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carlyle’s First-Quarter Profit Barely Budged', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group LP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T11:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f276621-cdc4-5b12-80a1-87bb1904ca83', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f276621-cdc4-5b12-80a1-87bb1904ca83'}, {'abstract': 'The hedge fund faithful gather in Las Vegas and New York for the two big conferences of the year. | The firm responsible for sending checks to homeowners as compensation for foreclosure abuses has erred again. | Tesla will stop detailing how many reservations exist for its cars. | More debate on whether Jamie Dimon should remain both C.E.O. and chairman of JPMorgan Chase.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/hedge-funds-step-into-the-limelight/', 'snippet': 'The hedge fund faithful gather in Las Vegas and New York for the two big conferences of the year. | The firm responsible for sending checks to homeowners as compensation for foreclosure abuses has erred again. | Tesla will stop detailing how many ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In two separate events in Las Vegas and New York on Wednesday, the hedge fund faithful gathered to hear investment advice and rub elbows with some of the biggest names in the industry. The newer of the two events, the SkyBridge Alternatives Conference, or SALT, is a Wall Street schmooze-fest in Sin City that lets the world’s richest investors mingle with funds peddling their services, DealBook’s Peter Lattman reports. Overseeing the star-studded event is Anthony Scaramucci, a hedge fund impresario, or, as Mr. Lattman puts it, a P.T. Barnum in a Ferragamo tie. “In a gilded industry that has preferred to stay below the radar, Mr. Scaramucci embraces the white-hot center of it all.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hedge Funds Step Into the Limelight', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T11:56:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30db99f0-5722-5147-a842-3e4a38f1084f', 'word_count': 1962, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30db99f0-5722-5147-a842-3e4a38f1084f'}, {'abstract': 'When Cambodia’s powers that be aren’t burnishing their credentials, they invent them out of whole cloth.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/in-cambodia-real-politicians-give-themselves-phony-degrees/', 'snippet': 'When Cambodia’s powers that be aren’t burnishing their credentials, they invent them out of whole cloth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHNOM PENH — Late last month, the prime minister of Cambodia awarded his long-serving finance minister the title “Kitti Setah Banditt,” which roughly translates to “Glorious Economist of Genius” and is the supposed equivalent of a doctoral degree. It was a variation on an obscure “Glorious Genius” honor that seemingly came out of nowhere in 2011, when it was given to two senior leaders of the governing Cambodian People’s Party as well as the prime minister’s wife. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Of Post Doctors and Glorious Geniuses', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hun Sen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T11:58:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Wallace', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wallace', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b0f6c71-806c-541e-8b3b-9146f4c31026', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b0f6c71-806c-541e-8b3b-9146f4c31026'}, {'abstract': 'It was supposed to be younger people who were driving the restaurant business these days while baby boomers slinked back to their kitchens, but it has not worked out that way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/booming/baby-boomer-restaurant-visits-are-up.html', 'snippet': 'It was supposed to be younger people who were driving the restaurant business these days while baby boomers slinked back to their kitchens, but it has not worked out that way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They were counting us out. They figured it was the millennials who were going to come in and save their bacon. But at the end of the day who’s filling up the booths at T.G.I. Fridays? Who’s getting the Lumberjack Slam breakfast at Denny’s? Who’s dialing up for pizza because, really, who feels like cooking tonight?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 702}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 876, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1499, 'width': 1281, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/06/booming/06question-booming-restaurants/06question-booming-restaurants-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Am I Dining Out So Often?', 'kicker': 'Question Mark', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T12:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f4a734d-0a3a-570b-b4ac-eb6ddda2f148', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f4a734d-0a3a-570b-b4ac-eb6ddda2f148'}, {'abstract': 'In detail and dialogue “The Great Gatsby” largely adheres faithfully to F. Scott Fitzgerald’s novel, but Baz Luhrmann has colored it lavishly with his feel for the era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/movies/the-great-gatsby-interpreted-by-baz-luhrmann.html', 'snippet': 'In detail and dialogue “The Great Gatsby” largely adheres faithfully to F. Scott Fitzgerald’s novel, but Baz Luhrmann has colored it lavishly with his feel for the era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best way to enjoy Baz Luhrmann’s big and noisy new version of “The Great Gatsby” — and despite what you may have heard, it is an eminently enjoyable movie — is to put aside whatever literary agenda you are tempted to bring with you. I grant that this is not so easily done. F. Scott Fitzgerald’s slender, charming third novel has accumulated a heavier burden of cultural significance than it can easily bear. Short and accessible enough to be consumed in a sitting (as in “Gatz,” Elevator Repair Service’s full-text staged reading), the book has become, in the 88 years since its publication, a schoolroom staple and a pop-cultural totem. It shapes our increasingly fuzzy image of the jazz age and fuels endless term papers on the American dream and related topics.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shimmying Off the Literary Mantle', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shimmying Off The Literary Mantle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luhrmann, Baz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiCaprio, Leonardo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maguire, Tobey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulligan, Carey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edgerton, Joel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Isla', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay-Z', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Great Gatsby (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Great Gatsby (Movie)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T12:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c9b616c-6936-5ab4-a124-899fb1eeac08', 'word_count': 1123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c9b616c-6936-5ab4-a124-899fb1eeac08'}, {'abstract': 'The only thing we have to fear is lack of fear itself.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/the-moral-equivalent-of-space-aliens/', 'snippet': 'The only thing we have to fear is lack of fear itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To almost everyone’s surprise, Japan — Japan! — has emerged as the advanced country most willing to break with austerian orthodoxy and try a combination of aggressive monetary and fiscal stimulus. The verdict on Abenomics is, of course, still out, although early indications are good. But how did this happen?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Moral Equivalent of Space Aliens', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T12:02:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04feca29-bc63-5d54-937e-7e3a66581107', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04feca29-bc63-5d54-937e-7e3a66581107'}, {'abstract': 'What to do if you inherit 700 dolls; a scientific, seven-minute workout; more errors in checks meant to aid homeowners and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/thursday-reading-what-to-do-with-a-700-doll-inheritance/', 'snippet': 'What to do if you inherit 700 dolls; a scientific, seven-minute workout; more errors in checks meant to aid homeowners and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York 