 '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Relations and Publicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohn, Gary D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T21:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1281b11b-7ecb-5545-84f5-028165a644d2', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1281b11b-7ecb-5545-84f5-028165a644d2'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Mahin’s Stella Barra, which opened last night, offers a mix of seasonal items and of course, his exceptional pies.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/09/food-matters-a-cant-miss-artisanal-pizzeria-in-hollywood/', 'snippet': 'Jeff Mahin’s Stella Barra, which opened last night, offers a mix of seasonal items and of course, his exceptional pies.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’m cooking a little more aggressively,” said Jeff Mahin of his new restaurant, Stella Barra, which opened last night in Hollywood. The chef had garnered praise for his artfully crafted, Neapolitan-style pizzas at Stella Rosa in Santa Monica, but he found himself wanting a kitchen where he could experiment. “Santa Monica is relaxed, a real pizza town,” he explained. “Hollywood is more open and edgy, so I needed to expand the menu.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Matters | A Can’t-Miss Artisanal Pizzeria in Hollywood', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pizza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hollywood (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-09T21:32:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f739df09-7d8a-5987-be87-19282cf6a2ec', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f739df09-7d8a-5987-be87-19282cf6a2ec'}, {'abstract': 'Yoga among dolphins? A museum of neon? Of course! This is Las Vegas, where a weekend can include almost anything.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/travel/36-hours-in-las-vegas.html', 'snippet': 'Yoga among dolphins? A museum of neon? Of course! This is Las Vegas, where a weekend can include almost anything.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From a tourism perspective, Las Vegas is ever the chameleon. New restaurants, shows, clubs and hotels are constantly reinventing Sin City with the aim of getting repeaters back to the tables. Big construction projects continue, and there are currently two competing Ferris wheels under construction on the Strip. But lately, developments have eschewed kitschy copies of foreign landmarks like an Egyptian pyramid in favor of celebrating Las Vegas’s own swinging style, as indicated by two new downtown museums. Yes, traffic still snarls the Strip, but a new terminal at McCarran International Airport has eased congestion for fliers.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/travel/12HOURS_SPAN-sf/12HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/travel/12HOURS_SPAN-sf/12HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 