ncy as he prepares to step down as director. Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Michael S. Schmidt write that “instead of coasting into retirement, Mr. Mueller will spend the final months answering tough questions about how the bombing suspects slipped away.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Signals', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2465630e-4bd3-5160-9fa1-2e4803bb8e16', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2465630e-4bd3-5160-9fa1-2e4803bb8e16'}, {'abstract': 'Some specialized units for dementia care do manage to improve on traditional nursing home care, recent studies suggest.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/dementia-care-units-may-improve-care-studies-suggest/', 'snippet': 'Some specialized units for dementia care do manage to improve on traditional nursing home care, recent studies suggest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By their euphemistic names, ye shall know them: The Memory Care Unit. The Reminiscence Neighborhood. Homestead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Studies Find Mixed Results for Dementia Units', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date'