ark-mazzetti.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Mazzetti argues that the C.I.A. gave up its traditional functions as it embraced manhunting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to remember, but for the last quarter of the 20th century, the C.I.A. took no part in assassinating bad guys. How the agency transformed itself into “a killing machine, an organization consumed with manhunting,” is the subject of Mark Mazzetti’s fascinating, trenchant, sometimes tragicomic account, “The Way of the Knife.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 254, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 254}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 865, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12KAPLAN/12KAPLAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Killing Machine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Killing Machine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Way of the Knife: The CIA, a Secret Army, and a War at the Ends of the Earth (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzetti, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Targeted Killings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:33:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fred Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fred', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8eea75a-42e6-5978-9f18-d17a32c947ce', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8eea75a-42e6-5978-9f18-d17a32c947ce'}, {'abstract': 'Arnon Grunberg’s hero wants to throw his daughter the best party ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/tirza-by-arnon-grunberg.html', 'snippet': 'Arnon Grunberg’s hero wants to throw his daughter the best party ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pop quiz: you are writing an insightful, strange, funny and dark investigation into a family of four coming together for the younger daughter’s high school graduation. Who will be your point-of-view character?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 635}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 968, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1754, 'width': 1657, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12HUTCHINS/12HUTCHINS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Graduate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Graduate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grunberg, Arnon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Hutchins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hutchins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf2caf40-9d53-51cc-aa36-5fbfc7bfe818', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf2caf40-9d53-51cc-aa36-5fbfc7bfe818'}, {'abstract': 'Albert Camus’s writing on Algeria reveals both hope and dread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/algerian-chronicles-by-albert-camus.html', 'snippet': 'Albert Camus’s writing on Algeria reveals both hope and dread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Albert Camus was that rare writer who enjoyed a celebrity usually reserved for rock stars, even while being taken very seriously as an artist and a public intellectual. His first novel, “The Stranger,” published in 1942 when he was 29, made him famous; after that came more novels, plays, lyrical essays, short stories, two major philosophical works (“The Myth of Sisyphus” and “The Rebel”), and countless newspaper articles, editorials and political commentaries. In 1957, at age 44, he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, the second-youngest writer ever to receive the prize (after Rud\xadyard Kipling). Less than three years later, in January 1960, he was killed in a car crash; people the world over mourned.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 789}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1558, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULEIMAN/12SULEIMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Postcolonial', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Postcolonial', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Camus, Albert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaplan, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:34:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Rubin Suleiman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': 'Rubin', 'lastname': 'Suleiman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c3c7692-d381-5771-ad5d-2dff456c538a', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c3c7692-d381-5771-ad5d-2dff456c538a'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of short stories by the Russian writer Nikolai Leskov, a contemporary of Dostoyevsky and Tolstoy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/nikolai-leskovs-enchanted-wanderer-and-other-stories.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of short stories by the Russian writer Nikolai Leskov, a contemporary of Dostoyevsky and Tolstoy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past century, the Russian writer Nikolai Leskov (1831-95) has been repeatedly rediscovered. As Richard Pevear points out in the introduction to “The Enchanted Wanderer” — a collection of 17 stories vibrantly translated by Pevear and Larissa \xadVolokhonsky — this has happened at least three times. The first rediscovery took place during Leskov’s own lifetime: in the 1880s, after he had suffered for years “an almost total critical neglect,”the young Chekhov hailed his oeuvre, declaring him “a mixture of an elegant Frenchman and a defrocked priest.” Leskov, Chekhov continued, “was considerable.”In the 1920s, the formalist critic Boris Eikhenbaum discerned Leskov’s influence in the work of such diverse writers as Andrei Bely, Isaac Babel and Maxim Gorky, who frankly “declared himself Leskov’s disciple.”And again, at the hands of critics like Walter Benjamin and Irving Howe, Leskov was the subject of yet another revival. “To go back to certain earlier writers is to regain a sense of human possibility,” Howe wrote. “To go back to Leskov is to regain a sense of the passion, sometimes the joy, that can be part of the human enterprise.” Now, thanks to this delicious new collection, the time is once again ripe for Leskov’s resurgence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12MESSUD/12MESSUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12MESSUD/12MESSUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 