rld, so these four picture books come along just in time to help kids do some exploring — from the backyard to overseas to underseas to outer space — and they all let the visuals lead the charge.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 318, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 318}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1086, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1/0512-bks-Lichtenheld1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Over, Under and Through', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Over, Under and Through', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Lieshout, Maria', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blechman, Nicholas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Viva, Frank', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyd, Lizi', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:37:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Lichtenheld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lichtenheld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f55c80c6-8f11-5806-9c47-bfb2395ccc2a', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f55c80c6-8f11-5806-9c47-bfb2395ccc2a'}, {'abstract': 'In far-flung regions, two girls’ dreams are impeded by family obligations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/the-vine-basket-and-a-girl-called-problem.html', 'snippet': 'In far-flung regions, two girls’ dreams are impeded by family obligations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Middle-grade and adult fiction share territory largely ignored by young adult literature: the exploration of countries and eras beyond the borders of the contemporary teenager’s navel. Two new middle-grade novels, “The Vine Basket,” by Josanne La Valley, and “A Girl Called Problem,” by Katie Quirk, are set, respectively, in a Uighur community in Central Asia and rural Tanzania in the late 1960s. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Endangered Daughters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Endangered Daughters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quirk, Katie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'La Valley, Josanne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:38:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Sue Park', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': 'Sue', 'lastname': 'Park', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ea3db58-3571-5b6b-b1ee-0b20c0f2546b', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ea3db58-3571-5b6b-b1ee-0b20c0f2546b'}, {'abstract': 'A girl pushed into musical excellence confronts her own desires.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/the-lucy-variations-by-sara-zarr.html', 'snippet': 'A girl pushed into musical excellence confronts her own desires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leopold Mozart shuttled Wolfgang Amadeus around Europe for years, parading his son in front of endless royalty to secure commissions and performances. More than two centuries later, a bullying stage father helped propel the Chinese pianist Lang Lang to superstardom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SCHWEITZER/12SCHWEITZER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 518, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SCHWEITZER/12SCHWEITZER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 518, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SCHWEITZER/12SCHWEITZER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SCHWEITZER/12SCHWEITZER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SCHWEITZER/12SCHWEITZER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Playing’s the Thing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Playing’s the Thing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zarr, Sara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:39:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a5be4d5-6ec5-5829-9e8e-abd8f19da57f', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a5be4d5-6ec5-5829-9e8e-abd8f19da57f'}, {'abstract': 'Norse mythology and the occult intrude on the modern world in these tales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/house-of-secrets-and-lokis-wolves.html', 'snippet': 'Norse mythology and the occult intrude on the modern world in these tales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Want to write a middle-grade fantasy adventure series? It’s easy! First, conjure up a plucky, prickly team of three — children who have to learn to trust one another and work together. Make the stakes really high; saving the world is always good. Use lots of wisecracking humor. Ensure the parents are absent (dead, missing, away — you’ll figure it out). Invoke classic themes and figures from folklore and mythology, but don’t bother becoming slavishly wedded to them. Be sure to include an intellectually or physically butt-kicking girl. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stuff of Legend', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Stuff of Legend', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ingall, Marjorie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vizzini, Ned', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marr, M A', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Armstrong, K L', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Columbus, Chris', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:39:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Ingall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ingall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0e4a7a6-cbce-5f26-aed9-343e614ed2c1', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0e4a7a6-cbce-5f26-aed9-343e614ed2c1'}, {'abstract': 'A multigenerational series by the creator of the Baby-Sitters Club.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/better-to-wish-by-ann-m-martin.html', 'snippet': 'A multigenerational series by the creator of the Baby-Sitters Club.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For some girls who grew up in suburbia in the ’80s and early ’90s, the defining cultural touchstone of the time might be New Kids on the Block or “Saved by the Bell.” For me, it’s “The Baby-Sitters Club,” Ann M. Martin’s block\xadbuster middle-grade series.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULLIVAN/12SULLIVAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 536, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULLIVAN/12SULLIVAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULLIVAN/12SULLIVAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULLIVAN/12SULLIVAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12SULLIVAN/12SULLIVAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rites of Passage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rites of Passage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, J Courtney', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Ann M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:39:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Courtney Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Courtney', 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a45f02f2-0787-5af7-b97c-c9feb8f54219', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a45f02f2-0787-5af7-b97c-c9feb8f54219'}, {'abstract': 'Three friends must lay a ghost to rest in Holly Black’s latest novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/doll-bones-by-holly-black.html', 'snippet': 'Three friends must lay a ghost to rest in Holly Black’s latest novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Holly Black’s newest middle-grade novel, “Doll Bones,” the main characters, Zach, Poppy and Alice, are at that intermediate stage between childhood and adolescence, when alliances shift and friendships subtly develop into something more, when children outgrow identities as quickly as they outgrow clothing, when boys and girls sometimes appear awkwardly patched together with emotional and physical features from various stages of development, like an evolutionary experiment gone haywire. For years, the three best friends have played a continuous, ever evolving game of make-believe embracing fictional kingdoms, characters and allegiances, using toy figurines and hastily assembled objects to stand in for the real thing. The Queen, a porcelain doll with vacant eyes and blond ringlet curls, presides over the kingdoms from her position in Poppy’s mom’s glassed-in cabinet.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fragile Things', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fragile Things', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Holly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:40:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Oliver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oliver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5762f839-db1d-5d05-90b5-e5d413956dd1', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5762f839-db1d-5d05-90b5-e5d413956dd1'}, {'abstract': 'In a novel set in 1944, a family in Florence aids the partisans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/hero-on-a-bicycle-by-shirley-hughes.html', 'snippet': 'In a novel set in 1944, a family in Florence aids the partisans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Historical fiction has an interesting place in the world of children’s literature. Regularly celebrated by adults with awards like the Newbery, these books nonetheless raise the question of whether the intended audience feels the same enthusiasm. What I’ve observed as a classroom teacher is that while not in the multitudes that flock to the goofy fun of Wimpy Kid or the wild fantasies of Percy Jackson, there are still plenty of young readers who can’t get enough of the past.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 180, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 180}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 307, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 1872, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/books/review/12edinger/12edinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ordinary Courage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ordinary Courage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Shirley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:40:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Edinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d68a03f0-a7d7-5cda-940d-139f5b6f7ce8', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d68a03f0-a7d7-5cda-940d-139f5b6f7ce8'}, {'abstract': 'A 14-year-old rugby player pines for an older classmate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/winger-by-andrew-smith.html', 'snippet': 'A 14-year-old rugby player pines for an older classmate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the young adult genres — the Dystopian Hellscape, the \xadHuman-Monster Romance, the Elite School-or-Camp for Nonmortals — the most popular right now may be the quietest: Aspiring John Green. GreenLit, as I like to call it, consists of realistic stories told by a funny, self-aware teenage narrator. These novels tend to have sharp dialogue, defective authority figures, occasional boozing, unrequited crushes and one or more heartbreaking twists. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uneven Field', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uneven Field', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, A J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Andrew (1959- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:40:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. J. Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfc22520-1cb6-5ff2-9549-840b887ef2ef', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfc22520-1cb6-5ff2-9549-840b887ef2ef'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage boy, who is caught acting as his brother’s drug courier, is sent to a juvenile detention center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/kindness-for-weakness-by-shawn-goodman.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage boy, who is caught acting as his brother’s drug courier, is sent to a juvenile detention center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James, a scrawny, thoughtful 15-year-old, is trapped in brutal penury in upstate New York. His father walked out long ago. His defeated mother, a barely functioning cocktail waitress, has brought home an abusive addict of a boyfriend. And his older brother, Louis, who left in a fury, is a muscle-bound drug dealer whom the police have in their sights.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Make No Mistake', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Make No Mistake', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bronner, Ethan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Shawn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T14:40:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a94a0632-7f4a-5da3-87fe-a4a419ab466d', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a94a0632-7f4a-5da3-87fe-a4a419ab466d'}, {'abstract': 'Three picture books look at intergenerational relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/books/review/oy-feh-so-by-cary-fagan-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three picture books look at intergenerational relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Extended family is not in the full-time business of being your family, and that is mostly to their advantage. They visit, they leave — even when they live with you, it is rarely forever. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't