5)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Coast (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:36:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Leigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44b81d61-9256-53e0-8ed8-5b5ce0c2cf52', 'word_count': 1308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44b81d61-9256-53e0-8ed8-5b5ce0c2cf52'}, {'abstract': '“The Memory Show,” a musical, reflects the tensions of caring for someone with Alzheimer’s disease — and being someone with Alzheimer’s disease.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/theater/reviews/the-memory-show-at-the-duke-theater.html', 'snippet': '“The Memory Show,” a musical, reflects the tensions of caring for someone with Alzheimer’s disease — and being someone with Alzheimer’s disease.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’re on the same side.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caregiver and Patient, Bound by Blood and Whining', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caregiver and Patient, Bound by Blood and Whining', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Sara', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transport Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Redler, Zach', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:36:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/93ff109f-d731-554b-907f-86fa80ce1de4', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93ff109f-d731-554b-907f-86fa80ce1de4'}, {'abstract': 'Rangers defenseman Marc Staal missed 29 games after a puck struck him in the right eye in March, but he returned for Game 3, but then did not dress for Game 4.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/rangers-staal-says-he-decided-to-sit-out-game-4/', 'snippet': 'Rangers defenseman Marc Staal missed 29 games after a puck struck him in the right eye in March, but he returned for Game 3, but then did not dress for Game 4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Speaking to reporters for the first time since his surprising absence from Game 4 because of the lingering effects of an eye injury, defenseman Marc Staal explained Friday that it was his decision to sit out, and that he was not sure when he would come back. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rangers’ Staal Says He Decided to Sit Out Game 4', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Staal, Marc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:36:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f83cc527-4833-5499-89fe-809e081030b4', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f83cc527-4833-5499-89fe-809e081030b4'}, {'abstract': 'The Moscow designer Vika Gazinskaya is making a name for herself, finding inspiration even in the unexpected.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/russian-revelation/', 'snippet': 'The Moscow designer Vika Gazinskaya is making a name for herself, finding inspiration even in the unexpected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, a woman in Florida contacted Vika Gazinskaya, a designer in Moscow, and bought $12,000 worth of clothes. Ms. Gazinskaya’s designs can be seen online, and purchased at Net-a-Porter, Moda Operandi and the Manhattan boutique Fivestory, as well as Forty Five Ten in Dallas. Maybe it didn’t occur to the Florida woman to contact those outlets, or maybe there were sizing or availability issues, but she ended up writing a letter to the designer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russian Revelation', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:39:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e02b1a34-315c-5285-bb7c-a40c1faec360', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e02b1a34-315c-5285-bb7c-a40c1faec360'}, {'abstract': 'A look at some of the social media dispatches from SALT conference in Las Vegas. The SkyBridge Alternatives Conference brings together more than 1,800 wealthy investors and hedge funds for four days of conferences, concerts and revelry.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/a-social-media-view-of-the-davos-of-hedge-funds/', 'snippet': 'A look at some of the social media dispatches from SALT conference in Las Vegas. The SkyBridge Alternatives Conference brings together more than 1,800 wealthy investors and hedge funds for four days of conferences, concerts and revelry.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A look at some of the social media dispatches from SALT conference in Las Vegas. The SkyBridge Alternatives Conference brings together more than 1,800 wealthy investors and hedge funds for four days of conferences, concerts and revelry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Social Media View of the Davos of Hedge Funds', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scaramucci, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:40:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bfb426c-8c6b-5a24-8ac5-f1c4f62ed694', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bfb426c-8c6b-5a24-8ac5-f1c4f62ed694'}, {'abstract': 'Two creative people, both pursuing expression and abstraction in their work, find that they have even more in common.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/anna-schuleit-and-yotam-haber-vows.html', 'snippet': 'Two creative people, both pursuing expression and abstraction in their work, find that they have even more in common.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time Yotam Haber was introduced to Anna Schuleit, in 2010 at the MacDowell artists retreat in Peterborough, N.H., she was helping a fellow artist bend steel spikes over a flame. She wore a bandanna over her blond hair and seemed utterly dedicated to her task — so much so that she took no notice of him. Still, he thought “she had an aura.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1195, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/fashion/weddings/12VOWS1_SPAN/12VOWS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Creative Partnership, in Life and in Art', 'kicker': 'Anna Schuleit and Yotam Haber', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Creative Partnership, in Life and in Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:51:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Lipton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f4d5930-0e85-51ba-8d5f-fc3911a36cba', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f4d5930-0e85-51ba-8d5f-fc3911a36cba'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Lambesis, the lead singer of As I Lay Dying, had bail set at $3 million.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/singer-accused-of-trying-to-hire-hitman-pleads-not-guilty/', 'snippet': 'Tim Lambesis, the lead singer of As I Lay Dying, had bail set at $3 million.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Prosecutors say the lead singer of the band As I Lay Dying, who is accused of trying to hire someone to kill his estranged wife, handed an envelope containing $1,000 in cash to an undercover officer, along with instructions, a photograph, an address, security gate codes and dates when he would have an alibi, The Associated Press reported.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Singer Accused of Trying to Hire Hitman Pleads Not Guilty', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lambesis, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'As I Lay Dying (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:53:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58797f21-ba2f-58ec-a30a-01c976ff2118', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58797f21-ba2f-58ec-a30a-01c976ff2118'}, {'abstract': 'When Christopher Guest has his director’s hat on droll is generally the order of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/television/a-mockumentary-digs-for-roots.html', 'snippet': 'When Christopher Guest has his director’s hat on droll is generally the order of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Christopher Guest has his director’s hat on, as for films like “Best in Show” and “A Mighty Wind,” droll is generally the order of the day. He carries that tradition to the small screen with “Family Tree,” a new comedy series that he created with Jim Piddock, which has its premiere Sunday night on HBO. The show, shot mockumentary style, centers on a 30-year-old named Tom Chadwick (Chris O’Dowd) who inherits a trunk that is full of family memorabilia. He begins poking through it and tries to learn more about the family members he encounters, beginning in the premiere with the man in full uniform whose photograph he finds.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mockumentary Digs for Roots', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mockumentary Digs For Roots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:54:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79e3fece-25c4-5bc1-9799-658cfa06dddc', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79e3fece-25c4-5bc1-9799-658cfa06dddc'}, {'abstract': 'It’s May, and dueling outdoor festivals have arrived.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/music/indie-in-brooklyn-electric-in-queens.html', 'snippet': 'It’s May, and dueling outdoor festivals have arrived.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s May, and dueling outdoor festivals have arrived. There’s a clear aesthetic choice — synthetic and transnational versus handmade and local — between Electric Daisy Carnival, at Citi Field on Friday and Saturday, and the Great Googa Mooga Festival, in Prospect Park Friday to Sunday. Electric Daisy presents more than 80 electronic dance music acts on five stages in and around the stadium. Trance, house, techno and dubstep dominate the lineup, with acts like Steve Angello, Dirty South, Afrojack, Richie Hawtin and 12th Planet. (electricdaisycarnival.com; two-day tickets are $239.83 general admission, one-day $132.05.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indie in Brooklyn, Electric in Queens', 'kicker': 'Pop Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indie in Brooklyn, Electric in Queens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72d30043-0fbe-5594-8db2-e22c81a24928', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72d30043-0fbe-5594-8db2-e22c81a24928'}, {'abstract': 'When it comes to trendy, glamorous repertory, it’s hard to think of an American company as well stocked as Hubbard Street Dance Chicago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/dance/sweden-and-israel-via-chicago.html', 'snippet': 'When it comes to trendy, glamorous repertory, it’s hard to think of an American company as well stocked as Hubbard Street Dance Chicago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to trendy, glamorous repertory, it’s hard to think of an American company as well stocked as Hubbard Street Dance Chicago. The list of choreographers who have worked with this immaculately technical group reads like an international who’s who of contemporary dance. Take, for instance, Sweden’s Mats Ek, revered in Europe but rarely seen in the United States; Aszure Barton, originally from Alberta, Canada; and Israel’s Ohad Naharin, the director of the Batsheva Dance Company, whose improvisation method, Gaga, has a global following. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sweden and Israel Via Chicago', 'kicker': 'Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweden and Israel Via Chicago', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:56:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b23592a-61fe-580a-b3ba-8f277e466de5', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b23592a-61fe-580a-b3ba-8f277e466de5'}, {'abstract': 'As a guest conductor, Mr. Rattle will lead the orchestra in its Carnegie Hall concert on Friday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/music/from-romanticism-to-apocalypse.html', 'snippet': 'As a guest conductor, Mr. Rattle will lead the orchestra in its Carnegie Hall concert on Friday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the conductor Simon Rattle’s work has been defined for over a decade by his adventurous tenure as principal conductor of the great Berlin Philharmonic, he has continued to maintain associations with other important ensembles, especially the Philadelphia Orchestra. As a guest conductor, Mr. Rattle will lead the orchestra in its Carnegie Hall concert on Friday night. The program opens with Webern’s Passacaglia, Op. 1, a glittering score written in 1908 when the composer was in his mid-20s and still under the allure of lush, late Romanticism. Then Mr. Rattle conducts Three Fragments from Berg’s opera “Wozzeck.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Romanticism To Apocalypse', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Romanticism To Apocalypse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:57:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd41d0d1-0cf4-5efa-8ad0-f6c5c9db2dc0', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd41d0d1-0cf4-5efa-8ad0-f6c5c9db2dc0'}, {'abstract': 'The new show, “Junior Masterchef,” will be one of five reality shows from Gordon Ramsay on the network.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/business/media/new-cooking-show-on-fox-to-feature-children-as-chefs.html', 'snippet': 'The new show, “Junior Masterchef,” will be one of five reality shows from Gordon Ramsay on the network.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How else to tinker with cooking reality shows? How about putting children in the kitchen?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Cooking Show on Fox to Feature Children as Chefs', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramsay, Gordon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Upfronts (Television)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Media', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/582b2349-0b5e-5f9c-8e6c-c3fd903d7c42', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/582b2349-0b5e-5f9c-8e6c-c3fd903d7c42'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s exhibition on the Met’s roof garden, which opens Tuesday, is also a study in perspective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/design/at-met-park-views-big-and-small.html', 'snippet': 'This year’s exhibition on the Met’s roof garden, which opens Tuesday, is also a study in perspective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1998 when Ellsworth Kelly became the first living artist to exhibit on the roof of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, he called the picture-postcard setting “an instant background.” When his sculptures — as tall as 14 feet — were installed there they looked enormous and the skyscrapers behind them tiny. Mr. Kelly compared this reversal of scale to Chinese landscape painting in the way it played with perspective. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Met, Park Views Big and Small', 'kicker': 'Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Met, Park Views Big and Small', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:58:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b968cb47-c01f-5e97-b8b1-e12c8a44ba6a', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b968cb47-c01f-5e97-b8b1-e12c8a44ba6a'}, {'abstract': 'In a scathing ruling, the judge said the process of denying broad distribution of the drug has been “corrupted by political interference” for years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/health/judge-refuses-to-drop-order-on-contraceptive-pill-without-regard-to-age.html', 'snippet': 'In a scathing ruling, the judge said the process of denying broad distribution of the drug has been “corrupted by political interference” for years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A federal judge on Friday stepped up his criticism of the Obama administration, accusing the Justice Department of making “frivolous” and “silly” arguments in its attempt to delay making the morning-after emergency contraceptive pill available to women and girls of all ages without a prescription.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge Refuses to Drop His Order Allowing Morning-After Pill for All Ages', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judge Refuses to Drop His Order Allowing Morning-After Pill for All Ages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plan B (Contraceptive)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korman, Edward R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Human Services Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sebelius, Kathleen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T17:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/410f9703-78c2-504e-a3ac-98e2da0d6d9c', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/410f9703-78c2-504e-a3ac-98e2da0d6d9c'}, {'abstract': 'N.H.T.S.A. may investigate a problem with Corvette low-beam headlights.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/n-h-t-s-a-to-investigate-corvette-headlight-failures/', 'snippet': 'N.H.T.S.A. may investigate a problem with Corvette low-beam headlights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After receiving complaints from owners of 2005-7 Chevrolet Corvettes about low-beam headlights failing, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration is opening an investigation, according to a report posted on the agency’s Web site. The action comes as the agency continues to mull over whether to investigate low-beam failures on about 248,000 General Motors crossovers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.H.T.S.A. to Investigate Corvette Headlight Failures', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T18:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60fe5c27-e051-5d39-a295-f885ab6bcbcc', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60fe5c27-e051-5d39-a295-f885ab6bcbcc'}, {'abstract': 'Two new DVDs — “The Deadly Companions” from VCI Entertainment and “Major Dundee” from Twilight Time — present Sam Peckinpah at two stages in his development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/movies/homevideo/new-dvds-peckinpahs-deadly-companions-and-major-dundee.html', 'snippet': 'Two new DVDs — “The Deadly Companions” from VCI Entertainment and “Major Dundee” from Twilight Time — present Sam Peckinpah at two stages in his development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sam Peckinpah’s life, like many of his movies, ended in a kind of apocalyptic debacle. Too many arguments with producers, too much alcohol-fueled misbehavior and (always the real problem) too many disappointments at the box office had rendered the director of “The Wild Bunch” (1969) effectively unemployable by t