eight': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/10/science/video-bat-tongue/video-bat-tongue-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/science/video-bat-tongue/video-bat-tongue-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/10/science/video-bat-tongue/video-bat-tongue-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/science/video-bat-tongue/video-bat-tongue-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tongue Made for Slurping', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:07:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'Cally Harper, Brainerd & Swartz Labs/Brown University', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Brainerd', 'middlename': 'Swartz', 'lastname': 'Labs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f9b756c6-a72e-5a44-ad5c-38883567540b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f9b756c6-a72e-5a44-ad5c-38883567540b'}, {'abstract': 'The designers behind the firm Grain branch out from personal accessories to furniture, introducing two versions of their Dish table at the International Contemporary Furniture Fair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/garden/tables-inspired-by-african-stools.html', 'snippet': 'The designers behind the firm Grain branch out from personal accessories to furniture, introducing two versions of their Dish table at the International Contemporary Furniture Fair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though they’ve been in business for five years, the product designers behind Grain, a firm based on Bainbridge Island off the Seattle coast, have only just gotten around to doing furniture. Before this, the team — Chelsea Green, 32, and James Minola, 30 — designed what they call “personal accessories,” like a recyclable shower curtain and a hand mirror. At the International Contemporary Furniture Fair, May 18to 21 at the Jacob K. Javits Convention Center in Manhattan, they plan to introduce two versions of their Dish table (one is a side table, the other a coffee table). The models were hand-carved African stools that Ms. Green received from her parents and grandparents, who grew up in Africa (her father was born in Nigeria; her mother lived for a time in Ethiopia and Ghana). “We were a bit inspired by the scooped-out shape but wanted it to be more functional, so we flattened it out and cleaned it up a bit,” she said. The tables are made of ash and available in either a clear oil finish or black stain; the side table is $1,950, and the coffee table is $2,500. Grain also plans to present a new mirror and candlesticks at the fair. Information: (206) 965-9302 or graindesign.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1479, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/garden/10FURNITURE/10FURNITURE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tables Inspired by African Stools', 'kicker': 'Currents | Furniture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tables', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:09:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb3d7eb9-145a-51c3-9826-5b23b85b1876', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb3d7eb9-145a-51c3-9826-5b23b85b1876'}, {'abstract': 'Eight partners at Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz are proposing that company boards consider adopting a bylaw prohibiting shareholder activists from compensating director nominees.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/a-strong-response-to-paying-board-nominees/', 'snippet': 'Eight partners at Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz are proposing that company boards consider adopting a bylaw prohibiting shareholder activists from compensating director nominees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stakes just got higher for shareholder activists.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Strong Response to Paying Board Nominees', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:13:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/080fe6a6-af5a-58a4-bd7b-9e7641bcad24', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/080fe6a6-af5a-58a4-bd7b-9e7641bcad24'}, {'abstract': 'One species of bat has evolved a way to eat while flying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/science/bat-species-evolves-to-suck-nectar-mid-flight.html', 'snippet': 'One species of bat has evolved a way to eat while flying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Certain bats, like Pallas’s long-tongued bat, hover over flowers and suck nectar. Hovering takes a lot of energy, so the bats need to gather as much liquid as possible with each flick of their long tongues. Scientists have long known that hairlike structures known as papillae at the tips of the bats’ tongues help trap the nectar. Now, using high-speed video cameras, researchers at Brown University have seen that as the tongues extend, the papillae flare outward, becoming perpendicular.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tongue Like a Mop', 'kicker': 'Optics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brown University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/467fddcb-1bdb-579c-bf6a-5a6995474575', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/467fddcb-1bdb-579c-bf6a-5a6995474575'}, {'abstract': 'The actor’s Fox drama “Touch” steadily lost viewers, but its exit has inspired talk of a return to “24.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/kiefer-sutherland-and-the-fox-ratings/', 'snippet': 'The actor’s Fox drama “Touch” steadily lost viewers, but its exit has inspired talk of a return to “24.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of the Fox drama “Touch,” which stars Kiefer Sutherland, would be wise to savor the Season 2 finale on Friday. A day before, the network announced that this episode would in fact be the series finale. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kiefer Sutherland and Fox Ratings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': '24 (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sutherland, Kiefer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FX (TV Network)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7c48584-744f-55cf-8b13-2a5660fa4a0d', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7c48584-744f-55cf-8b13-2a5660fa4a0d'}, {'abstract': 'Hedge fund manager and S.E.C. reach deal. | JPMorgan sued over credit card debt cases. | Enron’s Skilling strikes a deal for a shorter prison sentence. | Hedge fund impresario plays host in Las Vegas. | Errors afflict more checks issued to aid homeowners.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/week-in-review-big-names-and-big-money-in-las-vegas/', 'snippet': 'Hedge fund manager and S.E.C. reach deal. | JPMorgan sued over credit card debt cases. | Enron’s Skilling strikes a deal for a shorter prison sentence. | Hedge fund impresario plays host in Las Vegas. | Errors afflict more checks issued to aid hom...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hedge fund manager and S.E.C. reach deal. | JPMorgan sued over credit card debt cases. | Enron’s Skilling strikes a deal for a shorter prison sentence. | Hedge fund impresario plays host in Las Vegas. | Errors afflict more checks issued to aid homeowners. | Small firm could turn the vote on Jamie Dimon. | New York investigates advances on pensions. | A humbled Kleiner Perkins adjusts its strategy. | Two big banks face suits in mortgage pact abuses. | Andrew Ross Sorkin says that for Warren Buffett, the past isn’t always a prologue. | A call for new blood on the JPMorgan board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week in Review: Big Names and Big Money in Las Vegas', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Owles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Owles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc94e859-9821-5108-91fb-0b3597edc369', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc94e859-9821-5108-91fb-0b3597edc369'}, {'abstract': 'Food film festivals, exploring the art of the preparation, are cropping up across the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/movies/charleston-and-other-film-festivals-that-love-food.html', 'snippet': 'Food film festivals, exploring the art of the preparation, are cropping up across the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — For four hours, the filmmakers Natasha Subramaniam and Alisa Lapidus constructed a four-tiered dessert out of whipped cream, amaretto cookies, baked meringue and locally sourced edible flowers. “It’s a Pavlova meets a croquembouche,” Ms. Subramaniam said. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/arts/12FOODIE1_SPAN/12FOODIE1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Food for the Epicurean Cinephile', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Food for the Epicurean Cinephile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:22:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Ito', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ito', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff79ed69-7087-59db-9f4a-1eacf2b3f864', 'word_count': 1092, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff79ed69-7087-59db-9f4a-1eacf2b3f864'}, {'abstract': 'The first attack ad of the 2016 campaign accuses the former secretary of state of helping “cover up” what really happened in Benghazi.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/karl-rove-versus-hillary-clinton/', 'snippet': 'The first attack ad of the 2016 campaign accuses the former secretary of state of helping “cover up” what really happened in Benghazi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American Crossroads, the Karl Rove-led super PAC, has released the first attack ad of the 2016 campaign, accusing Hillary Clinton of mishandling the Benghazi killings. It’s a pretty shameless spot, filled with innuendo and as many unflattering pictures of the former secretary of state as possible.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Karl Rove Versus Hillary Clinton', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:29:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0deaf173-fbec-5752-90b7-7fc336a1054d', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0deaf173-fbec-5752-90b7-7fc336a1054d'}, {'abstract': 'A 6,637-square-foot residence with six bedrooms and a private 1,814-square-foot terrace was the most expensive residential sale of the week.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/big-ticket-16-295-million-for-wraparound-views/', 'snippet': 'A 6,637-square-foot residence with six bedrooms and a private 1,814-square-foot terrace was the most expensive residential sale of the week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A pristine 12-room sponsor unit at 535 West End Avenue, a 1920 brick building reinvented, expanded and marketed as a luxury destination by the Extell Development Company, sold for $16.295 million and was the most expensive residential sale of the week, according to city records. The 6,637-square-foot residence, No. 16, had an original listing price of $16.9 million.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | $16.295 Million for Wraparound Views', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wraparound Views', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T19:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79474aad-fbdb-5f08-a1cd-f7c1f7ef086b', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79474aad-fbdb-5f08-a1cd-f7c1f7ef086b'}, {'abstract': 'The other shows are a comedy, “Undateable,” and a spinoff of “Chicago Fire.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/business/media/nbc-adds-3-more-shows-including-an-updated-ironside.html', 'snippet': 'The other shows are a comedy, “Undateable,” and a spinoff of “Chicago Fire.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The busy rounds of television shows coming and going continued on Friday as NBC added new series to a growing list for fall, while others found themselves facing a last roundup.', 'source': 'The New Yor