 materials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bryce Reed, a paramedic who helped evacuate people after the fertilizer plant explosion in West, Tex., was charged with possessing bomb-making materials.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Against Bryce Reed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Explosions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'West Chemical and Fertilizer Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T21:07:09+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7d43062f-f2dc-5789-abac-24953054c3c8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7d43062f-f2dc-5789-abac-24953054c3c8'}, {'abstract': 'Annie Lowrey fielded questions on Twitter about the effects of mounting student debt on the financial lives of young graduates.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/student-debts-impact-on-young-graduates/', 'snippet': 'Annie Lowrey fielded questions on Twitter about the effects of mounting student debt on the financial lives of young graduates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Student loan debt now exceeds $1 trillion, and as Annie Lowrey writes, that debt is an increasing burden to the financial lives of young graduates. It is forcing them to delay purchases of things like homes, cars and other big-ticket items and acting as a drag on growth, economists say. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Debt’s Impact on Young Graduates', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T21:11:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By ECONOMIX EDITORS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Economix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'EDITORS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a6f1b67-ac69-5514-ad76-cf8c6b956426', 'word_count': 85, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a6f1b67-ac69-5514-ad76-cf8c6b956426'}, {'abstract': 'As we show in our visual roundup, flora is everywhere you look at this year’s edition of the massive art extravaganza.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/all-the-rage-this-years-frieze-new-york-artists-are-mad-about-plants/', 'snippet': 'As we show in our visual roundup, flora is everywhere you look at this year’s edition of the massive art extravaganza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Considering the dozens of languages, thousands of artworks and billions of dollars coursing through the tent at Frieze New York — the second edition of which opened to the public on Friday and runs through Monday — you might expect the fair to offer a decisive statement about what matters in art right now. In truth, that’s probably expecting too much from what is ultimately a highbrow pop-up shopping mall, but certain motifs always emerge from these extravaganzas, and what’s more fun than sussing out the pattern? This year, the predominant trend is flora, with plant life sprouting up in photographs and potted into sculptures. A reflection of the mania for sustainability and urban gardens? A nod to the season? Decide for yourself. We’ll see if the greenery rakes in the green, but watch out for some of these leafy highlights as you venture past the air kisses and hipster restaurants into Frieze New York’s white cube jungle.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Rage | This Year’s Frieze New York Artists Are Mad About Plants', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All The Rage: Nature’s Artwork Catches On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frieze Art Fair', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T21:12:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fa39c11-5f52-5a8f-b959-fb18c82eaba3', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fa39c11-5f52-5a8f-b959-fb18c82eaba3'}, {'abstract': 'Actavis, the maker of generic drugs, said it was in early talks to acquire Warner Chilcott.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/actavis-in-preliminary-talks-with-warner-chilcott/', 'snippet': 'Actavis, the maker of generic drugs, said it was in early talks to acquire Warner Chilcott.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Actavis is in talks with another drug maker about a potential merger after talks with Valeant Pharmaceuticals International fell through.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Actavis in Preliminary Talks With Warner Chilcott', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T21:16:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be267cbd-c56c-54a7-937d-a9b5037e7774', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be267cbd-c56c-54a7-937d-a9b5037e7774'}, {'abstract': 'A 25-year-old Texas law student explains why he posted instructions on how to make a gun using 3-D printing technology, which were downloaded more than 100,000 times before being taken down.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/printable-gun-instructions-spread-online-after-state-dept-orders-their-removal/', 'snippet': 'A 25-year-old Texas law student explains why he posted instructions on how to make a gun using 3-D printing technology, which were downloaded more than 100,000 times before being taken down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The State Department, citing possible compliance concerns regarding the export of firearms, has ordered a Web site to remove what are believed to be the world’s first online instructions on how to build a 3-D printable handgun. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Printable-Gun Instructions Spread Online After State Dept. Orders Their Removal', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': '3-D Printers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T21:19:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b475ea32-c725-5a76-8e60-4c83483ebd4e', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b475ea32-c725-5a76-8e60-4c83483ebd4e'}, {'abstr