s uncommon dishes, bare tables and a place to converse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/nyregion/a-review-of-rainbow-thai-in-westport.html', 'snippet': 'Rainbow Thai in Westport features uncommon dishes, bare tables and a place to converse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two suggestions if you visit Rainbow Thai in Westport: first, reserve ahead, as this tiny place seats only 40, and second, request, if it is available, a table overlooking the Saugatuck River. If you are lucky you might even catch a glimpse of crews rowing below you on the river.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 752}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 818, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1635, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Thai Discoveries in a Spare Setting', 'kicker': 'Connecticut Dining | Westport', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thai Discoveries in a Spare Setting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westport (Conn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rainbow Thai (Westport, Conn, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T22:54:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd4c7c5a-3603-581d-9d8c-4542cade9299', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd4c7c5a-3603-581d-9d8c-4542cade9299'}, {'abstract': 'Do you wonder these at all.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/friday-night-music-sarah-jarosz-tell-me-true/', 'snippet': 'Do you wonder these at all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was looking for a live version with the full trio, and here it is. They’re performing in New York in a couple of weeks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Night Music: Sarah Jarosz, Tell Me True', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T22:58:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/716f3cd7-9e70-516d-906a-38567edfbe6e', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/716f3cd7-9e70-516d-906a-38567edfbe6e'}, {'abstract': 'Travel, and travel writing, should be able to open our minds — and even to change them.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/10/editors-letter-away-we-go/', 'snippet': 'Travel, and travel writing, should be able to open our minds — and even to change them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travel journalism often falls into one of two camps — the highly didactic and servicey piece, “go here, stay there, see that,” or the long-winded narrative in which the writer has the experience and we are meant to be satisfied by the first-person blow-by-blow he or she has brought back for us: “the road into town was dusty and unpaved and the driver kept getting lost.” The former is best served up by the Internet on demand. (You can find all of our hotel and restaurant suggestions for the stories in this issue at tmagazine.com.) And the pure version of the latter is perhaps best left to brilliant writers who promise to reward you for coming along (Edith Wharton, Patrick Leigh Fermor, Evelyn Waugh, Jan Morris). There are of course also intellectual and cultural histories of locales, in which the writer seeks to tease out the influences — historical, economic, social, stylistic, culinary and political — that make the place uniquely what it is (V. S. Naipaul, Bruce Chatwin, Ryszard Kapuscinski). The type we favor at T is a hybrid of the three, in which great writing and personal narrative is done in the service of not just traipsing along with the writer who got to go on an amazing journey that we didn’t, but of bringing you back a new understanding of a place that is enlightening and satisfying regardless of whether you ever plan to visit. And yet, if you do choose to go, we’re there to guide you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editor’s Letter | Away We Go', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T23:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Needleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Needleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b890c71d-dbe2-5b79-be74-c5b403b6e299', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b890c71d-dbe2-5b79-be74-c5b403b6e299'}, {'abstract': 'The wines sold under second-label programs can be serviceable, pleasurable and not too far in quality from their superior siblings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/nyregion/for-casual-crowds-long-island-vintners-second-labels.html', 'snippet': 'The wines sold under second-label programs can be serviceable, pleasurable and not too far in quality from their superior siblings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the number of guests at a party or a barbecue threatens to overwhelm the wine budget, second labels can come to the rescue.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Casual Crowds, Second Labels', 'kicker': 'Long Island Vines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Casual Crowds, Second Labels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacNeille, Suzanne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-10T23:04:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard G. Goldberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Goldberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8de8149-1f2a-582b-80ec-cd47c8534bcf', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8de8149-1f2a-582b-80ec-cd47c8534bcf'}, {'abstract': 'La Cucina is the kind of place you leave with at least two resolutions: to eat nothing at all the next day, and to return as soon as you can.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/nyregion/a-review-of-la-cucina-in-hewlett.html', 'snippet': 'La Cucina is the kind of place you leave with at least two resolutions: to eat nothing at all the next day, and to return as soon as you can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As we walked into La Cucina, a year-and-a-half-old Italian restaurant in Hewlett, on a recent Saturday night, something resembling a dinner party was in full swing. The owner, Luigi Muto, greeted us with a warm smile and a worried glance over his shoulder. Behind him, the small minimalist dining room — awash in soft jazz, seductive aromas and animated conversation — was packed. ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINELI3/12DINELI3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINELI3/12DINELI3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 620}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12DINELI3/12DINELI3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 992, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/12/nyregion/12