ws_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shreeya Sinha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shreeya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sinha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/835d5845-af32-520f-ae27-2faa17bdcfbd', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/835d5845-af32-520f-ae27-2faa17bdcfbd'}, {'abstract': 'I admit that for many years I forswore gin because I thought it made me sad. But I was wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/whos-afraid-of-gin/', 'snippet': 'I admit that for many years I forswore gin because I thought it made me sad. But I was wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If I had a dollar for every time someone told me “Tequila makes me crazy,” I would have a pretty hefty wad of cash. And just yesterday a man told me he doesn’t drink whiskey because it makes him angry. (I’ve heard that one before, too. Plenty.) Still, I believe gin has the most fearsome reputation of all liquors, and it just doesn’t seem deserved. In my Drink column this week, I admit that for many years I forswore gin because I thought it made me sad. But the gin was not to blame; normal life circumstances were — and so were the lingering effects of having seen William Hogarth’s “Gin Lane.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 712}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 863, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2009, 'width': 1692, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/10/magazine/10-gin-lane/10-gin-lane-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s Afraid of Gin?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gin (Liquor)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-11T13:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf36a4ee-4a7d-5af0-ab24-411153249180', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf36a4ee-4a7d-5af0-ab24-411153249180'}, {'abstract': 'We’re living in a golden age.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/11/in-praise-of-econowonkery/', 'snippet': 'We’re living in a golden age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mike Konczal has an interesting piece on the general question of whether wonk-blogging — the practice of putting up fairly analytical data-heavy posts bearing on policy issues directly on the web, rather than going through more traditional publication channels — is a good thing. He puts it in the context of liberal politics, which it mostly (though not entirely) is; but I’d like to think about it more generally as a way in which data and analysis can be brought quickly to bear on policy discussion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Praise of Econowonkery', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-11T13:13:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By PAUL KRUGMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'KRUGMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62828170-d30e-51a3-9067-070d3e9ea662', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62828170-d30e-51a3-9067-070d3e9ea662'}, {'abstract': 'Voters turned out for a historic election as threats of attacks were borne out, with violence across the country leaving at least 21 people dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/05/11/world/asia/20130512-PAKISTAN.html', 'snippet': 'Voters turned out for a historic election as threats of attacks were borne out, with violence across the country leaving at least 21 people dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Voters turned out for a historic election as threats of attacks were borne out, with violence across the country leaving at least 21 people dead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/11/world/asia/20130512-PAKISTAN-slide-XHW0/20130512-PAKISTAN-slide-XHW0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge',