Pigs, Little Pigs, Let Us In on Your Mysterious Past', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-12T15:30:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edna Ishayik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ishayik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e8d03f0-1650-53d8-819a-5ec592dfbe9e', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e8d03f0-1650-53d8-819a-5ec592dfbe9e'}, {'abstract': 'In a 60-year career, Ms. Brookes appeared in films and on television but drew her widest notice for performances in stage productions of Shakespeare, Pirandello and Edward Albee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/theater/jacqueline-brookes-actress-dies-at-82.html', 'snippet': 'In a 60-year career, Ms. Brookes appeared in films and on television but drew her widest notice for performances in stage productions of Shakespeare, Pirandello and Edward Albee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jacqueline Brookes, an actress who appeared in films and on television but who won her widest acclaim on the stage in New York and around the country, performing the work of Shakespeare, Molière, Pirandello, Edward Albee and other dramatists over a 60-year career, died on April 26 in Manhattan. She was 82.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1261, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/arts/BROOKES-obit/BROOKES-obit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jacqueline Brookes, Actress and Teacher, Dies at 82', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jacqueline Brookes, 82, Actress and Teacher', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brookes, Jacqueline (1930-2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Douglas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-12T15:33:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/81ad1836-22b2-5578-8086-f71361f19455', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81ad1836-22b2-5578-8086-f71361f19455'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:30 P.M. (HBO) FAMILY TREE In this new, largely improvised series from Christopher Guest and Jim Piddock, Chris O’Dowd plays the down-on-his-luck Tom Chadwick, a 30-year-old Briton grappling with his identity since losing his girlfriend and his job. But when his Great-Aunt Victoria bequeaths him a chest of curios, he starts investigating his lineage to understand better who he and his unconventional family actually are. The ventriloquist Nina Conti plays his sister, Bea, who speaks through a monkey hand puppet; Michael McKean, above right, with Mr. O’Dowd, is their father. In this premiere episode, Tom finds a black-and-white portrait thought to be of his great-grandfather Harry and tracks down Neville St. Aubrey (Christopher Fairbank), an oddball antique photography expert. Tom’s best friend, Pete (Tom Bennett), meanwhile, fixes him up with a model. But what kind, exactly, no one is sure. “There is a familiarity to the series,” Mike Hale wrote in The New York Times. “As in Mr. Guest’s films, its story is less important than the presentation of a gallery of eccentrics, ranging from mildly odd to completely loony.” One result, Mr. Hale said, is that the series “can feel a little loose and inconsequential” as Tom “unearths one dotty, inglorious relative after another.” But “that also means that we get to spend more time with Mr. Guest’s crack cast of improvisers,” he added, “and there are moments in each half-hour that pay off.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-12T15:37:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/d028dc49-280d-5a6a-add8-3f2900e809e4', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d028dc49-280d-5a6a-add8-3f2900e809e4'}, {'abstract': 'Shades of modern Japan in 1930s Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/12/nevillenomics/', 'snippet': 'Shades of modern Japan in 1930s Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nicholas Crafts has a really interesting piece about UK economic policy in the 1930s. The gist is that monetary policy drove recovery through the expectations channel; the Bank of England managed to credibly promise to be irresponsible, that is, to generate inflation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nevillenomics', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-12T16:06:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26a26f2c-ecfa-5587-9759-f126d6337e92', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26a26f2c-ecfa-5587-9759-f126d6337e92'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas R. Pickering, who led the State Department’s inquiry into the attack on a diplomatic outpost, said there had been no need to interview the secretary of state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/us/politics/gop-pushes-for-more-details-of-benghazi-attack.html', 'snippet': 'Thomas R. Pickering, who led the State Department’s inquiry into the attack on a diplomatic outpost, said there had been no need to interview the secretary of state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The State Department board that reviewed last year’s attack on a diplomatic outpost in Libya never questioned Hillary Rodham Clinton, the secretary of state at the time, because it had already decided responsibility lay below her level, the board’s chairman said Sunday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/us/BENGHAZI/BENGHAZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/13/us/BENGHAZI/BENGHAZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/us/BENGHAZI/BENGHAZI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/us/BENGHAZI/BENGHAZI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1357, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/us/BENGHAZI/BENGHAZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', '