tname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/325a1639-a957-582a-af64-b059307bfcee', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/325a1639-a957-582a-af64-b059307bfcee'}, {'abstract': 'At the age of 88, the jazz pianist Barbara Carroll, playing at Birdland, still exudes the spirit and enthusiasm of a teenager.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/arts/music/barbara-carroll-at-birdland.html', 'snippet': 'At the age of 88, the jazz pianist Barbara Carroll, playing at Birdland, still exudes the spirit and enthusiasm of a teenager.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the many reasons Barbara Carroll has been called the first lady of jazz piano is her quietly expressive breadth. At Birdland on Saturday evening, Ms. Carroll, who is 88 but exudes the spirit and enthusiasm of a very sophisticated teenager, played a set whose moods ran from playful to reflective to a light stomp, sometimes all in the same song. Ms. Carroll has always had special simpatico with the rarefied wit of Cy Coleman and Carolyn Leigh. Singing and playing the obscure “I Wanna Be Yours,” she evoked the golden age of screwball comedy with the sly, teasing innuendo of lines like, “I wanna be all the cream in your dairy/your favorite tooth fairy.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/arts/14BIRDLAND/14BIRDLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/14/arts/14BIRDLAND/14BIRDLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 812}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/arts/14BIRDLAND/14BIRDLAND-jumbo.jpg',