role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4044a36-0dcb-5ef7-b72d-5aa58ecb6c3b', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4044a36-0dcb-5ef7-b72d-5aa58ecb6c3b'}, {'abstract': 'The I.R.S. is supposed to enforce the tax code. Under current law, however, it has little choice but to exercise discretion in the constitutionally dangerous waters of campaign finance.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/congresss-role-in-the-i-r-s-focus-on-conservative-groups/', 'snippet': 'The I.R.S. is supposed to enforce the tax code. Under current law, however, it has little choice but to exercise discretion in the constitutionally dangerous waters of campaign finance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Outrage continues to escalate over the revelation that Internal Revenue Service employees focused on conservative groups applying for tax-exempt status. The indignation is understandable: political targeting is an abuse of power, and the idea of using the I.R.S. to go after one’s enemies is a classic dirty trick. Unfortunately, the incident provides a new fuel source for the paranoid style in American politics. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress’s Role in the I.R.S. Focus on Conservative Groups', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service Political Profiling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-13T22:13:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Fleischer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fleischer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e1e84d-b7a7-57ec-8bca-839c9b2c696d', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e1e84d-b7a7-57ec-8bca-839c9b2c696d'}, {'abstract': 'Readers share their experiences trying out this month’s Recipe Lab selection, Southern fried chicken, in their own kitchens.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/notes-on-southern-livings-fried-chicken/', 'snippet': 'Readers share their experiences trying out this month’s Recipe Lab selection, Southern fried chicken, in their own kitchens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So, dear readers, how have you fared so far with this month’s Recipe Lab assignment? One home cook, Erin P. of Brooklyn, N.Y., said her attempt at the featured recipe, by Norman King, a food editor at Southern Living, resulted in the best fried chicken she had ever made on her own. Does this ring true for you?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/dining/friedchickenpic/friedchickenpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/13/dining/friedchickenpic/friedchickenpic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/13/dining/friedchickenpic/friedchickenpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': '