Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9559c4cc-aff5-5b08-bd8d-4295d0b2bb56', 'word_count': 1282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9559c4cc-aff5-5b08-bd8d-4295d0b2bb56'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition of work by the influential French photographer shows his knack for capturing the female form.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/on-view-patrick-demarcheliers-bodies-of-desire/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition of work by the influential French photographer shows his knack for capturing the female form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French photographer Patrick Demarchelier famously started out taking passport pictures as a teenager, and ever since then he’s been creating images that take people to unfamiliar places. Now 69, he is internationally renowned for his portraits of supermodels, princesses and celebrities for publications like Vogue, Harper’s Bazaar, Elle and Vanity Fair. Since his career took off in the 1970s, he has had an outsize influence on the way artists portray fashion and stardom. “Desire,” a new exhibition of his black-and-white photographs opening tomorrow in Paris, is a testament to Demarchelier’s ability to capture the female body. The 20 images include Laetitia Casta, stretching her naked ribcage taut; Helena Christensen, crouching and eyeing the camera with a panther stare; and Kate Moss and Carla Bruni, sitting together like interlocking puzzle pieces. Every contour suggests both purity and provocation, speaking to the very essence of desire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Patrick Demarchelier’s Bodies of Desire', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Demarchelier, Patrick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-13T22:22:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bb24a3f-b0cb-5dbf-b697-e404d1119b8e', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bb24a3f-b0cb-5dbf-b697-e404d1119b8e'}, {'abstract': 'A car explosion on a busy street in Benghazi stirred new anger at the failure of the transitional government to fill the security vacuum left by the ouster of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/world/africa/car-bomb-in-libya.html', 'snippet': 'A car explosion on a busy street in Benghazi stirred new anger at the failure of the transitional government to fill the security vacuum left by the ouster of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BENGHAZI, Libya — A car explosion on a busy street killed at least four people and injured more than a dozen on Monday, stirring new anger at the failure of the transitional government to fill the security vacuum left by the ouster of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Car Bomb Kills at Least 4 in Libya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Libya: Deadly Car Bombing in Benghazi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirkpatrick, David D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 's