rs of the N.R.A. and most of its leaders may be un-American. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the N.R.A. Un-American?', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Second Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T01:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a731af84-db85-5e21-b49a-f510588f7496', 'word_count': 1584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a731af84-db85-5e21-b49a-f510588f7496'}, {'abstract': 'What I learned about being a writer from composing thank-you notes.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/13/the-art-of-repetition/', 'snippet': 'What I learned about being a writer from composing thank-you notes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the months after my mother died, we started the task of sorting. It’s a strange process that makes you part curator and part detective. You read every letter in every file, empty every box, open every drawer. We weren’t looking for clues — my mother was remarkably transparent — but I found one anyway. A clue to why I had become a writer. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Repetition', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T01:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Sollisch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sollisch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6bacf54-3c2d-5848-a907-6dbe5167a5fb', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6bacf54-3c2d-5848-a907-6dbe5167a5fb'}, {'abstract': 'Recent developments in health and science news. This week: a revelation from a Wright brother, more analysis of Twitter posts and a call for a science laureate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/science/a-concrete-plane-the-nectar-mops-of-bats-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Recent developments in health and science news. This week: a revelation from a Wright brother, more analysis of Twitter posts and a call for a science laureate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you watch only one video about bat tongues this week, let it be one made by biologists at Brown University, who figured out how to illuminate the animals’ long tongues without bothering their sensitive bat eyes. “We shot color high-speed video of the bats gathering nectar, which is challenging to obtain because color cameras require a lot of light,” said Cally J. Harper, lead author of a paper on the research. The nimble camera work, she said, produced “the ‘aha’ moment” of the study — read on for the results.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 253, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 253}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/science/14WEEK1_SPAN/14WEEK1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Concrete Plane, the ‘Nectar Mops’ of Bats and More', 'kicker': 'The Week', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kingson, Jennifer A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Orville', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyson, Neil DeGrasse', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T01:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer A. Kingson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Kingson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c2f626a-d44e-5daf-a25c-db4bbd3bc020', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c2f626a-d44e-5daf-a25c-db4bbd3bc020'}, {'abstract': 'The success of Pacers center Roy Hibbert in the second-round playoff series against the Knicks is a testament to his mixed martial arts training and evolving offensive game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/sports/basketball/mixed-martial-arts-evoke-the-hard-side-of-hibbert.html', 'snippet': 'The success of Pacers center Roy Hibbert in the second-round playoff series against the Knicks is a testament to his mixed martial arts training and evolving offensive game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INDIANAPOLIS — Integrated Fighting Academy sits in a well-manicured office park along the southern rim of the city, sharing a flat concrete building with a security system supply company and a lawn with a flock of geese. Two truck tires, weighing about 300 pounds each, lie in the back of the parking lot.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/sports/jp-yHIBBERT/jp-yHIBBERT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/14/sports/jp-yHIBBERT/jp-yHIBBERT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 892}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/sports/jp-yHIBBERT/jp-yHIBBERT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 689, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/sports/jp-yHIBBERT/jp-yHIBBERT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1378, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': 