y Fountain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fountain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7282238b-9650-578a-8dc2-ea4a27d798b1', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7282238b-9650-578a-8dc2-ea4a27d798b1'}, {'abstract': 'The initial talking points to describe the attack in Benghazi, Libya, though inept, is not the real issue. But Republicans are making it into one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/opinion/white-house-under-fire-it-fumbles-benghazi-response.html', 'snippet': 'The initial talking points to describe the attack in Benghazi, Libya, though inept, is not the real issue. But Republicans are making it into one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No matter how often Republicans denounce them, the initial talking points used by the White House to describe the attack in Benghazi, Libya are not the real point. But they have become a major political issue in large part because of the White House’s inept response to questions about how they were written. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bumbling Response on Talking Points', 'kicker': 'Editorial | White House Under Fire', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bumbling Response on Talking Points', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T01:20:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6685c546-0d95-59b7-9907-9aa33311a4e4', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6685c546-0d95-59b7-9907-9aa33311a4e4'}, {'abstract': 'The I.R.S. should have scrutinized every group seeking a tax exemption for “social welfare” activity — Democrat or Republican, conservative or liberal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/opinion/white-house-under-fire-it-condemns-irs-audits.html', 'snippet': 'The I.R.S. should have scrutinized every group seeking a tax exemption for “social welfare” activity — Democrat or Republican, conservative or liberal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Internal Revenue Service was absolutely correct to look into the abuse of the tax code by political organizations masquerading as “social welfare” groups over the last three years. The agency’s mistake — and it was a serious one — was focusing on groups with “Tea Party” in their name or those criticizing how the country is run.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The I.R.S. Audits Are Condemned', 'kicker': 'Editorial | White House Under Fire', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Condemns I.R.S. Audits of Political Groups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', '