ction_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/402edd4b-6b0d-5ac9-829c-0b852ae98160', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/402edd4b-6b0d-5ac9-829c-0b852ae98160'}, {'abstract': 'Last month’s factory tragedy could cost the country millions of jobs. Or it could just raise the price of a T-shirt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/magazine/economic-recovery-made-in-bangladesh.html', 'snippet': 'Last month’s factory tragedy could cost the country millions of jobs. Or it could just raise the price of a T-shirt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A couple of years ago, I was in an industrial park in Port-au-Prince, Haiti, as a textiles executive pitched me on becoming a rich T-shirt manufacturer. It was easy, he said, to teach basic sewing to even the most poorly educated farmers. If I could spend $500,000 on used sewing machines (he knew a guy), rent a concrete building with no air-conditioning and hire a few dozen peyizan (Creole for “peasants”) for around $3 per day, I could recoup my investment within two years. And if it didn’t work out, he noted, I could sell the equipment to an entrepreneur in another poor nation.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19economy/mag-19Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19economy/mag-19Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19eco