 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e172e06-e5da-5cce-a56d-3ac5296f1ba4', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e172e06-e5da-5cce-a56d-3ac5296f1ba4'}, {'abstract': 'The story of the 55th Massachusetts Infantry, a black unit that demanded, and won, just compensation.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/fighting-for-the-union-and-equal-pay/', 'snippet': 'The story of the 55th Massachusetts Infantry, a black unit that demanded, and won, just compensation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly after the Emancipation Proclamation declared that African-Americans, “of suitable condition, will be received into the armed service of the United States,” the governor of Massachusetts, John Albion Andrew – one of the most aggressively antislavery officials to hold public office during the Civil War – put out a call across the North for men to fill the ranks of an exclusively black volunteer regiment. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting for the Union – and Equal Pay', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T16:31:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Soodalter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soodalter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7b7533f-efaa-5db4-a2ff-0a51d32f9638', 'word_count': 1386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7b7533f-efaa-5db4-a2ff-0a51d32f9638'}, {'abstract': 'Why the latest wave of scandals poses a particular challenge to Obama-ism.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/the-obama-synthesis-under-siege/', 'snippet': 'Why the latest wave of scandals poses a particular challenge to Obama-ism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The true ideological inclinations of the Obama White House can be endlessly debated, but slightly more than halfway through this presidency I think it’s fair to make the following generalization: Obama has governed as a business-friendly social democrat and an aggressive social liberal, as a hawkish interventionist when intervention seems cheap and easy (drones, missiles, etc.) and a cautious realist when it doesn’t, and as a surprisingly vigorous defender of presidential prerogatives across a variety of fronts. A few weeks ago, I characterized Obama-era liberalism as featuring “an imperial presidency, a corporatist economic policy, and then a libertarian turn on almost every social issue,” and while that line misses various nuances and complexities, as one-sentence summaries go I think it’s pretty good.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Obama Synthesis Under Siege', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosnell, Kermit', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obam