enter the living room with successful consumer electronics that connect the TV to the Internet, Google has continually failed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you walk into my living room you will see a standard, flat television surrounded by a dozen gadgets. There’s a Microsoft Xbox, a Sony PlayStation, the Apple TV, and there’s even a computer with a wireless mouse. But you might not recognize one of the gadgets, a round black ball that I haven’t used in a year: the\xa0Nexus Q made by Google.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/technology/bits-q/bits-q-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/technology/bits-q/bits-q-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/technology/bits-q/bits-q-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/14/technology/bits-q/bits-q-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/technology/bits-q/bits-q-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Living Room, Still No Sign of Google', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television Sets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T22:25:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/102f2c5d-7f18-545e-9579-e9c0ec5fcbd1', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/102f2c5d-7f18-545e-9579-e9c0ec5fcbd1'}, {'abstract': 'The president said it was. But technically, it’s not.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/is-the-i-r-s-an-independent-agency/', 'snippet': 'The president said it was. But technically, it’s not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is no evidence that President Obama knew about the Internal Revenue Service’s recent practice of singling out conservative groups that applied for tax-exempt status, subjecting them to unusually detailed questioning. Calling the reports “outrageous,” he has promised to “make sure that we find out exactly what happened on this.” That’s as it should be. But the president misspoke on Monday when he said that the I.R.S. was an “independent agency.” It is not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the I.R.S. an Independent Agency?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service Political Profiling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shulman, Douglas H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T22:28:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teresa Tritch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tritch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/285ed222-6613-5f72-8f38-2514145c3874', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/285ed222-6613-5f72-8f38-2514145c3874'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Estes’s life captured America’s imagination, with its tales of business scams, political payoffs, covered-up killings and White House conspiracies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/15/us/billie-sol-estes-texas-con-man-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Estes’s life captured America’s imagination, with its tales of business scams, political payoffs, covered-up killings and White House conspiracies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Billie Sol Estes, a fast-talking Texas swindler who made millions, went to prison and captivated America for years with mind-boggling agricultural scams, payoffs to politicians and bizarre tales of covered-up killings and White House conspiracies, was found dead on Tuesday at his home in Granbury, Tex. He was 88.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1401, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/15/us/ESTES1-obit/ESTES1-obit-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Billie Sol Estes, Texas Con Man Whose Fall Shook Up Washington, Dies at 88', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Billie Sol Estes, Texas Con Man Whose Fall Shook Up Washington, Dies at 88', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McFadden, Robert D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estes, Billie Sol (1925-2013)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-14T22:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. McFadden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b093ac0-1db6-5411-b39f-6c99a2701905', 'word_count': 1609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b093ac0-1db6-5411-b39f-6c99a2701905'}, {'abstract': 'A Syrian rebel commander who appeared to bite into the flesh of a government soldier’s corpse in a horrifying video clip posted on YouTube this week defended his actions in an interview with Time magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/14/syrian-rebel-reportedly-defends-sinking-his-teeth-into-dead-soldiers-flesh-on-camera/', 'snippet': 'A Syrian rebel commander who appeared to bite into the flesh of a government soldier’s corpse in a horrifying video clip posted on YouTube this week defended his actions in an interview with Time magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Syrian rebel commander who appeared to bite into the flesh of a government soldier’s corpse in a horrifying video clip posted on YouTube this week defended his actions in an interview with Time magazine on Tuesday. The rebel, Khalid al-Hamad, known by the nom de guerre Abu Sakkar, confirmed that he sank his teeth into an internal organ that he had laboriously carved out of the dead soldier’s chest as a colleague recorded the scene. “Our slogan,” he said, “is an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/world/middleeast/lede_khaled_hamad/lede_khaled_hamad-articleLarge.png', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/14/world/middleeast/lede_khaled_hamad/lede_khaled_hamad-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/world/middleeast/lede_khaled_hamad/lede_khaled_hamad-jumbo.png', 'height': 377, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/14/world/middle