'Eduardo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Porter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4761964-6277-588a-9f8d-abdb0e52747e', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4761964-6277-588a-9f8d-abdb0e52747e'}, {'abstract': 'A Southern legal advocacy group has claimed in a lawsuit that the State of South Carolina should not have surgically altered a 16-month-old child in its custody.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/15/us/south-carolina-sex-surgery-unnecessary-lawsuit-alleges.html', 'snippet': 'A Southern legal advocacy group has claimed in a lawsuit that the State of South Carolina should not have surgically altered a 16-month-old child in its custody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Southern legal advocacy group has claimed in a lawsuit that the State of South Carolina should not have surgically altered a 16-month-old child in its custody who was born with both male and female genitalia. The suit, filed in both state and federal courts, was brought on behalf of the child’s adoptive parents and is intended to restrict the practice of medically unnecessary sex assignment surgeries on infants, said lawyers with the Southern Poverty Law Center. The child was in the care of the South Carolina Department of Social Services when doctors, in cooperation with social services employees, removed the child’s male genitals. The child, now 8, identifies himself as a boy, according to his parents, Mark and Pam Crawford of Richland County. “They disfigured him because they could not accept him for who he was,” Mrs. Crawford said in a statement, “not because he needed any surgery.” Increasingly, doctors and those who were born with a condition once called hermaphroditism recommend that children be assigned a gender at birth but postpone surgery until they are old enough to choose. A spokeswoman for the state’s Social Services Department said there would be no comment on pending legal action. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Carolina: Sex Surgery Unnecessary, Lawsuit Alleges', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Carolina: Sex Surgery Unnecessary, Lawsuit Alleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Social Services', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern Poverty Law Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-15T00:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/46cac7f9-b98d-56a5-8f12-79a3d5d40af0', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46cac7f9-b98d-56a5-8f12-79a3d5d40af0'}, {'abstract': 'Scores of other countries have elaborate retirement schemes and policies that Americans could learn from to address some of the biggest pitfalls of their system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/15/business/retirementspecial/international-retirement-plans-offer-insight-to-aid-americas-system.html', 'snippet': 'Scores of other countries have elaborate retirement schemes and policies that Americans could learn from to address some of the biggest pitfalls of their system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE United States can boast that it has the world’s best basketball players, fighter jets and country and western singers. But hardly anyone would ever boast that the United States has the world’s best retirement system.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How They Do It Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Retirement Special Section', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How They Do It Elsewhere', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenhouse, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Security (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Individual Retirement Accounts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '401(k), 403(b) and 457 Plans', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Singapore', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-15T00:15:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Retirement', 'byline': {'origin