r and a bit different than the usual hump day fare.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Worries You, Masters You', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-15T02:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0082a17b-efab-5ffc-b677-4c243fb10442', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0082a17b-efab-5ffc-b677-4c243fb10442'}, {'abstract': 'A rule stated that if retirees withdrew 4.5 percent of their savings every year — adjusted for inflation — their nest eggs would last 30 years. Not anymore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/15/business/retirementspecial/the-4-rule-for-retirement-withdrawals-may-be-outdated.html', 'snippet': 'A rule stated that if retirees withdrew 4.5 percent of their savings every year — adjusted for inflation — their nest eggs would last 30 years. Not anymore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE thing most retirees want to avoid is outliving their money. Since the mid-1990s many of them have relied on a staple of retirement planning known as the 4 percent rule to avoid that. Although the name says 4 percent, the rule is that if retirees withdraw 4.5 percent of their savings every year, adjusted for inflation, their nest egg should last 30 years, the length of time generally used for retirement planning. ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '4% Rule for Retirement Withdrawals Is Golden No More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None,