b_date': '2013-05-15T20:43:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31a0134e-e404-580f-bbf5-205ad2ab139c', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31a0134e-e404-580f-bbf5-205ad2ab139c'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a rare event to have New York City Ballet perform an entire program of Peter Martins’s shorter works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-at-the-david-h-koch-theater.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a rare event to have New York City Ballet perform an entire program of Peter Martins’s shorter works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been 30 years since the death of George Balanchine, the unsurpassed choreographer and co-founder of New York City Ballet, which also means it has been 30 years since Peter Martins took over the stewardship of the company. In addition to running City Ballet and its affiliate School of American Ballet, Mr. Martins has choreographed nearly 90 ballets for the troupe. Yet only once, in 2000, did he present a program made up exclusively of his own shorter works. On Tuesday night at the David H. Koch Theater, he did it again.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/arts/16CALCIUM/16CALCIUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/16/arts/16CALCIUM/16CALCIUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/arts/16CALCIUM