': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee886581-6686-5eca-8903-bc47ca1f2afd', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee886581-6686-5eca-8903-bc47ca1f2afd'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court ruled Wednesday that a German family could not claim asylum in the United States because it would face penalties in Germany for home-schooling its children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/us/tennessee-home-schooling-family-is-denied-asylum.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court ruled Wednesday that a German family could not claim asylum in the United States because it would face penalties in Germany for home-schooling its children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court ruled Wednesday that a German family could not claim asylum in the United States because it would face penalties in Germany for home-schooling its children. The Romeike family wanted to educate five children at home “largely for religious reasons,” the decision stated, but were prohibited by German law and faced prosecution. The Romeikes moved to Tennessee in 2008 and were granted asylum by an immigration judge, but the Board of Immigration Appeals overturned that decision in 2012. The United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, in Cincinnati, ruled unanimously Wednesday that the Romeikes could not prove that home-schoolers were singled out for persecution in Germany, and thus were not eligible for asylum. The family intends to appeal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tennessee: Home-schooling Family Is Denied Asylum', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tennessee: Home-Schooling Family Is Denied Asylum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Schooling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T03:19:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Campbell Robertson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Campbell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea175aa1-f398-5502-a48f-c09a044fbf31', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea175aa1-f398-5502-a48f-c09a044fbf31'}, {'abstract': 'Parents in Chicago have filed two federal lawsuits opposing proposed school closings. In March, the Chicago Public Schools identified 53 schools that it planned to close.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/us/illinois-lawsuits-filed-over-chicago-school-closings.html', 'snippet': 'Parents in Chicago have filed two federal lawsuits opposing proposed school closings. In March, the Chicago Public Schools identified 53 schools that it planned to close.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Parents in Chicago have filed two federal lawsuits opposing proposed school closings. In March, the Chicago Public Schools identified 53 schools that it planned to close to save $560 million over 10 years. The school board will vote on the proposal next Wednesday. The suits, filed Wednesday in Federal District Court for the Northern District of Illinois Eastern Division, say the closings would violate the Americans With Disabilities Act. In a complaint seeking to postpone them for a year, three parents seeking class-action status allege that the closings would pose “significant disruption” to special-education students. The second suit, which seeks to stop the closings altogether, alleges that they would “needlessly uproot, transfer and destabilize the children.” The suit further says the closings would violate Illinois civil rights law because the schools fall disproportionately in areas where the vast majority of students are black. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Illinois: Lawsuits Filed Over Chicago School Closings', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illinois: Lawsuits Filed Over Chicago School Closings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T03:21:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce8dcade-9b6a-5565-b9ab-4d8c38d5a729', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce8dcade-9b6a-5565-b9ab-4d8c38d5a729'}, {'abstract': 'Horse racing may be a family pastime, but it is also a business — an expensive one — and the Phippses have used savvy methods in improving their stock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/05/16/sports/16racing_ss.html', 'snippet': 'Horse racing may be a family pastime, but it is also a business — an expensive one — and the Phippses have used savvy methods in improving their stock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Horse racing may be a family pastime, but it is also a business — an expensive one — and the Phippses have used savvy methods in improving their stock.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarg