 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T03:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/fb93dfc3-57bb-541f-9263-b1752ecbd505', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/fb93dfc3-57bb-541f-9263-b1752ecbd505'}, {'abstract': 'A lawyer for Ariel Castro, the Cleveland man accused of imprisoning three women in his home for about a decade, said that his client planned to plead not guilty to criminal charges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/us/ohio-abduction-suspect-plans-to-plead-not-guilty.html', 'snippet': 'A lawyer for Ariel Castro, the Cleveland man accused of imprisoning three women in his home for about a decade, said that his client planned to plead not guilty to criminal charges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lawyer for Ariel Castro, the Cleveland man accused of imprisoning three women in his home for about a decade, said Wednesday that his client planned to plead not guilty to criminal charges. The lawyer, Jaye M. Schlachet, said his client had been “demonized” in news reports and expressed concern about a fair trial. Mr. Castro, 52, has been accused of kidnapping and raping the women, who vanished between 2002 and 2004. They were rescued last week, along with a daughter that one gave birth to in captivity. A paternity test confirmed that Mr. Castro is the father of the child, who is now 6.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/us/alt-16brfs-castro/alt-16brfs-castro-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/16/us/alt-16brfs-castro/alt-16brfs-castro-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 847}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty