-05-16T18:55:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7118838e-992a-5f81-adae-a1ddb1428a03', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7118838e-992a-5f81-adae-a1ddb1428a03'}, {'abstract': 'In a new Manhattan gallery show, the Belgian artist Wim Delvoye offers a more mature version of his usual pot-stirring antics.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/on-view-new-provocations-from-the-belgian-bad-boy-wim-delvoye/', 'snippet': 'In a new Manhattan gallery show, the Belgian artist Wim Delvoye offers a more mature version of his usual pot-stirring antics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Belgian artist Wim Delvoye is known for making his friends perform sexual acts in front of X-ray machines, tattooing live pigs to look like Louis Vuitton handbags (and selling the hides and taxidermied animals from his farm in China) and finding the “humanness” in processes, like digestion, that don’t exactly scream “artful.” But the show he opened last weekend at the Bowery gallery Sperone Westwater suggests a new maturity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Provocations From the Belgian Bad Boy Wim Delvoye', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louvre Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T19:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marina Cashdan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cashdan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17464427-59f6-50b0-adb9-5eafa5af35f1', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17464427-59f6-50b0-adb9-5eafa5af35f1'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Mooallem’s first book explores our complicated relationship to endangered species.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/what-we-see-when-we-look-at-wild-animals/', 'snippet': 'Jon Mooallem’s first book explores our complicated relationship to endangered species.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jon Mooallem, the author of last week’s cover story about the mysterious murders of some Hawaiian monk seals, has written often for the magazine about animals and what they mean to us. He has explored same-sex coupling in the animal world (“The Love that Dare Not Squawk Its Name”), for example, and helping turtles hatch after an oil spill (“The BP-Spill Baby-Turtle Brigade”). His first book, “Wild Ones,” published today, is also about animals or, more properly, about “looking at people looking at animals.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We See When We Look at Wild Animals', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T19:04:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ef12a89-606d-5655-94e8-5067cffb1f9e', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ef12a89-606d-5655-94e8-5067cffb1f9e'}, {'abstract': 'Making sandwiches out of bread is just not practical when you’re orbiting Earth.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/tortillas-in-space/', 'snippet': 'Making sandwiches out of bread is just not practical when you’re orbiting Earth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'http://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=AZx0RIV0wss', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tortillas in Space', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Space Station', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tortillas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadfield, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-16T19:06:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80c5a60c-fc56-56e1-b4df-bb5d0aed1fd2', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80c5a60c-fc56-56e1-b4df-bb5d0aed1fd2'}, {'abstract': 'I created a page on youarelistening.to that reads The New York Times headlines aloud while ambient music plays in the background.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/16/you-are-listening-to-the-new-york-times/', 'snippet': 'I created a page on youarelistening.to that reads The New York Times headlines aloud while ambient music plays in the background.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the San Francisco Giants won the 2010 World Series, the post-victory celebrations got out of control. Revelers smashed windows, got into fistfights and started fires. A Muni bus and the metaverse were both set