k and Avon Avenues. Everyone scattered. After the cops arrived and taped off the intersection and determined that no one at the scene had been hit, the street slowly stirred back to life. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1419, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/opinion/0517OPEDmenchin/0517OPEDmenchin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gunshots on Warm Spring Evenings', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gunshots on Warm Spring Evenings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schuppe, Jonathan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T01:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schuppe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuppe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c02f027-14c7-53cd-bab5-8434c21d8532', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c02f027-14c7-53cd-bab5-8434c21d8532'}, {'abstract': 'How malpractice risk can make hospitals safer places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/opinion/how-health-care-is-learning-from-lawsuits.html', 'snippet': 'How malpractice risk can make hospitals safer places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — MUCH of the discussion over the Affordable Care Act has focused on whether it will bring down health care costs. Less attention has been paid to another goal of the act: improving patient safety. Each year tens of thousands of people die, and hundreds of thousands more are injured, as a result of medical error.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning From Litigation', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Learning From Litigation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Joanna C', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malpractice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T01:05:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna C. Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/91aa529d-8d72-582c-8837-fa14c06c03c2', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91aa529d-8d72-582c-8837-fa14c06c03c2'}, {'abstract': 'How can we reconcile the advancement of legal same-sex marriage in much of Latin America with the stereotype of Latin culture as a bastion of religiosity and machismo?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/opinion/how-latin-culture-got-more-gay.html', 'snippet': 'How can we reconcile the advancement of legal same-sex marriage in much of Latin America with the stereotype of Latin culture as a bastion of religiosity and machismo?', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAMBRIDGE, Mass. — BRAZIL is potentially poised to become the third and largest country in Latin America to legalize same-sex marriage, following a judicial order on Tuesday. Argentina was the first, in 2010, after the government brushed aside objections from Cardinal Jorge Bergoglio of Buenos Aires, now the pope. The Uruguayan legislature followed suit last month. Mexico City has allowed such unions since 2010, and the Mexican state of Quintana Roo since 2011. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Latin Culture Got More Gay', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Latin Culture Got More Gay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T01:06:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Héctor Carrillo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Héctor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrillo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ea8358b-09ca-5506-82db-8db04d7b43c9', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ea8358b-09ca-5506-82db-8db04d7b43c9'}, {'abstract': 'For seekers of religious or secular reading on the Upper East Side, Logos is a cozy bookstore with a lumpy recliner and a black cat named Boo Boo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/nyregion/neighborhood-joint-yorkville-bookshop-nurtures-mind-and-spirit.html', 'snippet': 'For seekers of religious or secular reading on the Upper East Side, Logos is a cozy bookstore with a lumpy recliner and a black cat named Boo Boo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To readers of philosophy, “logos” means reason and rational argument. To readers of the Gospels, it is the word of God made incarnate in Jesus Christ. But for seekers of all kinds on the Upper East Side, Logos is also a cozy bookshop with a lumpy recliner and a black cat named Boo Boo, who sleeps near a stack of Bibles.', 'print