 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'SARA BECK', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'BECK', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ea25da6a-84b7-58ea-b577-d83656a464c9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ea25da6a-84b7-58ea-b577-d83656a464c9'}, {'abstract': 'Foxconn Technology has made progress toward better safety conditions, but employees are still working longer than Chinese law allows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/business/foxconn-audit-reveals-workweek-still-too-long.html', 'snippet': 'Foxconn Technology has made progress toward better safety conditions, but employees are still working longer than Chinese law allows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Foxconn Technology, the company that manufactures Apple’s popular iPads and iPhones, has made substantial progress toward improving safety and other working conditions at three of its Chinese plants dedicated to making Apple products. But it has not yet achieved the most difficult goal: reducing the average workweek to the maximum allowed by Chinese law, a global monitoring group said on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/business/Foxconn/Foxconn-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/17/business/Foxconn/Foxconn-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/business/Foxconn/Foxconn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non