aph': 'I woke to a nudge. “The pilot’s going to make an announcement,” the flight attendant said. I palmed at my eyes, nodded and looked around, feeling my hangover creep back in. The girl next to me was flicking at her nails while she paged through a fashion magazine. I slumped back against the window after the flight attendant passed. White clouds blanketed the sky floor.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 266, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 266, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Plane Was About to Crash. Now What?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Land Unknown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shannon, Noah Gallagher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Gallagher Shannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': 'Gallagher', 'lastname': 'Shannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03961a32-6119-5f98-a059-9f549461d862', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03961a32-6119-5f98-a059-9f549461d862'}, {'abstract': 'It’s all in the delivery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/magazine/does-the-postal-service-owe-me-money.html', 'snippet': 'It’s all in the delivery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently sent a parcel via U.S. Express Mail. It was guaranteed to be delivered the next day by noon. The final delivery scan was at 12:03 p.m. I am entitled to a full refund of the postage I paid. However, the difference between noon and 12:03 is negligible — it did not affect me or the recipient. I simply needed the item delivered overnight and received the service I paid for. I’m fully satisfied. Is it ethical to request a full refund under these circumstances? ROSS NIZLEK, BURLINGTON, VT.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},