ticle/2bcc5d0c-073a-59c4-bc25-c9b4d739e04f'}, {'abstract': 'It wasn’t until dawn on Thursday that the Hess Corporation settled a proxy fight waged by Elliott Management. | Laurene Powell Jobs is tiptoeing into the public sphere to push her philanthropic agenda. | Fred Eckert, a onetime Goldman Sachs partner who lost his fortune, is trying to make a comeback. | Andrew Mason, Groupon’s former chief, has returned to the spotlight.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/elliott-and-hess-shake-hands/', 'snippet': 'It wasn’t until dawn on Thursday that the Hess Corporation settled a proxy fight waged by Elliott Management. | Laurene Powell Jobs is tiptoeing into the public sphere to push her philanthropic agenda. | Fred Eckert, a onetime Goldman Sachs partne...', 'lead_paragraph': 'BITTER STANDOFF AT HESS GIVES WAY TO SETTLEMENT \xa0|\xa0 It wasn’t until dawn on Thursday, just hours before the annual meeting, that the Hess Corporation finally settled a proxy fight waged by the activist hedge fund Elliott Management. The oil company agreed to give Elliott three board seats in exchange for the fund’s support of the company’s slate of five directors; all told, nine new directors are joining the board, DealBook’s Michael J. de la Merced reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Elliott and Hess Shake Hands', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T11:32:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/681f2f24-c39f-5e90-ae15-397c1f22566f', 'word_count': 1707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/681f2f24-c39f-5e90-ae