inal': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4adb60b2-9d9a-5f33-aec7-60e6ffb76924', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4adb60b2-9d9a-5f33-aec7-60e6ffb76924'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: A family waiting to board a flight wondered what kind of animal was making loud squeaking noises in a pet carrier.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/a-skittish-bird-at-the-airport/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: A family waiting to board a flight wondered what kind of animal was making loud squeaking noises in a pet carrier.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Skittish Bird at the Airport', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newark Liberty International Airport (NJ)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T12:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Debbie Shapiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Debbie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shapiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66a25c37-ee90-515a-8ade-21e144b81515', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66a25c37-ee90-515a-8ade-21e144b81515'}, {'abstract': 'There are puzzles, but not the ones the Bernanke-haters claim.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/too-much-talk-about-liquidity/', 'snippet': 'There are puzzles, but not the ones the Bernanke-haters claim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antonio Fatas is annoyed at Gillian Tett, who talks to the I-see-bubbles crowd and assumes that they have The Truth — namely, that those crazy central banks are flooding the world with liquidity, driving asset prices to crazy levels, and it will all end in terrible grief. Pretty much the same discussion we’ve been having about the armageddon hedgies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Too Much Talk About Liquidity', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T12:47:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c209e061-76a6-5b29-a787-1020e7fa5c03', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c209e061-76a6-5b29-a787-1020e7fa5c03'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Mason, the co-founder of Groupon, will be a part-time partner at Y Combinator, the Silicon Valley start-up incubator. He also plans to start a company.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/andrew-masons-life-after-groupon/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Mason, the co-founder of Groupon, will be a part-time partner at Y Combinator, the Silicon Valley start-up incubator. He also plans to start a company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Mason is back.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Andrew Mason’s Life After Groupon', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Part-Time Employment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mason, Andrew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Groupon Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Y Combinator', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T12:47:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d939f9cc-f9fb-587c-9039-8ee67df2615d', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d939f9cc-f9fb-587c-9039-8ee67df2615d'}, {'abstract': 'The campaign to elect Israel’s top rabbis is more competitive, fractious and, yes, dirty than running for the Knesset.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/to-elect-israels-chief-rabbis-a-dirty-political-campaign/', 'snippet': 'The campaign to elect Israel’s top rabbis is more competitive, fractious and, yes, dirty than running for the Knesset.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — The two presiding rabbis of Israel’s Chief Rabbinate — one Ashkenazi, the other Sephardic — completed their 10-year term three months ago, but they were asked to stay until new chief rabbis are elected. The new rabbis can’t be chosen until a so-called electing body — 150 members, including mayors, rabbis and other assorted appointees — is selected by a committee of five, comprising two politicians, two rabbis and a judge appointed by the minister of religious affairs. And the new electing body can’t be selected until Israeli politicians can agree on how.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rabbis’ Campaign', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rabbis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Metzger, Yona', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T12:52:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c3dee9b-60d6-5997-8ea1-a32bd05968e6', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c3dee9b-60d6-5997-8ea1-a32bd05968e6'}, {'abstract': 'A creative couple collaborates to create gorgeous, white oak furniture for Ralph Pucci International.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/objects-chris-lehrecke-and-gabriella-kiss/', 'snippet': 'A creative couple collaborates to create gorgeous, white oak furniture for Ralph Pucci International.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The furniture designer Chris Lehrecke and the jewelry designer Gabriella Kiss have been married for more than two decades, and their upstate studios are a stone’s throw from one another. Given their mutual affinity for nature — he treats wood in an organic way, and her subject matter includes plants and insects — it’s a bit surprising that they’ve never done a big project together. Until now, that is. “After the Storm,” a collection that makes its debut on Monday at Ralph Pucci International, is the couple’s first major collaboration. Although the show features solo designs by each (wooden tortoises and lily-pad tables by Kiss, and tree-mushroom-shaped wall shelves by Lehrecke, to name a few), their joint efforts include a series of pieces in white oak designed by Lehrecke with cast-bronze pulls (also mushroom-shaped) by Kiss. The long, lean chest of drawers shown here, which incorporates a branch from a dead oak tree, is $22,000 at Ralph Pucci International (44 West 18th Street).', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Objects | Chris Lehrecke and Gabriella Kiss’s Wooden Wonders', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Objects: Outdoors In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lehrecke, Chris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T13:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93eb65e1-a4ee-508e-abb2-ecd9da8a86d7', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93eb65e1-a4ee-508e-abb2-ecd9da8a86d7'}, {'abstract': 'It’s always sunny in Ireland.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/that-hideous-strength/', 'snippet': 'It’s always sunny in Ireland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The FT has an interesting article on problems with Irish GNP (not GDP) accounting; essentially, measured Irish income is being inflated by foreign companies with no real activity there that nonetheless find ways to make profits materialize in a low-tax jurisdiction. We sort of knew this was happening — that, for example, a lot of the apparent rise in productivity was just a shift to pharma companies that produce a lot of reported value-added but add little to the Irish economy. But the new ESRI report suggests that the problem is bigger than realized.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'That Hideous Strength', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T13:01:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d93866d-3300-5e56-b130-5aa4f17a651e', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d93866d-3300-5e56-b130-5aa4f17a651e'}, {'abstract': 'After an eventful week, some thoughtful pieces are worth readers’ attention.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/for-extra-credit-a-little-light-reading-on-press-rights/', 'snippet': 'After an eventful week, some thoughtful pieces are worth readers’ attention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With press rights very much in the news this week, here are some of the most noteworthy pieces I’ve come across on the subject.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Extra Credit: A Little Light Reading on Press Rights', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T13:02:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/631277aa-a1d7-542d-ac39-d09289a61148', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/631277aa-a1d7-542d-ac39-d09289a61148'}, {'abstract': 'That Whitewater feeling.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/that-90s-show/', 'snippet': 'That Whitewater feeling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I picked a good week to be away — and I am still away, mostly, although playing a bit of hooky on the notebook right now. For it has been the week of OBAMA SCANDALS, nonstop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'That 90s Show', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T13:11:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb2c3d86-d120-5042-a16c-7bea8577427d', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb2c3d86-d120-5042-a16c-7bea8577427d'}, {'abstract': 'A number of controversies have put Jay Carney, a former reporter turned White House press secretary, under a white-hot spotlight, but he says he likes it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/18/us/politics/reporter-turned-white-house-spokesman-enjoys-the-hot-seat.html', 'snippet': 'A number of controversies have put Jay Carney, a former reporter turned White House press secretary, under a white-hot spotlight, but he says he likes it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — For the better part of a decade, Jay Carney sat slumped in his tiny chair in the White House briefing room, parsing, challenging and at times pillorying the words of an array of press secretaries.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '