 [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacey Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f0d859f-f7cf-50f1-aded-c7b483b5ae93', 'word_count': 4509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f0d859f-f7cf-50f1-aded-c7b483b5ae93'}, {'abstract': 'Brian Stelter recounts the intrigue, infighting and personal crises in the morning TV shows’ ratings wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/top-of-the-morning-by-brian-stelter.html', 'snippet': 'Brian Stelter recounts the intrigue, infighting and personal crises in the morning TV shows’ ratings wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Was Matt mean to Ann? How is Robin’s health? Is Charlie, that nocturnal man about town, \xadreally suited to rise-and-shine rigmarole? That most readers need no help in supplying context — the players here are Matt Lauer and Ann Curry on NBC’s “Today” show, Robin Roberts on ABC’s “Good Morning America” and Charlie Rose on whatever that daytime CBS program is called these days — is a testament to how personally so many millions of viewers take their morning TV. Specifically, it’s a toothbrush-holding thumbs-up to their watch-it-then-and-there network morning TV, an impressive distinction (not to mention a relic) in a DVR-time-shifted world of countless cable and Internet options. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19SCHWARZBAUM/19SCHWARZBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', '