l', 'snippet': 'The troubles of Tom Drury’s characters seem to stretch back into the inscrutable past, and out into an equally murky future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a paragraph you might find a little strange: “Micah had ridden three times, got thrown once. He found horses hard to read. Their thoughts might go back to the beginning of horse time, or they might be afraid of a candy wrapper on the ground. He was wary of anything that big that bit.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 785}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1565, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19HANDLER/19HANDLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Aberrations and Apparitions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aberrations and Apparitions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drury, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:16:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Handler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e867c15d-765b-5a44-b40d-b8caa1e7be0e', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e867c15d-765b-5a44-b40d-b8caa1e7be0e'}, {'abstract': 'Fiona Maazel’s antic new novel examines the contradictory but concurrent urges for solitude and intimacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/woke-up-lonely-by-fiona-maazel.html', 'snippet': 'Fiona Maazel’s antic new novel examines the contradictory but concurrent urges for solitude and intimacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Loneliness can be divided into two types: transient and chronic. The first is more common than the second, meaning most people feel lonely sometimes and some people feel lonely most of the time. Transients aren’t interesting. Like hunger or fatigue, their loneliness comes and goes and isn’t defining: talk to them for an hour and they forget it. Chronics, though, are another story. They feel lonely playing games and being with family and moving through crowds and having sex. They can rack up thousands of Facebook friends and build a nation of Twitter followers, and it doesn’t matter. They know they are inescapably alone.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1996, 'width': 1675, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19EMMONS/19EMMONS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Love Me. Leave Me Alone.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love Me. Leave Me Alone.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maazel, Fiona', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Emmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbf2a2ee-1438-5762-a419-e6b9c28b8d40', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbf2a2ee-1438-5762-a419-e6b9c28b8d40'}, {'abstract': 'Recording an audiobook requires talent, discipline and a lot of coffee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/when-words-sing.html', 'snippet': 'Recording an audiobook requires talent, discipline and a lot of coffee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you spend a lot of time with audiobooks, you start paying close attention to the people who read them, and probably develop a stable of favorites. Listeners know that the best narrators can make a good book take wing and a merely decent book grow more engaging. They can carry us through the dry parts of nonfiction, and might get us to try something we otherwise might not have. (That’s how I ended up enjoying Elmore Leonard’s “Stick” — I wanted to hear the great Frank Muller.) A mediocre reading? Well, I stop listening, and if it’s a book I really need to read, I switch to paper. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-bks-Schwartz1/0519-bks-Schwartz1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-bks-Schwartz1/0519-bks-Schwartz1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-bks-Schwartz1/0519-bks-Schwartz1-jumbo.jpg', '