tiglitz argues, isn’t the result of uncontrollable technological and social change; it stems from the unrestrained power of well-heeled interests. In this comprehensive counterargument to both neoliberalism and laissez-faire theories, the author, a Nobel laureate and economics professor at Columbia, examines the effects of inequality on the economy, democracy and globalization.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f01803d-75c8-5af9-80e6-938903a10c0d', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f01803d-75c8-5af9-80e6-938903a10c0d'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SLEEPWALKERS: How Europe Went to War in 1914,  by Christopher Clark. (Harper, $29.99.) A Cambridge professor offers a thoroughly comprehensible account of the polarization of a continent, without fixing guilt on one leader or nation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b13674f2-386c-55e7-a412-21b6fff70f00', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b13674f2-386c-55e7-a412-21b6fff70f00'}, {'abstract': '“Happy, Happy, Happy” by Phil Robertson is No. 1 on the hardcover nonfiction list — the second best seller spawned by the reality show “Duck Dynasty.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“Happy, Happy, Happy” by Phil Robertson is No. 1 on the hardcover nonfiction list — the second best seller spawned by the reality show “Duck Dynasty.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' DUCK SEASON: Phil Robertson, the backwoods Louisiana patriarch and hunting entrepreneur who stars on the A&E reality show “Duck Dynasty,” rules the hardcover nonfiction list this week — his memoir, “Happy, Happy, Happy” (written with Mark Schlabach), is new at No. 1. It’s not the first best seller the show has spawned, either: “The Duck Commander Family,” by Phil’s son Willie and daughter-in-law Korie (with Schlabach again), is at No. 7 in its sixth week, riding the coattails of a season finale that drew 9.6 million viewers last month. “Happy, Happy, Happy” takes its title from Robertson’s catchphrase, and presents itself as a rustic guide to the good life — one chapter, “Redneck Caviar,” declares that “Rule No. 11 for living happy, happy, happy” is to “suck the head of a crawfish (you’ll want to do it again and again).” Part of the Robertsons’ charm is that they seem genuinely well adjusted, like the Waltons’ hairier cousins; every episode ends with a family prayer and a dinner prepared by Phil’s wife, Kay, whom he married when he was 16 and she was 15. “If you can find a nice, pretty country girl who can cook and carries her Bible,” he advises readers, “now, there’s a woman. She might even be ugly, but if she cooks squirrels and dumplings, then that’s the woman you go after.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19list-img/19list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19list-img/19list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 457, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19list-img/19list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19list-img/19list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19list-img/19list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:32:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cb6d819-1a23-5e2c-8ccd-9544c98a6d67', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cb6d819-1a23-5e2c-8ccd-9544c98a6d67'}, {'abstract': 'At the center of the plot lines in Jean Thompson’s novel is a philanthropist who wants to bribe people into virtue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/the-humanity-project-by-jean-thompson.html', 'snippet': 'At the center of the plot lines in Jean Thompson’s novel is a philanthropist who wants to bribe people into virtue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Linnea is a good kid from a home that lies somewhere in that wide gray zone between stable and chaotic, more dysfunctional than not — in other words, an emblematic American household. Her stepdad is “O.K.,” her younger half-brother is “pretty cool,” her stepsister is a mean girl and a bully; but Linnea loves her mom, and since she can’t remember her feckless, long-lost father, she doesn’t really miss him. Theirs is a typical blended family, so sadly representative that the nightmare that demolishes their relative peace and turns Linnea into an unreachable, volatile teenager is a school shooting — a signature American tragedy. Suffice it to say that the details of that awful day are blood-chilling and unbearable. As survivors go, Linnea is especially unlucky. She’s earned her trauma, and she wears it like a badge.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1293, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/0519-Schulman/0519-Schulman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paid to Be Good', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paid to Be Good', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Jean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:34:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Schulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a12a3af5-fcdf-5ef4-84a9-baf1c7410cb5', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a12a3af5-fcdf-5ef4-84a9-baf1c7410cb5'}, {'abstract': 'Sportswriters used the soccer star David Beckham’s retirement to analyze his global appeal and to recall him as a brand as much as a player.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/theres-soccer-and-then-theres-beckham.html', 'snippet': 'Sportswriters used the soccer star David Beckham’s retirement to analyze his global appeal and to recall him as a brand as much as a player.', 'lead_paragraph': "For a while, Thursday seemed like a perfect day for goodbyes. And in a way, the timing was perfect. It is, after all, graduation season. David Beckham kicked things off by announcing he would graduate to being just a global icon, minus the soccer-playing part. The Knicks seemed poised to move on to the golf-playing portion of their season with an elimination game against the Indiana Pacers, but decided to stick around, perhaps just to annoy people, including their doomsaying fans. But we did say goodbye to Rangers goalie Henrik Lundqvist’s perfection and any chance the N.B.A.'s Western Conference playoffs would be moderately fun to watch.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1408, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/18/sports/soccer/18leading/18leading-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bigger Than Soccer Itself', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckham, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manchester United (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duncan, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Antonio Spurs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lundqvist, Henrik', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marchand, Brad', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, George', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T15:34:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41d91992-a969-528b-9855-73fee3d2afd6', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41d91992-a969-528b-9855-73fee3d2afd6'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Kingsley’s 150-year-old morality fable reflects the contradictory impulses of the Victorian era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/books/review/beautiful-creatures.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Kingsley’s 150-year-old morality fable reflects the contradictory impulses of the Victorian era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The golden age of children’s literature — beginning in the 1860s with Charles Kingsley’s “Water-\xadBabies” and Lewis Carroll’s “Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland” and ending with the publication of the Winnie-the-Pooh books in the 1920s — earned its name by turning stories into luminous contact zones for adults and children. A million golden arrows point to Neverland, and you can reach Oz by passing through a gate studded with glittering emeralds. The heft of “Or else!” in cautionary tales about children going up in flames after playing with matches was replaced with the incandescent beauty of “What if?” Writers aspired to lure children into fantasy worlds that would leave them, as Frances Hodgson Burnett put it, “breathing quite fast with excitement, and wonder, and delight.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19tatar/19tatar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19tatar/19tatar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 598}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/books/review/19tatar/19tatar-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1021, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None