e, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e3b05f1-d031-55c3-9e0b-4c5b2029a6cc', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e3b05f1-d031-55c3-9e0b-4c5b2029a6cc'}, {'abstract': 'A flurry of discussion about public misperception of climate scientists’ views misses another science perception gap.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/17/the-other-climate-science-gap/', 'snippet': 'A flurry of discussion about public misperception of climate scientists’ views misses another science perception gap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much has been made this week of the gap between what the public thinks about the consensus among climate scientists over the human factor in global warming and the actual level of consensus. The discussion has centered on a new study reviewing how anthropogenic global warming was characterized in more than 12,000 climate science papers between 1991 and 2011. More than 97 percent of the papers stating a cause for warming, the authors found, pointed to humans. In contrast, surveys consistently show that Americans are pretty evenly divided when asked whether they think scientists agree that humans are causing global warming.\xa0(Read\xa0my e-mail exchange with two authors\xa0for more background.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other Climate Science Gap', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 