lated Park and Conservation in the Anthropocene', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-17T18:49:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14fbcce7-ab8b-5a50-b222-ed4fa4f8f0b8', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14fbcce7-ab8b-5a50-b222-ed4fa4f8f0b8'}, {'abstract': 'For guests who like to spend the night in a place marbled with local flavor, these seven properties, from Maine to California, can accommodate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/travel/hotels-warm-up-with-local-accents.html', 'snippet': 'For guests who like to spend the night in a place marbled with local flavor, these seven properties, from Maine to California, can accommodate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While sleek minimalism set the tone for urban boutique hotels in the 1990s, rustic elements are warming up the newest crop of small hotels. These trendsetters emphasize their sense of place through local themes, building materials, restaurant fare, entertainment or a combination of them all.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/travel/19SUBHOTEL3/19SUBHOTEL3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/19/travel/19SUBHOTEL3/19SUBHOTEL3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/travel/1