'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52417c78-821b-563c-8e8e-01ca7c4ee89e', 'word_count': 1025, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52417c78-821b-563c-8e8e-01ca7c4ee89e'}, {'abstract': 'From Simone de Beauvoir to Jimmy Savile, on the trail of the intellectual origins of the “culture of abuse.”', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/savile-beauvoir-and-the-charms-of-the-nymph/', 'snippet': 'From Simone de Beauvoir to Jimmy Savile, on the trail of the intellectual origins of the “culture of abuse.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since the details of the Jimmy Savile case started to come to light, I have, like some philosophical gumshoe, been pursuing an investigation into the possible intellectual origin of his mentality. In other words, I have been looking into what has loosely been referred to as the “culture of abuse,” which may or may not have been a factor at the BBC, or what Michel Foucault would have called the “epistémé” — a kind of conspiracy at the level of language and ideas and metaphor that (in this context) could conceivably promote or justify the sexual exploitation of young girls. (Alongside its continuing “Savile review,” the BBC has also promised to examine the case of Stuart Hall, another broadcaster, who has pleaded guilty to indecently assaulting 13 girls over three decades.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Persistence of the ‘Lolita Syndrome’', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beauvoir, Simone de', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savile, Jimmy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-19T23:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8920f105-0295-55a6-aed0-b2990f380dc7', 'word_count': 2027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8920f105-0295-55a6-aed0-b2990f380dc7'}, {'abstract': 'Tomorrow evening.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/19/inequality-and-growth-discussed/', 'snippet': 'Tomorrow evening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been in transit all day, so no column in tomorrow’s paper. Incidentally, I saw evidence of impressive investment in infrastructure while away — unfortunately, by beavers, not state and local governments:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality and Growth, Discussed', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-19T23:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2f85f74-dd95-5e72-9770-85be4c865abc', 'word_count': 66, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2f85f74-dd95-5e72-9770-85be4c865abc'}, {'abstract': 'The director of the Center on National Security at Fordham has made herself an expert on matters of torture, terrorism trials and the military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/nyregion/vexed-by-terrorism-trials-and-guantanamo-and-bearing-witness.html', 'snippet': 'The director of the Center on National Security at Fordham has made herself an expert on matters of torture, terrorism trials and the military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps a meal was not the ideal setting to talk about something as unappetizing as a hunger strike. But there was no way, even with lunch on the table, to avoid that grim topic with Karen J. Greenberg, who has made herself an expert on matters of torture, terrorism trials and the military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 909, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 909}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1352, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/nyregion/BREAD/BREAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {