arning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0573f9df-8371-50d1-925c-c6a0cc2decca', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0573f9df-8371-50d1-925c-c6a0cc2decca'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/arts/television/whats-on-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9\xa0P.M. (HBO) INSIDE OUT: THE PEOPLE’S ART PROJECT The French Street artist known by the initials J R and by his ubiquitous sunglasses and fedora, above, built his reputation on billboard-size photographic portraits of everyday people in urban settings, starting with his friends as they illegally painted their names on the Métro and on roofs in Paris. In 2011 he won a TED Prize, given annually to a person with a bold vision to prompt global change. The result was the Inside Out Project, which challenged residents of often perilous and politically unstable environments to generate their own photographic messages — no logos or hate messages allowed — and send them to him. J R then printed giant images of the photographs and returned them to their creators, to be pasted up “in a place with meaning.” This documentary, directed by Alastair Siddons, follows J R as he travels the world to motivate communities to define their most important causes. More than 130,000 people have pasted messages in some 10,000 places in 100 countries, including Brazil, Tunisia, Pakistan, Mexico, South Africa, Thailand, France and the United States. The exhibitions are documented at insideoutproject.net.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T04:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Ar