': None, 'lastname': 'Mack-Askew', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2aa2e9-b039-5c68-814f-78a1525fcd53', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2aa2e9-b039-5c68-814f-78a1525fcd53'}, {'abstract': 'Seriousness, not sadism.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/macroeconomic-machismo/', 'snippet': 'Seriousness, not sadism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Atrios, weighing in on the Kinsley Kontroversy, suggests that the evident urge to make Someone Suffer — Someone Else, of course — reflects sadism. But I don’t think that’s right. Lack of compassion, sure; an inability to imagine what it must be like for someone less fortunate than oneself and one’s friends, definitely. But I think that the linked Scott Lemieux post, which equates the austerian fixation on stagflation with the neocon fixation on Munich, is much closer to the mark.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Macroeconomic Machismo', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T14:18:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/186c2805-2d88-5c22-838f-5e78516d8c11', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/186c2805-2d88-5c22-838f-5e78516d8c11'}, {'abstract': 'Resisting the government during an investigation can be difficult for companies, but there can be good strategic reasons for making prosecutors work harder to build a case.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/the-case-for-not-cooperating-with-prosecutors/', 'snippet': 'Resisting the government during an investigation can be difficult for companies, but there can be good strategic reasons for making prosecutors work harder to build a case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is something rarely heard from a firm that is the focus of a government investigation: “Our cooperation is no longer unconditional.” With that cryptic phrase in a letter to its investors, SAC Capital Advisors signaled that it had entered a new phase in the insider trading investigation involving the firm and might be willing to fight efforts to gather evidence of violations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case for Not Cooperating With Prosecutors', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T14:21:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa98afe8-2a72-5940-9399-75f1851f3dac', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa98afe8-2a72-5940-9399-75f1851f3dac'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/what-were-reading-696/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Wall Street Journal: Barbecue is such a sacred cow of American cuisine that compelling new approaches have been slow in coming. Josh Ozersky touches base with a few pit masters who have decided that it’s time to innovate. — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T14:24:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62e527c0-999c-5b86-9871-a062cccaf035', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62e527c0-999c-5b86-9871-a062cccaf035'}, {'abstract': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/weekend-gun-report-may-17-19-2013/', 'snippet': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benton, Ill. Greenwich Village, N.Y. Prattville, Ala. Statesville, N.C. Houston, Tex. Beech Island, S.C. Saginaw, Mich. Phoenix, Ariz. North Philadelphia, Pa. Rochester, N.Y. West Palm Beach, Fla. New Haven, Conn. Banning, Calif. Albuquerque, N.M.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Gun Report: May 17-19, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AKRON (OHIO)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALBUQUERQUE (NM)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Antioch (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bedford-Stuyvesant (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'