Jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/arts/mm/mm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/20/arts/mm/mm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/20/arts/mm/mm-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mad Men’ Recap: Dr. Feelgood Arrives', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Matthew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T16:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sloane Crosley and Logan Hill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sloane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crosley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Logan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/922b7c36-2e75-51f3-9bb0-c6889fc72180', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/922b7c36-2e75-51f3-9bb0-c6889fc72180'}, {'abstract': 'The latest edition of the International Contemporary Furniture Fair in New York is awash in high-design products aimed at gentlemanly leisure pursuits.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/all-the-rage-toys-for-boys-at-icff/', 'snippet': 'The latest edition of the International Contemporary Furniture Fair in New York is awash in high-design products aimed at gentlemanly leisure pursuits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 25th installment of the International Contemporary Furniture Fair, the massive design trade show that runs through tomorrow at the Javits Convention Center in Manhattan, is a playground for bachelors (or would-be bachelors) with big pocketbooks. Its booths are awash in upscale, masculine, nostalgic furniture and accessories — less Sharper Image, more Lord Grantham’s study in “Downton Abbey” — with provenances ranging from Pennsylvania to Paris, Los Angeles to London, northern Italy to the Brooklyn Navy Yard. Scattered everywhere are accoutrements for esoteric gentlemanly pursuits like falconry, backgammon, whittling and spinning tops in leather, horsehair, Donegal tweed and hand-turned bronze. There’s a monolithic pool table made of concrete, gramophone-style speakers in steel-cast aluminum and white Baltic birch, even hand tools mounted and displayed like artwork in a frame. Low-slung, campaign-style chairs look perfect for poker night, leather-swathed folding chairs would be ideal for watching a rugby match from the sidelines, and the wingback chair seems made for kicking back with P.G. Wodehouse and a Scotch before bedtime. Even the vitrine — the traditional glass case for displaying specimens — is making a comeback, rendered in all shapes and finishes and with everything from cathode-ray tubes to shirt collars inside.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Rage | Toys for Boys at ICFF', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All The Rage: Goods for Sports', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T16:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Perry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50ed6604-0391-53ad-bd13-c76376939f2d', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50ed6604-0391-53ad-bd13-c76376939f2d'}, {'abstract': 'The recalled cars are the 2005-8 Maserati Quattroporte and GranTurismo and the 2008 Alfa Romeo 8C.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/20/maserati-to-recall-7400-cars-for-suspension-problem/', 'snippet': 'The recalled cars are the 2005-8 Maserati Quattroporte and GranTurismo and the 2008 Alfa Romeo 8C.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maserati will recall about 7,400 vehicles because a rear suspension failure can cause the driver to lose control, the automaker has informed the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration in a report posted over the weekend on the agency’s Web site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maserati to Recall 7,400 Cars for Suspension Problem', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alfa Romeo Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Maserati SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-20T16:31:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material':