: 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83404118-5b76-5d3e-9589-98ca52ff04b1', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83404118-5b76-5d3e-9589-98ca52ff04b1'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the best synonym for “cri de coeur”?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/21/test-yourself-english-may-21-2013/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the best synonym for “cri de coeur”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the article “Seeking to Engage Citizens, Athens Turns to New York for Advice.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, May 21, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T08:20:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1478ad5a-64db-552f-b4a8-c70000124de1', 'word_count': 43, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1478ad5a-64db-552f-b4a8-c70000124de1'}, {'abstract': 'What is a “supercell” storm? How many tornadoes touched down in four states this weekend?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/21/massive-tornado-in-oklahom/', 'snippet': 'What is a “supercell” storm? How many tornadoes touched down in four states this weekend?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A video of the tornado captured from nearby Newcastle as it approached Moore, uploaded to Youtube by Michael Welch. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massive Tornado in Oklahoma Kills Dozens', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T08:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten and Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cc67036-98da-5399-8896-03ab474f9f64', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cc67036-98da-5399-8896-03ab474f9f64'}, {'abstract': 'Psychologists are beginning to unravel the mystery behind this brand of American political paranoia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/magazine/why-rational-people-buy-into-conspiracy-theories.html', 'snippet': 'Psychologists are beginning to unravel the mystery behind this brand of American political paranoia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the days following the bombings at the Boston Marathon, speculation online regarding the identity and motive of the unknown perpetrator or perpetrators was rampant. And once the Tsarnaev brothers were identified and the manhunt came to a close, the speculation didn’t cease. It took a new form. A sampling: Maybe the brothers Tsarnaev were just patsies, fall guys set up to take the heat for a mysterious Saudi with high-level connections; or maybe they were innocent, but instead of the Saudis, the actual bomber had acted on behalf of a rogue branch of our own government; or what if the Tsarnaevs were behind the attacks, but were secretly working for a larger organization?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26eureka/26eureka-arti