st, The (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Koerth-Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koerth-Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dd9df7b-bf7f-5f09-9c3a-745a68d93b3b', 'word_count': 1311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dd9df7b-bf7f-5f09-9c3a-745a68d93b3b'}, {'abstract': 'Hélène Binet’s architectural photos pit light against dark to evoke depth. From her days photographing dancers, she has plumbed the tension in seemingly empty spaces.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/21/dancing-in-the-dark-the-architectural-photography-of-helene-binet/', 'snippet': 'Hélène Binet’s architectural photos pit light against dark to evoke depth. From her days photographing dancers, she has plumbed the tension in seemingly empty spaces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the second and third dimensions were battling each other, the Swiss photographer Hélène Binet would be something of the cunning go-between. Well known for photographing architecture, she has a knack for giving depth to the flat plane of a 2-D photograph.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dancing in the Dark: The Architectural Photography of Hélène Binet', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T09:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}]