tive Tornado', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T16:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/4a68b4e3-d286-5236-8a96-28e53783c990', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/4a68b4e3-d286-5236-8a96-28e53783c990'}, {'abstract': 'Senator James Inhofe, who voted against Sandy aid, suggested he would support relief for Oklahoma because that will be “totally different.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/21/hurricane-sandy-and-the-oklahoma-tornado/', 'snippet': 'Senator James Inhofe, who voted against Sandy aid, suggested he would support relief for Oklahoma because that will be “totally different.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Senator James Inhofe of Oklahoma voted against the Hurricane Sandy relief package, arguing at the time that the bill was a “slush fund.” But on MSNBC this morning he suggested that he would support disaster aid for Oklahoma, because that process will be “totally different.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hurricane Sandy and the Oklahoma Tornado', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-21T16:55:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier