tname': 'Feige', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f3ac45e2-03d5-5faa-9d7a-15f607209ad4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f3ac45e2-03d5-5faa-9d7a-15f607209ad4'}, {'abstract': 'A small community known as Miracle Village, surrounded by sugar cane fields in South Florida, has become a refuge for more than 100 registered sex offenders who seek to rebuild their lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/opinion/sex-offender-village.html', 'snippet': 'A small community known as Miracle Village, surrounded by sugar cane fields in South Florida, has become a refuge for more than 100 registered sex offenders who seek to rebuild their lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For this Op-Doc video, we visited a small community in Florida known as “Miracle Village,” where more than 100 registered sex offenders have settled since 2009. Surrounded by sugar cane fields, the community has become a rare refuge for them as they try to rebuild their lives in one of the only communities that will have them: stringent residency requirements make it almost impossible for them to live anywhere else. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sex Offender Village’', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Sex Offender Village’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ex-Convicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T01:11:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa F. Jackson and David Feige', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Jackson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feige', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f9a3230-06ca-5325-a14e-437b9e0aeedb', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f9a3230-06ca-5325-a14e-437b9e0aeedb'}, {'abstract': 'While federal firefighters explore the use of drones to map a wildfire’s size and speed, safety requirements of the F.A.A. is holding up testing and implementation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/us/faas-concerns-hold-up-use-of-wildfire-drones.html', 'snippet': 'While federal firefighters explore the use of drones to map a wildfire’s size and speed, safety requirements of the F.A.A. is holding up testing and implementation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — As wildfire season begins in Western landscapes that were covered in smoky haze for weeks at a time last summer, the federal government’s firefighters are exploring the use of small remote-controlled drones with infrared cameras that could map a fire’s size and speed, and identify hot spots, a particular danger. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/22/us/minidrone/minidrone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/22/us/minidrone/minidrone-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/22/us/minidrone/minidrone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ