None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Kings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Jose Sharks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T04:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b450bf11-3793-5cbb-8a93-ad464b5fc68b', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b450bf11-3793-5cbb-8a93-ad464b5fc68b'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '7 A.M. (USA) DUPLICITY (2009) She — Julia Roberts as the long-legged Claire, standing in a flowery dress in a garden, looking bored — is C.I.A. He — Clive Owen (above with Ms. Roberts) as Ray, on whom a suit becomes a brazenly sexy uniform — is MI6. Five years after meeting over frozen drinks at a barbecue and ending up in bed, they decide to abandon their respective governments and go to work on opposite sides of the beauty products industry, with puffed-up executives portrayed by Paul Giamatti and Tom Wilkinson. “It’s a caper movie, a love story,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times about this film, written and directed by Tony Gilroy, “an extra-dry corporate satire. However you describe it, ‘Duplicity’ is superior entertainment, the most elegantly pleasurable movie of its kind to come around in a very long time.” At 9:30 in “Notting Hill” (1999) he (Hugh Grant this time) is a bashful London bookstore owner who’s not quite sure he’s ready to reopen his wounded heart for business. She (Ms. Roberts again) is a Hollywood diva — but also, as she says, “just a girl, standing in front of a boy, asking him to love her.” You know the rest. “The movie has lots of glossy charm,” Janet Maslin wrote in The Times, “even if Ms. Roberts and Mr. Grant seem less like lovers than members of a support group for the desperately attractive.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b6a78d3-5738-57d3-b361-cb861269d479', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b6a78d3-5738-57d3-b361-cb861269d479'}, {'abstract': 'Rising bond yields left the central bank with less room to maneuver as it injects money into the economy, analysts said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/business/global/japan-keeps-monetary-policy-steady.html', 'snippet': 'Rising bond yields left the central bank with less room to maneuver as it injects money into the economy, analysts said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — The Bank of Japan stood pat on monetary policy Wednesday amid market anxiety over volatility in bond markets, which has threatened to undermine the country’s battle to end deflation and stimulate growth in the giant economy.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/business/Yen/Yen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'x