m the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lifehacker: I’ve always noticed that some chefs cut their lemons lengthwise to squeeze the juice instead of through the Equator as do most home cooks, but never thought about why. Turns out there’s a good reason: you get more juice. — Melissa Clark', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T14:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/211bdadf-5e39-5d87-a3c5-3dc214e367d1', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/211bdadf-5e39-5d87-a3c5-3dc214e367d1'}, {'abstract': 'Aston Martin’s new concept roadster is built to reflect the company’s past while looking to its future.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/aston-martin-shows-the-cc100-in-germany/', 'snippet': 'Aston Martin’s new concept roadster is built to reflect the company’s past while looking to its future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many 100th birthday activities Aston Martin had going on during last weekend’s Nürburgring 24-hour race, almost lost in all the events was the introduction of the CC100 Speedster Concept.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aston Martin Shows the CC100 in Germany', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moss, Stirling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aston Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T14:55:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc372b7f-6931-5735-8674-a541dc4a4f34', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc372b7f-6931-5735-8674-a541dc4a4f34'}, {'abstract': 'A deeper look at tornadoes in a changing climate.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/seeking-clarity-on-terrible-tornadoes-in-a-changing-climate/', 'snippet': 'A deeper look at tornadoes in a changing climate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I explained earlier this week, questions related to any impact of human-driven global warming on tornadoes, while important, have almost no bearing on the challenge of reducing human vulnerability to these killer storms. The focus on the ground in Oklahoma, of course, will for years to come be on recovery and rebuilding — hopefully with more attention across the region to developing policies and practices that cut losses the next time. (With this in mind, please read John Schwartz’s great feature, “Why No Safe Room to Run To? Cost and Plains Culture.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeking Clarity on Terrible Tornadoes in a Changing Climate', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T14:58:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9084a76-d2cf-5b75-9975-49b642fc8673', 'word_count': 2994, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9084a76-d2cf-5b75-9975-49b642fc8673'}, {'abstract': 'The Chicago O’Hare International Airport will be the first airport in the United States to implement Automated Passport Control, a new technology designed to expedite customs clearance.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/speeding-customs/', 'snippet': 'The Chicago O’Hare International Airport will be the first airport in the United States to implement Automated Passport Control, a new technology designed to expedite customs clearance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chicago O’Hare International Airport will be the first airport in the United States to implement Automated Passport Control, a new technology designed to expedite customs clearance, the Chicago Department of Aviation announced recently. The program is expected to help travelers with American passports move more quickly through the United States border clearance process by allowing them to enter passport and other information at self-service kiosks instead of using paper declaration cards. The touch-screen technology, developed by the Vancouver Airport Authority and United States Customs and Border Protection, is already in use at Vancouver International Airport. Thirty-two kiosks are expected to be installed at O’Hare by July 1.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Speeding Customs', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Airports: Speeding Customs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Passports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T15:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7395eb44-3542-598a-9404-d68307c22ef9', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7395eb44-3542-598a-9404-d68307c22ef9'}, {'abstract': 'Building Blocks: A new exhibition, “Little Syria, N.Y.: An Immigrant Community’s Life and Legacy,” which runs through May 27, shows that Arab-Americans were once a vibrant presence downtown.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/a-brief-encore-for-little-syria-sights-sounds-and-even-smells/', 'snippet': 'Building Blocks: A new exhibition, “Little Syria, N.Y.: An Immigrant Community’s Life and Legacy,” which runs through May 27, shows that Arab-Americans were once a vibrant presence downtown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Little Syria hasn’t been so Syrian in a long while.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/17/blogs/20130516LittleSyriaEmbed3/20130516LittleSyriaEmbed3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/17/blogs/20130516LittleSyriaEmbed3/20130516LittleSyriaEmbed3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None