al)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T15:10:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Gregor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gregor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a861402f-00a8-508f-afbd-b02a1c5dd4dc', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a861402f-00a8-508f-afbd-b02a1c5dd4dc'}, {'abstract': 'Cannes audiences aren’t very receptive to either the latest collaboration between Nicolas Winding Refn and Ryan Gosling or Takashi Miike’s new cop movie.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/cannes-film-festival-miikes-honest-cop-winding-refns-wallpaper/', 'snippet': 'Cannes audiences aren’t very receptive to either the latest collaboration between Nicolas Winding Refn and Ryan Gosling or Takashi Miike’s new cop movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNES, France — The laughter started again when the soulful thug adrift in “Only God Forgives” slid a hand in his dead mother’s eviscerated belly, seemingly going for her womb. The mother, played by Kristin Scott Thomas with long, bleached-blond hair and her usual hauteur, has come to Bangkok because the eldest rancid fruit of said womb, Billy, has been murdered. Mom demands that her youngest, Julian (Ryan Gosling), do something. It’s complicated, he says – but alas, it really isn’t, despite the nods to Macbeth or Hamlet, Michael Mann or William Friedkin and whatever – explaining that Billy raped and killed a 16-year-old girl. “I’m sure,” Mom replies, “he must have had his reasons.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/arts/takashi/takashi-art