7b329-f1cb-5655-8c3e-5b47e0ac52d5'}, {'abstract': 'American air carriers took in more than $6 billion in fees from passengers in 2012, an increase of 3.8 percent for baggage and 7.3 for reservation changes over 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/22/fees-take-on-new-altitude/', 'snippet': 'American air carriers took in more than $6 billion in fees from passengers in 2012, an increase of 3.8 percent for baggage and 7.3 for reservation changes over 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American air carriers took in more than $6 billion in fees from passengers in 2012, an increase of 3.8 percent for baggage and 7.3 for reservation changes over 2011, according to the Bureau of Transportation Statistics. At the same time, passenger volume increased only slightly, 0.8 percent from 2011 to 2012. Delta Air Lines (above), which carried more passengers than any other domestic airline in 2012, collected the most money for both baggage and ticket-change fees. Spirit Airlines, in 14th place for passenger traffic in 2012, was No. 5 for baggage fees and No. 8 for change fees.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fees Take on New Altitude', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Airlines: Fees Take on New Altitude', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-22T17:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material