, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36491d02-13f5-5a89-95bf-6b3e2c1eb045', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36491d02-13f5-5a89-95bf-6b3e2c1eb045'}, {'abstract': 'Perhaps I’ve succeeded not despite, but because of, my disability.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/opinion/defining-my-own-dyslexia.html', 'snippet': 'Perhaps I’ve succeeded not despite, but because of, my disability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — I STARTED cataloging insults in the second grade. Notable put-downs heard outside my special-ed classroom included “dimwinky,” “retardochuckles” and “the meat in the sandwich of stupid.” The last of which, if you think about it, is a seriously impressive use of metaphor for a 7-year-old. I learned all the jokes about dyslexia, and told them to better effect than anyone else. Making fun of myself was my best defense. The other choices — hiding from my diagnosis or accepting myself as limited — didn’t appeal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/opinion/0523OPEDisland/0523OPEDisland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/23/opinion/0523OPEDisland/0523OPEDisland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/opinion/0523OPEDisland/0523OPEDisland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/opinion/0523OPEDisland/0523OPEDisland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1291, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capti