': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f3d72c9-0238-5c99-a779-3d551b59923c', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f3d72c9-0238-5c99-a779-3d551b59923c'}, {'abstract': 'After a serious setback, the former dictator’s case must be pursued to a conclusion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/opinion/justice-interrupted-in-guatemala.html', 'snippet': 'After a serious setback, the former dictator’s case must be pursued to a conclusion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Securing justice for victims of the brutal civil war in Guatemala that lasted from 1960 to 1996 was always going to be tough. But the ruling by the country’s highest court on Monday to overturn the genocide conviction of the former dictator, Gen. Efraín Ríos Montt, is a serious setback in the effort to demand accountability for those terrifying years and move the country toward reconciliation.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Interrupted in Guatemala', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justice Interrupted in Guatemala', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rios Montt, Efrain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guatemala', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T01:21:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d450eeea-eb2e-5a89-b0a7-0b11efbc69dd', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d450eeea-eb2e-5a89-b0a7-0b11efbc69dd'}, {'abstract': 'A measles outbreak in Britain would have never occurred if parents had respected the proven safety record of a vaccine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/opinion/the-aftermath-of-measles-vaccine-scare-in-britain.html', 'snippet': 'A measles outbreak in Britain would have never occurred if parents had respected the proven safety record of a vaccine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Britain is experiencing serious outbreaks of measles that look to be a delayed consequence of a failure to vaccinate infants and young children more than a decade ago. A prime cause of that failure was ill-founded fears among parents that a widely used vaccine to combat measles, mumps and rubella might cause autism. Because they shunned the vaccine, their children, now in their teens, are suffering the consequences.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aftermath of an Unfounded Vaccine Scare', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aftermath of an Unfounded Vaccine Scare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wakefield, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Measles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T01:21:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d712f313-cc36-5663-a118-8ed95750ff58', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d712f313-cc36-5663-a118-8ed95750ff58'}, {'abstract': 'Lost in the focus on the State Department are pressing questions about the C.I.A.’s role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/opinion/the-cias-part-in-benghazi.html', 'snippet': 'Lost in the focus on the State Department are pressing questions about the C.I.A.’s role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout months of Republican “investigation” into the tragedy in Benghazi, Libya, on Sept. 11 last year, the Central Intelligence Agency has escaped the scrutiny and partisan bashing aimed at the State Department and the White House. But we now know that the C.I.A., and not the State Department or the White House, originated the talking points that Republicans (wrongly) insisted were proof of a scandal. It was more central to the American presence in Benghazi than the State Department, and more responsible for security there.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The C.I.A.’s Part in Benghazi', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The C.I.A.’s Part in Benghazi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petraeus, David H', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T01:23:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/43ebbc24-8d21-5c28-a754-761a7c84b6b6', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43ebbc24-8d21-5c28-a754-761a7c84b6b6'}, {'abstract': 'Despite overwhelming evidence to the contrary presented by a Senate panel, Apple denies avoiding taxes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/opinion/nocera-here-comes-the-sun.html', 'snippet': 'Despite overwhelming evidence to the contrary presented by a Senate panel, Apple denies avoiding taxes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many things Tim Cook apparently learned at the knee of Steve Jobs, during his long tenure as Apple’s No. 2, was how to create a “reality distortion field.” Or so it would appear after watching Cook, now Apple’s chief executive, testify on Tuesday at a Senate hearing on the company’s tax avoidance schemes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-c