entury, Moore, Okla., has taken a battering in Tornado Alley. Despite the continual destruction, people have stayed put and rebuilt, while others have moved in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOORE, Okla. — Along a nearly 20-mile path of caved-in buildings, upside-down cars and muddy rubble, there was a different sort of stunning sight on Wednesday — a homeowner, broom in hand, sweeping the driveway, trying to make at least one patch of land here the way it used to be.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1339, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/us/Moore/Moore-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Cross Hairs of Tornadoes, a Town’s Residents Stay Put', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Cross Hairs of Tornadoes, A Town’s Residents Stay Loyal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fernandez, Manny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moore (Okla)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T01:42:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/838f4314-87af-5859-8bb9-5e967c023706', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/838f4314-87af-5859-8bb9-5e967c023706'}, {'abstract': '“Peter and the Starcatcher,” a prequel to the classic Peter Pan story, and “Murder Ballad,” about a dangerous love triangle, won’t make a natural double feature, but they are siblings of sorts under their skins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/theater/peter-and-the-starcatcher-and-murder-ballad.html', 'snippet': '“Peter and the Starcatcher,” a prequel to the classic Peter Pan story, and “Murder Ballad,” about a dangerous love triangle, won’t make a natural double feature, but they are siblings of sorts under their skins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City safety codes do not permit the nightly building of campfires at either New World Stages or the Union Square Theater. Yet I can’t think of two better places to indulge in the venerable summer tradition of listening in the dark to tales told before bedtime, the kind that sate the spirit even as they rouse curiosity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None,