ter the brutal killing of an off-duty soldier on a London street echoed many of the questions raised after the Boston marathon bombing just five weeks before. Were the suspects “lone wolves” or part of a wider conspiracy? Were they radicalized Islamists or disaffected locals? Were they motivated by events overseas or mouthing the rhetoric of someone else’s fight? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1511, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/23/blogs/23rdv-woolwich/23rdv-woolwich-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions in London Killing Echo Boston Bombing', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Richard C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Dzhokhar A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Tamerlan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f29790f-50c1-5ea7-8d23-3c43f31566f9', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f29790f-50c1-5ea7-8d23-3c43f31566f9'}, {'abstract': 'The composer David Malloy is a one-man band, almost — with at least four duties, including a starring role — in “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/theater/dave-malloy-on-his-roles-in-natasha-pierre-at-kazino.html', 'snippet': 'The composer David Malloy is a one-man band, almost — with at least four duties, including a starring role — in “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s no question that the composer Dave Malloy’s “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812” breaks many of the rules that have accrued around American musical theater, from its mostly unrhymed lyrics and sung-through libretto, adapted from a small portion of Leo Tolstoy’s “War and Peace,” to the eclectic blend of classical and electro-pop sounds. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26MALLOY1_SPAN/26MALLOY1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Composer Wears Many Hats', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Composer Wears Many Hats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malloy, Dave', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:06:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Weinert-Kendt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weinert-Kendt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93a7aed6-1a39-567d-b5ad-dcdece2ed73d', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93a7aed6-1a39-567d-b5ad-dcdece2ed73d'}, {'abstract': 'The Pacers’ Frank Vogel left himself open to questions of overcoaching by sitting his best big man at the end of overtime in Game 1 against the Heat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/sports/basketball/nba-playoffs-why-take-pacers-roy-hibbert-out.html', 'snippet': 'The Pacers’ Frank Vogel left himself open to questions of overcoaching by sitting his best big man at the end of overtime in Game 1 against the Heat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the looks of the N.B.A. playoff schedule, it appeared the league’s first instinct was to pencil in the Miami Heat for the N.B.A. finals. The first two games of the Western Conference finals came and went before the Heat players even had to dig their sneakers out of the closet. But perhaps realizing that the Spurs-Grizzlies series was going to alienate fans who cling to the idea that basketball is fun and involves scoring a lot of baskets, or realizing that fans had paid money for tickets to the foregone conclusion that is the Heat-Pacers series (hey, people go to movies when they know the ending, too), well, the N.B.A. relented and had an actual game in the Eastern Conference finals Wednesday night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'su