 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Urlacher, Brian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/374628dc-7bfd-5437-9c15-09dc43001918', 'word_count': 739, 'uri': 'nyt://article/374628dc-7bfd-5437-9c15-09dc43001918'}, {'abstract': 'Try telling my daughter that she should keep her disorders a secret because some jerk might Google her some day. She’ll give you exactly the eye roll you deserve.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/why-i-write-about-my-daughters-o-c-d/', 'snippet': 'Try telling my daughter that she should keep her disorders a secret because some jerk might Google her some day. She’ll give you exactly the eye roll you deserve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, I published an essay in the Times’s “Opinionator” section: “Two-Way Mirror: Facing a Daughter’s O.C.D.,” about my daughter Lucy’s treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder and Tourette Syndrome, as well as my own history of mental illness. By doing so, I became what Phoebe Maltz Bovy, a blogger for The Atlantic, calls a “parental oversharer,” a writer “mining [her] children’s lives for material.” I disagree with Ms. Bovy’s diagnosis, but she raises valid questions. Should parents write publicly about the lives of their children? What, if anything, should be off-limits? How much is too much?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why I Write About My Daughter’s O.C.D.', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Tourette's Syndrome", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:11:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beth Boyle Machlan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': 'Boyle', 'lastname': 'Machlan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/312d2892-c029-59a6-b707-b21c815ff619', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/312d2892-c029-59a6-b707-b21c815ff619'}, {'abstract': 'Foreign graduate students at U.C. Berkeley reflect on how immigration reform could make it easier for highly skilled workers to stay in the United States. Or encourage them to return home.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/the-future-of-high-tech-immigrants-to-the-u-s/', 'snippet': 'Foreign graduate students at U.C. Berkeley reflect on how immigration reform could make it easier for highly skilled workers to stay in the United States. Or encourage them to return home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times’s Sean Patrick Farrell talks to foreign graduate students studying technology at the University of California, Berkeley, about the jobs they will be starting after they graduate and their lives in the United States as immigration reform looks to be gaining momentum. One Chinese student tells Sean that if she cannot get visas to stay in the U.S. — currently required each year — then she will go home to Shanghai and probably start her own business there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Future of High-Tech Immigrants to the U.S.', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:15:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dfd9304-e182-54a9-a4d1-940a7b17670e', 'word_count': 79, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dfd9304-e182-54a9-a4d1-940a7b17670e'}, {'abstract': 'The North Korean envoy, Vice Marshal Choe Ryong-hae, said the North would “accept the proposal” by China, its biggest benefactor, with which it has strained relations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/world/asia/envoy-says-north-korea-open-to-dialogue.html', 'snippet': 'The North Korean envoy, Vice Marshal Choe Ryong-hae, said the North would “accept the proposal” by China, its biggest benefactor, with which it has strained relations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A North Korean envoy visiting China said his country would “accept the proposal” by Chinese officials to open up dialogue, the state-run China News Service reported Thursday, a possible signal that the North would agree to talks on its nuclear program.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea Envoy Agrees to Dialogue, China Says, in Possible Sign for Nuclear Talks ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea Envoy Agrees to Dialogue, China Says, in Possible Sign for Nuclear Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perlez, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choe Ryong-hae', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu Yunshan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:24:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2290122-d364-5d84-9cf9-f2793de6131a', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2290122-d364-5d84-9cf9-f2793de6131a'}, {'abstract': 'In an unusual twist, the photographer becomes the model for a designer’s latest collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/objects-rachel-comeys-sculptural-swimwear-through-a-photographers-lens/', 'snippet': 'In an unusual twist, the photographer becomes the model for a designer’s latest collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Objects | Rachel Comey’s Sculptural Swimwear Through a Photographer’s Lens', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Objects: No Eye Contact, Please', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Comey, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Felsenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Felsenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8b07db8-b74d-5005-b986-7bf9dae1760d', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8b07db8-b74d-5005-b986-7bf9dae1760d'}, {'abstract': 'Two officers from Illinois face a deadly challenge in Mississippi.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/the-last-15-feet-at-vicksburg/', 'snippet': 'Two officers from Illinois face a deadly challenge in Mississippi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gen. Thomas E.G. Ransom woke early on May 22, 1863. The Union brigade commander and his senior officers had spent part of the previous night reconnoitering the formidable enemy defenses that protected Vicksburg, Miss. Now, as the morning sun burned bright and hot, the general and his aides went in search of a vantage point to continue their observations. They soon found a spot near the camp of one of the regiments in Ransom’s Brigade, the 72nd Illinois Infantry, and struck up a conversation with its officers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Last 15 Feet at Vicksburg', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vicksburg (Miss)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T15:42:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald S. Coddington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Coddington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8723803e-b533-54c8-8346-d7fe11e7da2c', 'word_count': 1865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8723803e-b533-54c8-8346-d7fe11e7da2c'}, {'abstract': 'A nonprofit group representing scientists dings officials at both ends of the political spectrum for global warming distortions.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/science-group-criticizes-politicians-for-global-warming-distortions/', 'snippet': 'A nonprofit group representing scientists dings officials at both ends of the political spectrum for global warming distortions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s great to see the Union of Concerned Scientists offering nonpartisan criticism of elected officials for distorting — in both directions — what’s known about the role of human-driven global warming on several fronts, from tornado ferocity to hurricane losses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Science Group Criticizes Politicians for Global Warming Distortions', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tornadoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank':