', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T18:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee60acd6-2c04-54fa-8c58-d1c51e17608d', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee60acd6-2c04-54fa-8c58-d1c51e17608d'}, {'abstract': 'Drivers in the Lucas Oil Offroad Racing Series regularly fling trucks around a dirt track. This is what happens when they give one to a journalist.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/taking-a-spin-around-a-dirt-track-in-a-pro-lite-unlimited-racing-truck/', 'snippet': 'Drivers in the Lucas Oil Offroad Racing Series regularly fling trucks around a dirt track. This is what happens when they give one to a journalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAKE ELSINORE, Calif. – Casey Currie drummed in one message last Friday during our laps around the track at Lake Elsinore Motorsports Park: “Slow! Slow down! Slow into the turn!” Currie, 29, who has motorcycles and Baja 1000 trucks, had good reason to worry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking a Spin Around a Dirt Track in a Pro Lite Unlimited Racing Truck', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-23T18:33:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald Ahrens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahrens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e725fc8d-6ecd-539f-8726-c4289a6483b7', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e725fc8d-6ecd-539f-8726-c4289a6483b7'}, {'abstract': 'Nasser Azam’s large-scale colorful paintings turn Costata, the new SoHo steakhouse, into a gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/food-matters-steakhouse-art-that-goes-against-the-grain/', 'snippet': 'Nasser Azam’s large-scale colorful paintings turn Costata, the new SoHo steakhouse, into a gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nasser Azam, a 49-year-old London-based artist, is used to dealing in extremes. He once took an 11-day trip to Antarctica to paint large-scale abstract canvases outdoors, intentionally leaving the work on a frigid ice field overnight so it could be roughed up by the elements. Another time, Azam boarded a specially modified aircraft in Star City, Russia, and painted two triptychs honoring Francis Bacon while the plane flew in parabolas, thus enabling its artist-passenger to conduct his work in zero gravity. More recently, at London’s City Airport, Azam unveiled “Athena,” Britain’s tallest bronze sculpture. Azam’s debut in the United States, this week, is also marked by a certain degree of monumentality. His work has gone on permanent display in a steakhouse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Matters | Steakhouse Art That Goes Against the Grain', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'valu