'2013-05-24T01:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allison Arieff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arieff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abfb5fb0-d3f2-5c24-a95a-d007c431fe45', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abfb5fb0-d3f2-5c24-a95a-d007c431fe45'}, {'abstract': 'Five American men, led by Jack Sock, are trying to gain one of the 16 positions reserved for qualifiers in the French Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/23/five-americans-in-paris/', 'snippet': 'Five American men, led by Jack Sock, are trying to gain one of the 16 positions reserved for qualifiers in the French Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — On a cool and blustery afternoon, Jack Sock, 20, of Lincoln, Neb., won his second qualifying match at Roland Garros and found himself surrounded by French teenagers bearing cellphone cameras.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Americans in Paris', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T01:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN MARTIN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MARTIN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff36b6e5-e1a9-55f3-8ca6-81726dd1dd65', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff36b6e5-e1a9-55f3-8ca6-81726dd1dd65'}, {'abstract': 'Place is not meant to be eulogized. I don’t want to think that my place may have to be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/opinion/back-to-the-boardwalk-after-hurricane-sandy.html', 'snippet': 'Place is not meant to be eulogized. I don’t want to think that my place may have to be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEASIDE PARK, N.J. — LAST summer, three months before Hurricane Sandy, before the bay flooded Seaside Park and the ocean razed the boardwalk in Seaside Heights, my grandfather passed away. For six decades, he had been a central figure down the shore, the owner of Sonny’s & Rickey’s Arcade, a man generous to the scores he befriended and formidable to the hundreds he employed. I was his namesake, so the honor of writing his eulogy fell to me. So I wrote it and read it, and hoped some justice had been done to what we’d lost. Then the local police led his funeral procession along the length of the boardwalk, from Seaside Park to Seaside Heights.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/24/opinion/0524OPEDmcnaughton/0524OPEDmcnaughton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1048, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/24/opinion/0524OPEDmcnaughton/0524OPEDmcnaughton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1048}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/24/opinion/0524OPEDmcnaughton/0524OPEDmcnaughton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/24/opinion/0524OPEDmcnaughton/0524OPEDmcnaughton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/24/opinion/0524OPEDmcnaughton/0524