kicker': 'Steven Rattner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Corporate Tax Dodge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Shelters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Actavis Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T01:05:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Rattner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rattner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09f42eb4-9c73-5753-b669-268bdca793b9', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09f42eb4-9c73-5753-b669-268bdca793b9'}, {'abstract': 'American policy makers should consider a military cybercampaign to give Syrians the ability to communicate freely online.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/opinion/a-cyberattack-campaign-for-syria.html', 'snippet': 'American policy makers should consider a military cybercampaign to give Syrians the ability to communicate freely online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — LAST week Syrians lost access to the Internet for the second time in a month. While the Assad regime claims the lapses were the result of a faulty network link, the evidence suggests that they were deliberate efforts by the government to hamper the opposition’s ability to communicate inside the country and with the outside world. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cyberattack Campaign for Syria', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cyberattack Campaign for Syria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T01:05:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Finan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/eadb2dc2-7467-5224-af44-54e52845947a', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eadb2dc2-7467-5224-af44-54e52845947a'}, {'abstract': 'By voting to allow openly gay boys to join but forbidding gay leaders, the Boy Scouts send a poor message to youngsters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/opinion/scoutings-half-turn-from-bigotry.html', 'snippet': 'By voting to allow openly gay boys to join but forbidding gay leaders, the Boy Scouts send a poor message to youngsters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Boy Scouts of America voted on Thursday to discard part of its discriminatory policies about gay Americans and to allow openly gay boys to participate in its activities. The decision was made by about 1,400 delegates attending a national convention in Grapevine, Tex. It was a major step for one of the country’s oldest and most influential youth organizations that as recently as a year ago rejected any change in its membership policies. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scouting’s Move Toward Equality', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scouting’s Move Toward Equality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boy Scouts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T01:09:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdce4613-2c8b-56b9-a11f-4e247f884f2b', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdce4613-2c8b-56b9-a11f-4e247f884f2b'}, {'abstract': 'Japan appears to be turning around its economy. Could its economic experiment be the last, best hope for recovery for the rest of the world?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/opinion/krugman-japan-the-model.html', 'snippet': 'Japan appears to be turning around its economy. Could its economic experiment be the last, best hope for recovery for the rest of the world?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A generation ago, Japan was widely admired — and feared — as an economic paragon. Business best sellers put samurai warriors on their covers, promising to teach you the secrets of Japanese management; thrillers by the likes of Michael Crichton portrayed Japanese corporations as unstoppable juggernauts rapidly consolidating their domination of world markets.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'ra