lue': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greater New York Taxi Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freidman, Evgeny A', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T01:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bc6a669-8eb1-59d1-98d1-9ad261591e19', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bc6a669-8eb1-59d1-98d1-9ad261591e19'}, {'abstract': 'In the town where George Orwell wrote his first novel, “Burmese Days,” a group of locals are encouraging the authorities to restore his house and its unkempt garden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/world/asia/orwells-house-links-myanmar-to-its-burmese-days.html', 'snippet': 'In the town where George Orwell wrote his first novel, “Burmese Days,” a group of locals are encouraging the authorities to restore his house and its unkempt garden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATHA, Myanmar — George Orwell created his first novel, “Burmese Days,” a scathing portrait of the imperious attitudes of the British, from this former colonial outpost on the banks of the mighty Irrawaddy River. His brutish characters swilled too much whiskey at a whites-only club, and wilted in the vaporous heat. A train that crawled through the jungle from Mandalay provided a lifeline to the outside world.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/24/world/asia/Orwell-CITY/Orwell-CITY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': 