es His Nickname: No-Show', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tribute to Country Singer Evokes His Nickname: No-Show ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, George (1931-2013)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Demolition String Band', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T13:54:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c08b4afd-662e-570f-88c7-3bb511433cb6', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c08b4afd-662e-570f-88c7-3bb511433cb6'}, {'abstract': 'Amanda Knox tells her story of being charged with murder in Italy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/trial-and-error.html', 'snippet': 'Amanda Knox tells her story of being charged with murder in Italy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dubiously accused almost always disappoint, once their full stories are told. It is the crime that magnetizes our attention. Remove the stain of guilt, or at least of strong complicity, and what’s left? One more casualty, and casualties don’t command interest. They spread unease. And so it is with Amanda Knox — the Seattle college student accused of inducing Raffaele Sollecito, her boyfriend of one week, to join her in high jinks that led to the murder of her housemate Meredith Kercher when all three were studying in Perugia, Italy, in 2007. The two were convicted, along with Rudy Guede, a Perugino born in Ivory Coast, whose footprint and DNA matched the grisly trail in the room where Kercher’s half-naked body was found under a comforter, her throat slit. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 767}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1921, 'width': 1503, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26SUBTANENHAUSE/26SUBTANENHAUSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trial and Error', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trial and Error', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanenhaus, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knox, Amanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T13:54:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Tanenhaus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanenhaus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/edaff8be-b2c7-59e7-98c0-74612fcfdf69', 'word_count': 1565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edaff8be-b2c7-59e7-98c0-74612fcfdf69'}, {'abstract': 'California beaming.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/obamacare-will-be-a-debacle-for-republicans/', 'snippet': 'California beaming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Affordable Care Act, aka Obamacare, is a policy Rube Goldberg device — instead of doing the simple, obvious thing, which would just be to insure everyone, it basically relies on a combination of regulations and subsidies to rope, coddle, and nudge us into a rough approximation of a single-payer system. There were reasons for this, of course, mainly political: a complete displacement of the existing system would have been both too destructive of powerful interests and too radical for voters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obamacare Will Be A Debacle — For Republicans', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T13:55:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90099cce-586f-59c1-9285-41766b1f10b4', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90099cce-586f-59c1-9285-41766b1f10b4'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/what-were-reading-700/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bon Appétit: Patrick Dempsey, the actor best known for his role as McDreamy in “Grey’s Anatomy,” is now in the coffee business. He’s also a backyard locavore. “We grow vegetables and raise chickens for fresh eggs,” he says. “Seed-to-skillet is a great concept, especially if you have kids.” — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T13:58:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/963ef15d-dae1-5500-88f3-b48f5f113918', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/963ef15d-dae1-5500-88f3-b48f5f113918'}, {'abstract': 'William Dalrymple seeks contemporary lessons in Britain’s disastrous 19th-century invasion of Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/the-men-who-would-be-king.html', 'snippet': 'William Dalrymple seeks contemporary lessons in Britain’s disastrous 19th-century invasion of Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of the British invasion of Afghanistan in 1839-42 (what was later called the First Afghan War) can be briefly told. A British Army entered the country in April 1839, captured Kabul and ejected the ruler Dost Mohammad Khan. He was replaced by Shah Shuja, who had been living in exile since his overthrow by Dost Mohammad and his brother some 30 years earlier. The British expected Shah Shuja to be a more pliable king, a client of their Indian Raj, and a more reliable ally against the intrigues of the Russians. But they underestimated the resentment that their presence would arouse, and inflamed Afghan hostility by their overbearing behavior. After they failed to quell an uprising in Kabul and their envoy was murdered, they agreed to withdraw. But the retreating army of British officers, Indian soldiers and a multitude of camp followers was slaughtered almost to a man as it struggled back through the Khyber Pass in January 1842. Some months later, the British returned with an “army of retribution.” After a short stay in Kabul, and some indiscriminate killing, they withdrew once more. Dost Mohammad returned (with their blessing) to resume his place on the throne. Ever since this fiasco, an entire phalanx of writers has denounced the arrogance, folly and incompetence of the British aggressors (of which there was plenty) and has drawn a predictable lesson: those who invade Afghanistan pay a high price in treasure and blood — and also inflict one on its unlucky peoples. The latest (and most brilliant) in this long line of critics is William Dalrymple and his “Return of a King.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26DARWIN/26DARWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/26DARWIN/26DARWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 565}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/r