icle/30e42f55-f471-50b7-989b-ba0b85886232', 'word_count': 2201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e42f55-f471-50b7-989b-ba0b85886232'}, {'abstract': 'Nick Offerman, who plays Ron Swanson on “Parks and Recreation,” talks about his stage act and his new film, “The Kings of Summer.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/movies/nick-offerman-in-the-kings-of-summer.html', 'snippet': 'Nick Offerman, who plays Ron Swanson on “Parks and Recreation,” talks about his stage act and his new film, “The Kings of Summer.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Fans of Ron Swanson, the Indiana town bureaucrat played by Nick Offerman on NBC’s “Parks and Recreation,” know him for his gruff manner, love of meat and strong interest in woodworking. So they might find satisfaction in the many pleasures of Mr. Offerman’s real custom-furniture wood shop here in the Glassell Park neighborhood: the scent of fresh sawdust and perspiration; the huge slabs of walnut and oak stacked against the wall; and the sight of the bluejeaned Mr. Offerman, shaggier and more handsome than his television character, drizzling hot sauce on a bicep-size burrito.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN/26OFFERMAN2_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting at the Meat of the Matter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting at the Meat of the Matter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Offerman, Nick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Margy Rochlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Margy', 'lastname': 'Rochlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a78d80a-b8fc-5075-8be4-3b48ad1c049d', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a78d80a-b8fc-5075-8be4-3b48ad1c049d'}, {'abstract': 'He can probably finance his own movie. Does that mean he should have to?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/magazine/was-it-ethical-for-zach-braff-to-take-to-kickstarter.html', 'snippet': 'He can probably finance his own movie. Does that mean he should have to?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Was it ethical for the actor Zach Braff to ask the public to finance his movie? Consider that he has a personal wealth to easily cover the expenses himself and that the backers are not entitled to any of the profit the movie may eventually earn. Is this a fun way to get common people involved in the movie industry or another way for the 1-percenters to make the expenses “public” while keeping the profits “private”? MART JURISOO, TALLINN, ESTONIA', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailhei