’s death, I went looking for him in his old squadron.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father, Peter Rodrick, the commanding officer of VAQ-135, was killed when his EA-6B Prowler crashed in the Indian Ocean off the U.S.S. Kitty Hawk when I was 13 and he was 36. To me, Dad was a hero, but he was an apparition as well, gone 200 days of the year from when I was 6 until he died. He was such a ghost that I didn’t fully accept he was really gone for years.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26lives/mag-26Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 264, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26lives/mag-26Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 264, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26lives/mag-26Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26lives/mag-26Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/magazine/26lives/mag-26Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flying With the Black Ravens', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flying With the Black Ravens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodrick, Stephen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missiles and Missile Defense Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Rodrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rodrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e27223fc-4a5a-501c-94cd-d9f954f9f408', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e27223fc-4a5a-501c-94cd-d9f954f9f408'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Mark S. Weiner, Andrew T. Guzman, Ben Goldacre, Daniel Brook and Vishaan Chakrabarti.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/the-rule-of-the-clan-by-mark-weiner-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Mark S. Weiner, Andrew T. Guzman, Ben Goldacre, Daniel Brook and Vishaan Chakrabarti.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE RULE OF THE CLAN  What an Ancient Form of Social Organization Reveals About the Future of Individual Freedom  By Mark S. Weiner Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $27.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-PARK-SUB/0526-bks-PARK-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonfiction Chronicle: Cara Parks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldacre, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brook, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chakrabarti, Vishaan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:33:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/edb6e9c5-ac6b-5bda-b2d7-b22347cd1cf3', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edb6e9c5-ac6b-5bda-b2d7-b22347cd1cf3'}, {'abstract': 'The National Geographic Channel offers a special on Monday night called “American Heroes Fishing Challenge.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/arts/television/soldiers-recount-the-burden-of-war.html', 'snippet': 'The National Geographic Channel offers a special on Monday night called “American Heroes Fishing Challenge.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Memorial Day themes and specials will be all over television around the holiday, and the National Geographic Channel gets into the act with offerings that include a special on Monday night called “American Heroes Fishing Challenge,” in which injured veterans compete in a Martha’s Vineyard fishing derby. A more conventional tribute comes on Friday night, when the channel broadcasts “Inside World War II,” a three-hour look at that conflict.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soldiers Recount the Burden of War', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soldiers Recount The Burden of War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:35:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57f3d85e-19d9-5517-b86e-c2c4444e5a42', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57f3d85e-19d9-5517-b86e-c2c4444e5a42'}, {'abstract': 'Joss Whedon knows the way to a cultist’s heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/movies/question-time-for-a-cult-creator.html', 'snippet': 'Joss Whedon knows the way to a cultist’s heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joss Whedon knows the way to a cultist’s heart: through her television set. Mr. Whedon, the auteur behind the much-obsessed-over series “Buffy the Vampire Slayer” and its spinoff, “Angel,” not to mention the niche crowd pleasers “Dollhouse” and “Firefly,” has also made forays onto the big screen (“Marvel’s The Avengers”). On Thursday BAMcinématek hosts “An Evening With Joss Whedon,” in which Mr. Whedon will answer questions about his latest movie, “Much Ado About Nothing,” and introduce an older one, “Serenity.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Question Time for a Cult Creator', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Question Time For a Cult Creator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television Sets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51251a2a-e558-5d70-a8e6-89bcce243045', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51251a2a-e558-5d70-a8e6-89bcce243045'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph Kubera presents solo recitals less often than his stature warrants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/arts/music/plays-with-others-but-also-on-his-own.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph Kubera presents solo recitals less often than his stature warrants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who saw “HPSCHD,” the 1969 multimedia riot by John Cage and Lejaren Hiller recently recreated in New York as part of the Darmstadt Essential Repertoire series, likely lingered at some point near the stoic figure at the eye of the storm, who imperturbably played Mozart on a harpsichord surrounded by crumpled gold foil. The performer was Joseph Kubera, a venerated denizen of classical music’s outer limits for three decades and counting.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Plays With Others, But Also on His Own', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Plays With Others, But Also on His Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kubera, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:37:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/733caef1-1717-5f01-83d1-7629341c8b10', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/733caef1-1717-5f01-83d1-7629341c8b10'}, {'abstract': 'The Issue Project Room has invited Michelle Boulé to make a new work, “Wonder,” as part of its Emerging Artists Commission Program', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/arts/dance/peeling-back-the-layers.html', 'snippet': 'The Issue Project Room has invited Michelle Boulé to make a new work, “Wonder,” as part of its Emerging Artists Commission Program', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a dancer, Michelle Boulé has emerged. But as a choreographer, this enigmatic artist, best known for her grounding yet exhilarating presence in the work of Miguel Gutierrez, still falls into that category known as “emerging.” So it is that Issue Project Room, a Downtown Brooklyn arts center, has invited her to make a new work, “Wonder,” as part of its Emerging Artists Commission Program. The solo, set in the round, with sound design by Carmine Covelli and lighting by Natalie Robin, takes place at the Church of St. Luke and St. Matthew in Clinton Hill.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peeling Back The Layers', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peeling Back The Layers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:42:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62890811-0958-5a86-8820-12a376500d5c', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62890811-0958-5a86-8820-12a376500d5c'}, {'abstract': 'Some Russians say literature had more political power when it was forbidden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/bulgakovs-ghost.html', 'snippet': 'Some Russians say literature had more political power when it was forbidden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past year, dozens of activists have been arrested in Vladimir Putin’s crackdown on dissent. The blogger Aleksei A. Navalny, a prominent opposition figure who is on trial as of this writing, faces a possible sentence of 10 years. Nonprofit groups that receive financing from abroad are now required by law to identify themselves as “foreign agents.” The need for a robust literary outcry would seem as great as ever. But what has happened to Russia’s famous tradition of dissident literature — and to its readers? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 919, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 919}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 669, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1379, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Parker/0526-bks-Parker-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bulgakov’s Ghost', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bulgakov’s Ghost', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef2642c8-babb-5a67-afd9-6d07088c566c', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef2642c8-babb-5a67-afd9-6d07088c566c'}, {'abstract': '“The Civil War and American Art” is at the Met.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/arts/design/storms-of-war-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': '“The Civil War and American Art” is at the Met.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to imagine that any exhibition about the Civil War will surpass the Metropolitan Museum of Art’s “Photography and the American Civil War,” but viewers of that show will want to see “The Civil War and American Art,” also at the Met. Including about 60 paintings by artists like Frederic Edwin Church, Sanford Robinson Gifford and Winslow Homer, all made between 1852 and 1877, the show offers a broad exploration of the war’s effect on the American psyche. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Storms of War At the Met', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Storms of War At the Met', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee78b94e-2c71-595b-9e1e-c5038408a57d', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee78b94e-2c71-595b-9e1e-c5038408a57d'}, {'abstract': 'A furor has erupted over comments made by Paul Tudor Jones, a billionaire hedge fund manager and philanthropist, who was captured on video trying to explain why there is a scarcity of female traders.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/when-a-billionaire-speaks-off-the-cuff/', 'snippet': 'A furor has erupted over comments made by Paul Tudor Jones, a billionaire hedge fund manager and philanthropist, who was captured on video trying to explain why there is a scarcity of female traders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a hedge fund manager who carefully manages his public image, Paul Tudor Jones had a minor crisis on his hands.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Billionaire Speaks Off the Cuff on Motherhood', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Brokers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coles, Joanna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Paul Tudor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lebenthal, Alexandra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:44:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a08576b-493e-5b62-b823-1ba97f421b97', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a08576b-493e-5b62-b823-1ba97f421b97'}, {'abstract': 'Black Host, a new project led by the drummer and composer Gerald Cleaver, is a possible dream band.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/arts/music/at-the-corner-of-jazz-and-rock.html', 'snippet': 'Black Host, a new project led by the drummer and composer Gerald Cleaver, is a possible dream band.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Black Host, a new project led by the drummer and composer Gerald Cleaver, is a possible dream band for anyone interested in the new intersections between jazz and rock, as long as you expel preconceptions of that idea, whether the 1970s version (Mahavishnu Orchestra), the early-oughts version (young Berklee graduates playing Radiohead), or any other. The songs on “Life in the Sugar Candle Mines” (Northern Spy), the band’s first record, are less programmatic than any of that: constantly on the move, changing shape and sound and attack, structured, vibrant, crackling. They’ve got heavy-gauge free improvising from the pianist Cooper-Moore and saxophonist Darius Jones; postpunk textures from the guitarist Brandon Seabrook; and solid grooves from Mr. Cleaver and the bassist Pascal Niggenkemper. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Corner Of Jazz and Rock', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Corner Of Jazz and Rock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:44:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization'