“The Avengers,” Joss Whedon intended to take a vacation, but ended up making “Much Ado About Nothing” instead.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Honestly, if you’re not a workaholic,” Joss Whedon said recently, “this is hard to explain.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 295}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1008, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/arts/26WHEDON2/26WHEDON2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Shakespeare Saved ‘Avengers’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Shakespeare Saved ‘Avengers’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whedon, Joss', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Much Ado About Nothing (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T14:56:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d41e83fe-e1f7-553d-a50c-c81bd2e4b11c', 'word_count': 1350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d41e83fe-e1f7-553d-a50c-c81bd2e4b11c'}, {'abstract': 'Gail Godwin’s novel is populated almost exclusively by orphans of various stripes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/flora-by-gail-godwin.html', 'snippet': 'Gail Godwin’s novel is populated almost exclusively by orphans of various stripes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Orphans have long been a boon to literature. Part of their appeal is pragmatic: they’re good for plot (parents have such a pesky habit of squelching adventure). But they’re also ideal exemplars of a certain universal experience, what the writer Francis Spufford has called “the situation of abandonment.” In his memoir, “The Child That Books Built,” he says this experience “focuses a self-pity that everyone wants to feel sometimes,” and expresses “the solitude humans discover as we grow up no matter how well our kinship systems work.” In other words, we’re all orphans at one time or another, at least in our hearts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur