e': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50c2b811-ad54-55b7-8e30-006c7c5d6c09', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50c2b811-ad54-55b7-8e30-006c7c5d6c09'}, {'abstract': 'A researcher and his family watched as pairs of Irrawaddy dolphins breached and frolicked, their whimsical play in sharp contrast to the species’ grim reality.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/a-vital-river-drained-of-wildlife/', 'snippet': 'A researcher and his family watched as pairs of Irrawaddy dolphins breached and frolicked, their whimsical play in sharp contrast to the species’ grim reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeff Opperman, a senior freshwater scientist with the Nature Conservancy, is taking a once-in-a-lifetime trip down the Mekong River in Southeast Asia with his wife and two children, ages 8 and 10. Previous posts can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/21/science/21saw-dolphin2/21saw-dolphin2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Vital River, Drained of Wildlife', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dolphins and Porpoises', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mekong River', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T15:19:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Opperman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Opperman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/072f1ae9-3022-5f97-8756-441478089100', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/072f1ae9-3022-5f97-8756-441478089100'}, {'abstract': 'The poet Christian Wiman ruminates on his incurable illness and his return to Christian belief.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/my-bright-abyss-by-christian-wiman.html', 'snippet': 'The poet Christian Wiman ruminates on his incurable illness and his return to Christian belief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a daring and urgent book, written after the author learned he had a rare, incurable and unpredictable cancer. But it is not a conventional memoir of illness and treatment. Beyond informing us that he received his dire news in a “curt voice mail message,” Christian Wiman says very little about his experience of the medical world. He is after bigger game. More than any other contemporary book I know, “My Bright Abyss” reveals what it can mean to experience St. Benedict’s admonition to keep death daily before your eyes. As a poet, Wiman is more likely to quote a poet than a saint, and the many citations here — from sources as diverse as A. R. Ammons, Robert Browning, Paul Celan, George Herbert, Eugenio Montale, Osip Mandelstam, Rainer Maria Rilke, Richard Wilbur and William Wordsworth — offer a rich encounter with literature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 680}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 904, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1713, 'width': 1511, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/26/books/review/0526-bks-Norris-sub2/0526-bks-Norris-sub2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Faith Healing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Faith Healing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiman, Christian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T15:23:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c46f2d34-6dc0-5667-a479-57f83ee76b3b', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c46f2d34-6dc0-5667-a479-57f83ee76b3b'}, {'abstract': 'Limited-government egalitarianism and the case against the Senate immigration bill.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/what-conservative-reformers-dont-like-about-immigration-reform/', 'snippet': 'Limited-government egalitarianism and the case against the Senate immigration bill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a kind of companion piece to my post earlier this week on mass immigration and liberalism’s dilemmas, I thought I’d say something about a Matt Yglesias piece\xa0from a few weeks ago, in which he expressed a certain amount of bafflement at both liberals and conservatives who worry about immigration’s impact on the fortunes of low-wage-earning Americans:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Conservative Reformers Don’t Like About Immigration Reform', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T15:23:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/050b9f87-b4f6-57be-8ccf-8a4272df46d1', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/050b9f87-b4f6-57be-8ccf-8a4272df46d1'}, {'abstract': 'Lyrical prints, free of frippery and cinched at the waist, are a natural fit for the summer.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/24/magical-realism-elegant-summer-style/', 'snippet': 'Lyrical prints, free of frippery and cinched at the waist, are a natural fit for the summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lyrical prints, free of frippery and cinched at the waist, are a natural fit for the summer. Slide Show: A Poetic Aesthetic', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Magical Realism | Elegant Summer Style', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Traina, Vanessa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T15:25:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/485b2ce5-a16e-5bdd-9a90-d89951b9ad5f', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/485b2ce5-a16e-5bdd-9a90-d89951b9ad5f'}, {'abstract': 'The National Institute of Mental Health has distanced itself from the “D.S.M.,” the so-called bible of psychiatry, the fifth edition of which is published this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/books/review/battle-of-the-brain.html', 'snippet': 'The National Institute of Mental Health has distanced itself from the “D.S.M.,” the so-called bible of psychiatry, the fifth edition of which is published this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, Thomas Insel, the director of the National Institute of Mental Health, wrote a post on the institute’s Web site distancing it from the “Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders,” the so-called bible of psychiatry, whose fifth edition will be published this week. “The weakness is its lack of validity,” Insel wrote. “The D.S.M. diagnoses are based on a consensus about clusters of clinical symptoms, not any objective laboratory measure.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'c