 None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/25/opinion/0525OPEDkim/0525OPEDkim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/25/opinion/0525OPEDkim/0525OPEDkim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/25/opinion/0525OPEDkim/0525OPEDkim-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Tree Grow in the Bronx?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can a Tree Grow in the Bronx?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T23:37:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel L. Squadron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Squadron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d5a4c57-743c-5196-99c3-4b5d75f60aa6', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d5a4c57-743c-5196-99c3-4b5d75f60aa6'}, {'abstract': 'At least seven people have reported complications after being injected with a potentially contaminated medication made in a pharmacy in Tennessee, federal and state health officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/25/health/abscesses-reported-after-steroid-injections.html', 'snippet': 'At least seven people have reported complications after being injected with a potentially contaminated medication made in a pharmacy in Tennessee, federal and state health officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At least seven people have reported complications after being injected with a potentially contaminated medication made in a pharmacy in Tennessee, federal and state health officials said. The Food and Drug Administration said that it had not yet determined the nature of the illnesses, but that at least one person seemed to have a fungal infection. Last fall, fungal contamination of the same type of medication, an injectable steroid produced by a compounding pharmacy in Massachusetts, caused the deaths of more than 50 people. Officials in North Carolina, where two of the new cases were found, said that the symptoms were skin abscesses and that there had been no reports of meningitis, stroke or other life-threatening conditions. Illinois said it was investigating five cases of patients who developed abscesses after receiving the medication, which is used to treat back pain and swelling in joints, among other conditions. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Abscesses Reported After Steroid Injections', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Abscesses Reported After Steroid Injections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tavernise, Sabrina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-24T23:51:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sa