nizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-25T04:25:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa66c479-2337-55df-ae0f-739c7cb3c706', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa66c479-2337-55df-ae0f-739c7cb3c706'}, {'abstract': 'Jane Freilicher, who has an exhibition at the Tibor de Nagy Gallery in Midtown, was a star presence among her group of friends, which included several renowned poets of the New York School.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/nyregion/jane-freilicher-a-painter-amid-friends.html', 'snippet': 'Jane Freilicher, who has an exhibition at the Tibor de Nagy Gallery in Midtown, was a star presence among her group of friends, which included several renowned poets of the New York School.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1949, John Ashbery, unleashed to the world from Harvard, arrived in New York to begin his long and productive creative life in a small loft building on Third Avenue near 16th Street. Mr. Ashbery was to spend the season in the apartment of his college friend Kenneth Koch, who was visiting his parents in Cincinnati, and he was to pick up the key from a woman, Jane Freilicher, who lived one floor up. The consequence of this quotidian exchange was a deeply connected friendship now in its sixth decade, nourished by the bohemian reverie that characterized Greenwich Village for so much of the 20th century and migrated to the east end of Long Island with the arrival of Memorial Day.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlar