ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a10660ca-2335-5433-aa99-a47583a085ae', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a10660ca-2335-5433-aa99-a47583a085ae'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court should agree to hear argument on this crucial question.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/the-fight-over-recess-appointments.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court should agree to hear argument on this crucial question.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Senate is not available to confirm presidential nominees, the Constitution gives the president the power to temporarily fill government positions. Senate Republicans have gone out of their way to make themselves effectively unavailable, refusing to confirm nominees to agencies whose missions they oppose. That is why President Obama has had to make many recess appointments, including five to the National Labor Relations Board. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fight Over Recess Appointments', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fight Over Recess Appointments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d60b4ec0-f28c-569f-a2c2-d8c7769c10a6', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d60b4ec0-f28c-569f-a2c2-d8c7769c10a6'}, {'abstract': 'We should not try to play God with the planet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/geoengineering-our-last-hope-or-a-false-promise.html', 'snippet': 'We should not try to play God with the planet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANBERRA, Australia — THE concentration of carbon dioxide in the earth’s atmosphere recently surpassed 400 parts per million for the first time in three million years. If you are not frightened by this fact, then you are ignoring or denying science.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Geoengineering: Our Last Hope, or a False Promise?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Geoengineering: Our Last Hope, or a False Promise?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:09:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clive Hamilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clive', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hamilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d74d172-83f1-5501-8d4d-3a1144acc85b', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d74d172-83f1-5501-8d4d-3a1144acc85b'}, {'abstract': 'The program’s a looming non-disaster!', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/krugman-the-obamacare-shock.html', 'snippet': 'The program’s a looming non-disaster!', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Affordable Care Act, a k a Obamacare, goes fully into effect at the beginning of next year, and predictions of disaster are being heard far and wide. There will be an administrative “train wreck,” we’re told; consumers will face a terrible shock. Republicans, one hears, are already counting on the law’s troubles to give them a big electoral advantage.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Obamacare Shock', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Obamacare Shock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:10:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8f6c5e5-a80b-58ba-8632-b0a1138937d4', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8f6c5e5-a80b-58ba-8632-b0a1138937d4'}, {'abstract': 'A secret plan by Connecticut leaders to restrict information is misguided.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/public-records-on-the-newtown-shootings.html', 'snippet': 'A secret plan by Connecticut leaders to restrict information is misguided.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be hard to compound the tragedy of the Newtown school massacre, in which 20 children and six school employees died, but a misbegotten proposal aimed for the Connecticut Legislature would do just that. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Records on the Newtown Shootings', 'kicker': 'Editoral', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Public Records on the Newtown Shootings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malloy, Dannel P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:12:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca488441-7892-5be3-89bb-a7ed0728d391', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca488441-7892-5be3-89bb-a7ed0728d391'}, {'abstract': 'An examination of nonprofit applicants revealed activities that tax experts and former Internal Revenue Service officials said would provide a basis for closer review.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/us/politics/nonprofit-applicants-chafing-at-irs-tested-political-limits.html', 'snippet': 'An examination of nonprofit applicants revealed activities that tax experts and former Internal Revenue Service officials said would provide a basis for closer review.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When CVFC, a conservative veterans’ group in California, applied for tax-exempt status with the Internal Revenue Service, its biggest expenditure that year was several thousand dollars in radio ads backing a Republican candidate for Congress.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1372, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/27/us/IRS/IRS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Groups Targeted by I.R.S. Tested Rules on Politics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Groups Targeted By I.R.S. Tested Rules on Politics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service Political Profiling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Confessore, Nicholas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luo, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, J Russell', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:15:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Confessore and Michael Luo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Luo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d668479b-fb31-53fb-becc-255c4ec86cdb', 'word_count': 1570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d668479b-fb31-53fb-becc-255c4ec86cdb'}, {'abstract': 'A sharp interruption in the last flush of spring, with sober gestures, honor and humility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/the-silence-of-memorial-day.html', 'snippet': 'A sharp interruption in the last flush of spring, with sober gestures, honor and humility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you listen carefully, you can almost hear the silence at the heart of Memorial Day — the inward turn that thoughts take on a day set aside to honor the men and women who have died in the service of this country. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Silence on This Day', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silence on This Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memorial Day', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:17:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/44f1cfcc-ca49-5b13-8fa3-0929133567f1', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44f1cfcc-ca49-5b13-8fa3-0929133567f1'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s budget proposal overspends on warheads at a time when there is limited money for real needs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/opinion/throwing-money-at-nukes.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s budget proposal overspends on warheads at a time when there is limited money for real needs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States has about 180 B61 gravity nuclear bombs based in Europe. They are the detritus of the cold war, tactical weapons deployed in Belgium, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and Turkey to protect NATO allies from the once-feared Soviet advantage in conventional arms. But the cold war is long over, and no American military commander can conceive of their ever being used. Even so, President Obama has put $537 million in his 2014 budget proposal to upgrade these bombs. When all is said and done, experts say, the cost of the rebuilding program is expected to total around $10 billion — $4 billion more than an earlier projection — and yield an estimated 400 weapons, fitted with new guided tail kits so that they are more reliable and accurate than the current ones.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Throwing Money at Nukes', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Throwing Money at Nukes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T01:17:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b9d5f14-2dec-5f74-b77f-cc88c1c835f2', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b9d5f14-2dec-5f74-b77f-cc88c1c835f2'}, {'abstract': 'Controversies swirled around the first years of the National Cemeteries system.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/26/the-union-dead/', 'snippet': 'Controversies swirled around the first years of the National Cemeteries system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best-known national cemeteries – Arlington in Virginia, Golden Gate in California – mingle veterans of different wars. But in the burial grounds where space for graves ran out sooner, Civil War soldiers dominate. Fredericksburg, Glendale, Antietam, City Point: these smaller cemeteries have a sleepier air than the seemingly infinite green fields of Arlington. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Union Dead', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cemeteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense Contracts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarries', 'rank': 4, 'majo