_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. Claiborne Ray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6f05ce0-cae0-5750-aa50-cfd8a7a106be', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6f05ce0-cae0-5750-aa50-cfd8a7a106be'}, {'abstract': 'A photographer, known for cataloging the rich language of sleep, first turned the camera on himself.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/ted-spagnas-sleep-studies/', 'snippet': 'A photographer, known for cataloging the rich language of sleep, first turned the camera on himself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For sleep evangelists and connoisseurs, and even for sleep strivers, the publication this fall from Rizzoli of “Sleep” by Ted Spagna is a watershed event. Beginning in 1975, Spagna mounted time-lapse cameras in his subjects’ bedrooms, producing portraits from a “God’s-eye view” and cataloging, in full color, the rich language of sleep: tosses, turns, expressions, positions, intimacy, solitude. It was curiosity alone that led the photographer, who died in 1989, to his first sleeping subject: himself. “I was surprised to see another self that existed in sleep that I didn’t know about,” he has said. Looking at these photos, I’m reminded of John Steinbeck, who wrote, “It is a common experience that a problem difficult at night is resolved in the morning after the committee of sleep has worked on it.” Spagna, more than anyone else, took us inside the committee room.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ted Spagna’s Sleep Studies', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T16:36:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arianna Huffington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arianna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huffington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a288cac-f9aa-5384-aaa4-264497f5bb88', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a288cac-f9aa-5384-aaa4-264497f5bb88'}, {'abstract': '', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/05/28/science/28scan_martian.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '“Pop’s Song,” a sample track from the “Martian Chronicles.”', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-27T16:42:11+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/5d153ca0-ccb7-5877-a9cb-c36c91689b8a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/5d153ca0-ccb7-5877-a9cb-c36c91689b8a'}, {'abstract': 'Until a few weeks ago, there had not been any openly gay men in major North American team sports, and suddenly there are two. Sort of.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/sports/after-robbie-rogerss-debut-whats-next.html', 'snippet': 'Until a few weeks ago, there had not been any openly gay men in major North American team sports, and suddenly there are two. Sort of.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The blessing and curse of progress is that you cannot choose your heroes, no matter how long you wait for them to emerge.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/28/sports/soccer/BRANCH-2/BRANCH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 