/article/c18038a0-7016-5bfe-985d-739ab55b1619', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c18038a0-7016-5bfe-985d-739ab55b1619'}, {'abstract': 'Ray Monk’s “Robert Oppenheimer: A Life Inside the Center” aspires to be more comprehensive than earlier volumes about that American physicist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/books/robert-oppenheimer-a-life-inside-the-center-by-ray-monk.html', 'snippet': 'Ray Monk’s “Robert Oppenheimer: A Life Inside the Center” aspires to be more comprehensive than earlier volumes about that American physicist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ray Monk had begun work on his J. Robert Oppenheimer biography in 2005, when Kai Bird and Martin J. Sherwin’s “American Prometheus” was published. That book, billed as “the first full-scale biography” of Oppenheimer, the theoretical physicist, gave Mr. Monk good reason to worry. It covers scientific, historical, moral, political and personal aspects of Oppenheimer’s life, conveying his arrogance, brilliance, self-destructiveness and lady-killing charisma. What gaps remained for Mr. Monk to fill?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/28/arts/jpbook2/jpbook2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/28/arts/jpbook2/jpbook2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/28/arts/jpbook2/jpbook2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/28/arts/jpbook2/jpbook2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1580, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {