ect', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filipino-Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizenship and Naturalization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T00:54:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher M. Lapinig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f2959e1-a11b-509a-a873-0976a701e208', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f2959e1-a11b-509a-a873-0976a701e208'}, {'abstract': 'A halfhearted U.S. intervention in Syria won’t intimidate Iran’s leaders; it will embolden them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/opinion/in-syria-go-big-or-stay-home.html', 'snippet': 'A halfhearted U.S. intervention in Syria won’t intimidate Iran’s leaders; it will embolden them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — FROM liberal internationalists to hawkish conservatives, a chorus of influential voices in Washington is suggesting that American intervention in Syria would also do serious damage to Bashar al-Assad’s close ally, Iran.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Syria, Go Big or Stay Home', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Syria, Go Big or Stay Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T00:56:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Takeyh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Takeyh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4e79a24-c845-5389-bea7-0d9276350cad', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4e79a24-c845-5389-bea7-0d9276350cad'}, {'abstract': 'In Virginia, a candidate’s hateful speech is testing the Republican Party’s ability to reach mainstream voters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/opinion/a-virginia-republicans-addiction-to-zealotry.html', 'snippet': 'In Virginia, a candidate’s hateful speech is testing the Republican Party’s ability to reach mainstream voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Virginia Republicans delivered a rousing surprise at their gubernatorial nominating convention this month. They chose E. W. Jackson, an African-American minister and lawyer, as their candidate for lieutenant governor after he delivered a thunderously right-wing speech. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Addiction to Zealotry', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Addiction to Zealotry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, E W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T00:59:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d2c8ddb-c9ad-576f-b7e9-4f159849920e', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d2c8ddb-c9ad-576f-b7e9-4f159849920e'}, {'abstract': 'Letting go of one’s library is about more than just clearing shelf space.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/27/moving-on/', 'snippet': 'Letting go of one’s library is about more than just clearing shelf space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have sold my books. Not all of them, but most of them. I held on to the books I might need while putting the finishing touches on a manuscript that is now with my publisher. I also kept the books I will likely need when I begin my next project in the fall. But the books that sustained my professional life for 50 years — books by and about Milton, Spenser, Shakespeare, Skelton, Sidney, Herbert, Marvell, Herrick, Donne, Jonson, Burton, Browne, Bacon, Dryden, Hobbes — are gone (I watched them being literally wheeled out the door), and now I look around and see acres of empty white bookshelves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moving On', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T01:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastna