on in 2014. Many of the governors with the poorest job approval ratings were Republicans in Democratic-leaning and swing states.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popular Governors, and Prospects for 2016', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T16:11:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bbc6234-9f5c-5b1a-884d-7ab8cf50088d', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bbc6234-9f5c-5b1a-884d-7ab8cf50088d'}, {'abstract': 'A British company is selling a letter in which Rudyard Kipling writes that “it is extremely possible that I have helped myself promiscuously” in writing a poem in “The Second Jungle Book.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/kipling-letter-says-he-borrowed-promiscuously-for-the-jungle-book/', 'snippet': 'A British company is selling a letter in which Rudyard Kipling writes that “it is extremely possible that I have helped myself promiscuously” in writing a poem in “The Second Jungle Book.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Rudyard Kipling spun beloved characters out of creatures like a bear, a tiger, a snake and a panther for his classic story collection “The Jungle Book,” he may have been a bit of a vulture himself and made monkey’s uncles of his readers — at least according to a letter that suggests he made use of outside sources for a portion of the work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kipling Letter Says He Borrowed ‘Promiscuously’ for ‘The Jungle Book’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kipling, Rudyard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-28T16:22:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42bfca73-008b-5ab5-8b14-ef6d20f46cd9', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42bfca73-008b-5ab5-8b14-ef6d20f46cd9'}, {'abstract': 'While the international exhibition is in session, Caroline Corbetta, a curator in Venice, has teamed up with the online retailer yoox.com to turn the focus on local artists.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/28/on-view-at-the-biennale-venices-locals-stage-a-show-of-their-own/', 'snippet': 'While the international exhibition is in session, Caroline Corbetta, a curator in Venice, has teamed up with the online retailer yoox.com to turn the focus on local artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every other year, the art world descends on Venice for the Biennale, a six-month-long flurry of artistic activity. This year’s Biennale, which opens for previews tomorrow, comprises an international exhibition, 88 different national pavilions and a host of collateral events and shows. The missing piece of the pie, according to Caroline Corbetta, the curator behind Il Crepaccio, a tiny exhibition space based out of the window of a restaurant in Milan, is the involvement of the young artists who actually live and work in Venice. “Every two years their city becomes the real center of the art world, but they draw no particular benefit in terms of visibility,” 