name': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f39424f-3061-5f9c-ae2a-3119131b7dc4', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f39424f-3061-5f9c-ae2a-3119131b7dc4'}, {'abstract': 'The pill is supposed to work by tinkering with the brain’s chemistry. Does erotica do the same?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/what-do-50-shades-of-grey-and-a-desire-pill-have-in-common/', 'snippet': 'The pill is supposed to work by tinkering with the brain’s chemistry. Does erotica do the same?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several times in the course of editing Daniel Bergner’s cover story this past Sunday on drug trials for a new female-desire pill, I thought about the “50 Shades of Grey” phenomenon. Bergner’s story is about women who lose interest in having sex. But when you dig a little, you find out that what these women really lose interest in is having sex with their long-term partners. And the women who are taking part in the drug trials are desperate to again feel some lust for their spouse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/11/magazine/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/11/magazine/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/11/magazine/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2/11-fifty-shades-of-grey2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_