ame': 'subject', 'value': 'Wearable Computing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meeker, Mary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Snapchat Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T16:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5208c89d-8f1f-5be3-9035-0b6787a440f9', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5208c89d-8f1f-5be3-9035-0b6787a440f9'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/the-gun-report-may-29-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been long reported that the N.R.A. is a front for the nation’s gun manufacturers—Business Insider revealed in January that the gun industry and its corporate allies have given the organization between $20 million and $52.6 million since 2005. But did you know the N.R.A. gets paid by the gun? Buried in a recent behind-the-scenes chronicle of the N.R.A.’s national convention in the Daily Beast is this striking fact:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: May 29, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARLINGTON (TEX)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cincinnati (Ohio)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Glendale (Ariz)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Grand Rapids (Mich)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Holyoke (Mass)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newport News (Va)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phoenix (Ariz)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sacramento (Calif)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tampa (Fla)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toledo (Ohio)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wichita (Kan)', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wilmington (Del)', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T16:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88cff0e5-fc4a-5fe2-9b8d-9d0d80dbfa02', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88cff0e5-fc4a-5fe2-9b8d-9d0d80dbfa02'}, {'abstract': 'Look at other markets.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/rate-stories/', 'snippet': 'Look at other markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been getting some questions about the recent rise in long-term interest rates. Those rates are still at levels that would have seemed absurdly low not long ago — but they are up significantly from a few months ago. What should we make of this move?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rate Stories', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T16:58:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e993591-5fd5-5bc0-81d5-395dca795582', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e993591-5fd5-5bc0-81d5-395dca795582'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Greenberg’s new book is critical of the “Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders,” the so-called bible of psychiatry.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/the-nature-of-suffering-gary-greenberg-talks-about-the-book-of-woe/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Greenberg’s new book is critical of the “Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders,” the so-called bible of psychiatry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, the American Psychiatric Association published the fifth edition of the “Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders.” In “The Book of Woe,” Gary Greenberg, a practicing psychotherapist, criticizes the manual while investigating the history of mental suffering and our attempts to find scientific answers for it. In a recent e-mail interview, Mr. Greenberg discussed the fifth edition, the ties between the psychiatric and pharmaceutical industries, the effectiveness of antidepressants and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 763}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 805, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1610, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/books/29greenberg/29greenberg-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Nature of Suffering: Gary Greenberg Talks About ‘The Book of Woe’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenberg, Gary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T17:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a94ba01-30d5-55c8-8679-567c91012bbf', 'word_count': 1389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a94ba01-30d5-55c8-8679-567c91012bbf'}, {'abstract': 'Passengers of American Airlines traveling with one personal carry-on item that fits under the seat and do not use the overhead bin can board earlier than some others.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/reducing-overhead/', 'snippet': 'Passengers of American Airlines traveling with one personal carry-on item that fits under the seat and do not use the overhead bin can board earlier than some others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traveling light, without checking bags, saves passengers time on arrival and money in fees. Now, American Airlines has introduced a new incentive: people traveling with one personal carry-on item that fits under the seat in front of them and do not use the overhead bin can board earlier than some others. And customers don’t have to decide in advance; if they have carry-on bags that they were planning to place in overhead storage but wish to board early, they can gate-check one bag at no charge. (The typical fee is $25.) The carrier expects the system to reduce average boarding time, speed departures and thus increase on-time arrivals.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reducing Overhead', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Airlines: Reducing Overhead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN AIRLINES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T17:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ac8fc78-b5f1-5818-b294-9e3f3755735d', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ac8fc78-b5f1-5818-b294-9e3f3755735d'}, {'abstract': 'The trade group representing major cruise lines has recently adopted a passenger bill of rights that includes several measures suggested this year by Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/29/a-passengers-bill-of-rights/', 'snippet': 'The trade group representing major cruise lines has recently adopted a passenger bill of rights that includes several measures suggested this year by Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trade group representing major cruise lines has recently adopted a passenger bill of rights that includes several measures suggested this year by Senator Charles E. Schumer of New York. The Cruise Lines International Association adopted 10 rights, including access to backup power, professional emergency medical care; timely information and crews properly trained in emergency and evacuation procedures. The rights apply to American passengers and those who buy their cruise in North America on North American member cruise lines, regardless of the flag the ship sails under. Mr. Schumer called the bill of rights “a step in the right direction” but said that he had questions on the content and its enforcement.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Passengers’ Bill of Rights', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cruises: A Passengers’ Bill of Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruises', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-29T17:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b492111d-78e6-5bc4-8e58-803155b7045c', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b492111d-78e6-5bc4-8e58-803155b7045c'}, {'abstract': 'The waterfront market in County Cork is picking up slightly, spurred in part by foreign buyers and Irish expatriates returning home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/greathomesanddestinations/real-estate-in-ireland.html', 'snippet': 'The waterfront market in County Cork is picking up slightly, spurred in part by foreign buyers and Irish expatriates returning home.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A CONTEMPORARY WATERFRONT HOUSE IN COUNTY CORK, IRELAND', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/greathomesanddestinations/29-GH-IHH-SPAN/29-GH-IHH-SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/29/greathomesanddestinations/29-GH-IHH-SPAN/29-GH-IHH-SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/greathomesanddestinations/29-GH-IHH-SPAN/29-GH-IHH-SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/greathomesanddestinations/29-GH-IHH-SPAN/29-GH-IHH-SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJ