 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8614d072-84c4-5d13-a28d-55b08ebda062', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8614d072-84c4-5d13-a28d-55b08ebda062'}, {'abstract': 'A task force urges New York City’s schools to adopt new approaches to discipline that would not criminalize minor misbehaviors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/opinion/new-york-citys-school-to-prison-pipeline.html', 'snippet': 'A task force urges New York City’s schools to adopt new approaches to discipline that would not criminalize minor misbehaviors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'School officials across the country responded to a surge in juvenile crime during the 1980s and the Columbine High School shootings a decade later by tightening disciplinary policies and increasing the number of police patrolling public schools. One unfortunate result has been the creation of a repressive environment in which young people are suspended, expelled or even arrested over minor misbehaviors — like talking back or disrupting class — that would once have been handled by the principal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The School-to-Prison Pipeline', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The School-to-Prison Pipeline', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Discipline (Students)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T01:13:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/51c41e00-9b21-5d2a-a961-e8ec2c8fe5b3', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51c41e00-9b21-5d2a-a961-e8ec2c8fe5b3'}, {'abstract': 'A bipartisan bill introduced in the Senate deserves to be passed because it would be a significant advance over the current Toxic Substances Control Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/opinion/a-senate-opening-to-strengthen-chemical-regulations.html', 'snippet': 'A bipartisan bill introduced in the Senate deserves to be passed because it would be a significant advance over the current Toxic Substances Control Act.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0A bill co-sponsored by eight Democrats and eight Republicans was introduced in the Senate last week to revise the Toxic Substances Control Act, which governs the regulation of chemicals used in consumer products and manufacturing processes. Almost everyone agrees that the law, enacted in 1976, is badly flawed because of the complexity, costs and delays it imposes on regulating chemicals. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Opening to Strengthen Chemical Regulations', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Opening to Strengthen Chemical Regulations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lautenberg, Frank R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vitter, David', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T01:13:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7379ba2-7239-5da9-82ee-8aba72df0d3f', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7379ba2-7239-5da9-82ee-8aba72df0d3f'}, {'abstract': 'Concessions by Sprint and SoftBank should satisfy national security concerns and make the telecommunications industry more competitive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/opinion/a-softbank-sprint-deal-could-benefit-mobile-users.html', 'snippet': 'Concessions by Sprint and SoftBank should satisfy national security concerns and make the telecommunications industry more competitive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal government committee has cleared the proposed acquisition of Sprint by SoftBank, a Japanese telecommunications company, after securing concessions from both of them that should address national security concerns about the deal. Though they still have to clear a few more hurdles, the deal’s approval by the Committee on Foreign Investment in the United States is encouraging news. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Deal That Could Benefit Mobile Users', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Deal That Could Benefit Mobile Users', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sprint Nextel Corporation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SOFTBANK Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 4,