': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60eb7161-ac71-5487-be3a-0b81a018e47e', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60eb7161-ac71-5487-be3a-0b81a018e47e'}, {'abstract': 'Lumping mothers who make more money than their spouses in with single mothers who are breadwinners through necessity as much as choice makes public opinion research on working mothers less useful.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/whats-wrong-with-the-breadwinner-moms-study/', 'snippet': 'Lumping mothers who make more money than their spouses in with single mothers who are breadwinners through necessity as much as choice makes public opinion research on working mothers less useful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 40 percent of all households as of 2011, according to a new release from Pew Social Trends, the mother was either the sole or primary source of family income — the breadwinner. That’s up from 11 percent in 1960. A total of 5.1 million of those breadwinners (37 percent) are married mothers who have a higher income than their husbands, and 8.6 million (63 percent) are single mothers. Is there a social consensus to be found by lumping together those disparate groups? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Wrong With the Breadwinner Moms Study', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T14:13:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/575597e2-4f91-5954-a120-78bd85971fb6', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/575597e2-4f91-5954-a120-78bd85971fb6'}, {'abstract': 'T