formances on Broadway.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/who-needs-a-tony-nomination-midlers-one-woman-show-pays-off/', 'snippet': '“I’ll Eat You Last,” starring Bette Midler, recoups its $2.4 million capitalization after eight weeks of performances on Broadway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bette Midler may have been passed over for a Tony Award nomination for her Broadway play, “I’ll Eat You Last,” but there is no denying that she has become a box-office powerhouse this season. Her one-woman comedy, written by the Tony winner John Logan (“Red”) about the 1970s Hollywood super-agent Sue Mengers, has recouped its $2.4 million capitalization after eight weeks of performances, the show’s producers announced on Thursday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1465, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/theater/30eat/30eat-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Needs a Tony Nomination? Midler’s One-Woman Show Pays Off', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Midler, Bette', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:29:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d47970a-de6d-531f-b7e1-fc1494ab3660', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d47970a-de6d-531f-b7e1-fc1494ab3660'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs of a 1984 conference at Rutgers devoted to the work of Donald Davidson reveal a historic gathering of philosophers. The Stone invites readers to help name those yet unidentified.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/a-great-day-for-philosophy/', 'snippet': 'Photographs of a 1984 conference at Rutgers devoted to the work of Donald Davidson reveal a historic gathering of philosophers. The Stone invites readers to help name those yet unidentified.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Along with the words to be offered here is an interesting artifact— a photograph of an assembly of some of the most important and influential philosophers of the latter half of the 20th century on one fine day in April in 1984 in front of the Hyatt Hotel in downtown New Brunswick, N.J.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Great Day for Philosophy', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davidson, Donald', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ernie Lepore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ernie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lepore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d7659a-2dae-5479-bc55-0155d8e58328', 'word_count': 1411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d7659a-2dae-5479-bc55-0155d8e58328'}, {'abstract': 'She said there was a “breach of trust” between government and the people.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/ann-romneys-two-cents/', 'snippet': 'She said there was a “breach of trust” between government and the people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ann Romney, best known for standing by her husband in his unsuccessful bid for the presidency, decided to unburden herself this morning on the sad state of politics. She said there is a “breach of trust” between government and the people, which is of course a very bad thing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ann Romney’s Two Cents', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Ann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:31:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58e25a8d-330a-5603-934b-de4ed4aaf5ce', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58e25a8d-330a-5603-934b-de4ed4aaf5ce'}, {'abstract': 'Four books about listening to music as process, reaction and ritual.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/the-art-of-writing-about-listening/', 'snippet': 'Four books about listening to music as process, reaction and ritual.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These days I am writing a book about listening to music. Not in the neurochemical way! (Remember the late-’90s to late-oughts vogue for books about how your brain reacts to baroque counterpoint, and all that? Oliver Sacks, Robert Jourdain, Daniel Levitin? Are you more engaged with music after reading those books, or do you just ask your hypothalamus what it thinks about the new jams?) I mean listening through, like, the ears.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 893, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 893}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/30/books/30listening/30listening-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Writing About Listening', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jourdain, Robert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sacks, Oliver', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Small, Christopher', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:41:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9eade8e-dc2f-5b23-b501-e57fac20653d', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9eade8e-dc2f-5b23-b501-e57fac20653d'}, {'abstract': '“Cinderella/Cendrillon,” based on the Brothers Grimm version of the Cinderella tale, will run at New York Theater Workshop in May 2014, the theater announced on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/cinderella-adaptation-to-play-off-broadway-in-2014/', 'snippet': '“Cinderella/Cendrillon,” based on the Brothers Grimm version of the Cinderella tale, will run at New York Theater Workshop in May 2014, the theater announced on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cinderella is one of those characters and stories, like Peter Pan and Alice in Wonderland, that theater directors and writers love to reimagine – and that reliably sell tickets. “Rodgers and Hammerstein’s Cinderella,” which began performances on Broadway in January, has regularly surpassed $1 million a week at the box office, and another musical based on the mistreated beauty and her fairy godmother – “Ever After” – is being developed by Broadway producers and director Kathleen Marshall. But next up is an Off Broadway play, “Cinderella/Cendrillon,” which will begin performances at New York Theater Workshop in May 2014, the theater announced on Thursday. The adaptation, based on the Brothers Grimm version of the tale, is written and directed by the French theater artist Joel Pommerat, who founded the Compagnie Louis Brouillard in 1990 and has worked at several Parisian theaters. The play has been translated into English and is the fourth and final production of the theater’s 2013-14 season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cinderella Adaptation to Play Off Broadway in 2014', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Theater Workshop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:48:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59766520-1733-526d-b070-57f204691397', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59766520-1733-526d-b070-57f204691397'}, {'abstract': 'Members of Generation X have some catching up to do to prepare for retirement.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/generation-x-hit-hardest-by-recession/', 'snippet': 'Members of Generation X have some catching up to do to prepare for retirement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Members of Generation X, now in their late 30s to late 40s, were especially hard-hit by the recent recession, and are at risk for downward mobility in their retirement years, a new report finds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Generation X Hit Hardest By Recession', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Charitable Trusts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f01f854-ebe0-535b-b03d-9d833d1f5229', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f01f854-ebe0-535b-b03d-9d833d1f5229'}, {'abstract': 'Baby boomers are a critical constituency for the national parks, but they are also a graying one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/booming/boomer-visits-to-national-parks-increase.html', 'snippet': 'Baby boomers are a critical constituency for the national parks, but they are also a graying one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just call us Old Faithfuls. When it comes to supporting the national parks, few have been as reliable as baby boomers. Forget geysers: maybe they should carve a new face onto Mount Rushmore: Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt, Lincoln and the Unknown Boomer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 479, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 479}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1633, 'width': 2045, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/booming/30question-booming-parks6/30question-booming-parks6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Am I at a National Park?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Road Trips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T16:58:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93b71442-a2b5-5d9a-b51a-f91a41b3f237', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93b71442-a2b5-5d9a-b51a-f91a41b3f237'}, {'abstract': 'Belize plans to raise the fines for destroying its archaeological sites.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/belize-protects-antiquities/', 'snippet': 'Belize plans to raise the fines for destroying its archaeological sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Belize plans to raise the fines for destroying its archaeological sites, according to its tourism agency. The move comes in response to a construction company’s recent leveling of part of a Mayan pyramid, above, that dates back some 2,300 years. The ancient site, Noh Mul, which translates as “Big Hill,” is on private land in the northern part of the country. The government has completed an investigation into the case and plans to charge the company under its antiquities laws, which make it illegal to destroy any ancient monument.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Belize Protects Antiquities', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tourism Sites: Belize Protects Antiquities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belize', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T17:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12dbc537-69b6-54b3-8a18-e67edfbd0237', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12dbc537-69b6-54b3-8a18-e67edfbd0237'}, {'abstract': 'The actress Lou Doillon, daughter of Jane Birkin, brings some American soul to her debut album, “Places.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/listen-up-a-french-movie-star-turned-sultry-singer-songwriter/', 'snippet': 'The actress Lou Doillon, daughter of Jane Birkin, brings some American soul to her debut album, “Places.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lou Doillon was born into French cultural royalty — her mother is Jane Birkin, her father the director Jacques Doillon. So it’s something of a surprise how much American soul runs through her debut album, “Places.” She sings in English, in a throaty, charismatic drawl backed by warm, midtempo arrangements that sound like they were laid down in Memphis, not Paris. Doillon’s half-sister, Charlotte Gainsbourg, was an established actress in Europe who gained a fervent following in the United States only after turning her attention to music. From the sounds of it, Doillon, already a popular model and actress in France, could be on the same path. Verve; $10 on iTunes.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listen Up | A French Movie Star Turned Sultry Singer-Songwriter', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French Twist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doillon, Lou', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gainsbourg, Charlotte', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T17:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Ashlock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ashlock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d06b2d2-f8d1-5fb7-b858-86da0ca13e2f', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d06b2d2-f8d1-5fb7-b858-86da0ca13e2f'}, {'abstract': 'If Congress reduces the corporate tax rate from 35 percent to 25 percent, as many have suggested, it could help reduce systemic risk in the banking industry.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/reducing-the-corporate-tax-rate-could-stabilize-banks/', 'snippet': 'If Congress reduces the corporate tax rate from 35 percent to 25 percent, as many have suggested, it could help reduce systemic risk in the banking industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans and Democrats have both expressed a desire to reduce the corporate tax rate from 35 percent to something in the neighborhood of 25 percent, which would be more in line with our major trading partners. The recent attention to the legal loopholes employed by Apple, Google, Starbucks and other multinational corporations to reduce their taxes could threaten the prospects of reducing the corporate rate. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reducing the Corporate Tax Rate Could Stabilize Banks', 'kicker': 'DealBook', '