s, to examine what others see as indecipherable or unimportant, to sift a story from the chaff and to resurrect names, places, actions and ideas that would otherwise be lost. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1359, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/JPBOOKFOX/JPBOOKFOX-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Brooklyn Breaker of Ancient Codes', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Brooklyn Breaker of Ancient Codes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, Margalit', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T19:09:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matti Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matti', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d42393a8-0f49-5e26-8b77-23338de15838', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d42393a8-0f49-5e26-8b77-23338de15838'}, {'abstract': 'Dots is addictive and very popular. More than three million people have downloaded the game since its debut in May, and now there’s an updated version that increases the competitive ante.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/highly-addictive-dots-game-passes-3-million-players/', 'snippet': 'Dots is addictive and very popular. More than three million people have downloaded the game since its debut in May, and now there’s an updated version that increases the competitive ante.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday afternoon my entire family sat in my sister’s living room, fingers jabbing at our iPhones, as we yelled a series of numbers aloud: “124!” “217!” “Oh, I just got to 400!”\xa0We were\xa0playing Dots, the highly addictive free game from Betaworks. (I won, with 474 points.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/technology/30bits-dots/30bits-dots-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 249, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/30/technology/30bits-dots/30bits-dots-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 249, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo',