the war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve seen the awesome, soul-wrenching “Photography and the American Civil War” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, you might suppose that a second exhibition about the war would be redundant. It’s not. “The Civil War and American Art,” also at the Met, is a gripping show that sheds further light on how the nation experienced its fratricidal conflict. Organized by Eleanor Jones Harvey, senior curator at the Smithsonian American Art Museum, it is not the broad survey that its title suggests. It is a lean, pointedly selective show, consisting of about 60 paintings, from 1852 to 1877 — including outstanding works by Frederic Edwin Church, Sanford Robinson Gifford and Winslow Homer — and 18 photographs taken during the war. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/CIVIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/CIVIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/CIVIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/CIVIL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/31CIVIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/31CIVIL/31CIVIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbn