 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6f508fd-8c86-56ef-b288-b8da5d52579d', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6f508fd-8c86-56ef-b288-b8da5d52579d'}, {'abstract': 'An agenda for an almost-movement.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/what-is-reform-conservatism/', 'snippet': 'An agenda for an almost-movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I noted earlier this week, there’s been a lot of liberal discussion of conservative reformers recently, ranging from the Washington Monthly’s attempt at a taxonomy of the tendency to Mike Konczal’s recent piece expressing doubts (echoed by my colleague Paul Krugman) about whether the reforming tendency within conservatism adds up to anything significant at all. Reading through the responses to Konczal’s post from “reformish” types, I’m in the rare position of disagreeing ever-so-slightly with my friend and co-author\xa0Reihan Salam,\xa0who writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Reform Conservatism?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subj