30/a-mystery-of-smithfields-big-china-deal-what-continental-grain-will-do/', 'snippet': 'With its proposed sale to Shuanghui International, Smithfield Foods appears to be eschewing a recommendation by Continental Grain, one of its biggest shareholders, to break itself up. It isn’t clear how Continental will respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Investors in Smithfield Foods, the pork processor, appeared largely pleased on Wednesday with its proposed $4.7 billion sale to a giant meat producer in China.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mystery of Smithfield’s Big China Deal: What Continental Grain Will Do', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JBS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Premium Standard Farms', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shuanghui International', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Smithfield Foods Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T20:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a09a9a0-381b-581b-aa93-bff15cff8f3b', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a09a9a0-381b-581b-aa93-bff15cff8f3b'}, {'abstract': 'An exclusive first look at portraits by the photographer Albert Watson of celebrities wearing fedoras, part of a new campaign to promote immigration reform.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/30/gallery-a-melting-pot-of-celebrities-makes-immigration-reform-look-fashionable/', 'snippet': 'An exclusive first look at portraits by the photographer Albert Watson of celebrities wearing fedoras, part of a new campaign to promote immigration reform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What do the actress Julianne Moore, the funnyman John Leguizamo, the supermodel Christy Turlington Burns and the director George C. Wolfe have in common? They’re among the 15 celebrities brought together by We Belong Together, a new national initiative to promote immigration reform. On a rainy day last week, this melting pot of talent gathered at a West Village studio to have their portraits taken by the fashion photographer Albert Watson, who immigrated to the United States from Scotland in 1970. The resulting black-and-white photos are the calling card for Fedoras for Fairness, an ad campaign created by We Belong Together that makes its debut today with support from the celebrity activist Rosie Perez. “I’m really proud of being a first-generation Puerto Rican-American,” said the actress, adding that America is “supposed to be known as the nation of fairness of democracy and of justice. And right now, the way immigration stands, that is not applied.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gallery | A Melting Pot of Celebrities Makes Immigration Reform Look Fashionable', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-30T20:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alainna Lexie Beddie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alainna', 'middlename': 'Lexie', 'lastname': 'Beddie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38cdbb71-24e9-50e9-a062-25aabc56dcf3', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38cdbb71-24e9-50e9-a062-25aabc56dcf3'}, {'abstract': 'In a surprise verdict, two close aides of the former Serbian President Slobodan Milosevic were found not liable for crimes committed by secret combat units they had commanded.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/world/europe/un-court-acquits-2-serbs-of-crimes-in-balkan-wars.html', 'snippet': 'In a surprise verdict, two close aides of the former Serbian President Slobodan Milosevic were found not liable for crimes committed by secret combat units they had commanded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — In one of the most surprising verdicts of its checkered prosecution of Balkan war crimes, a United Nations court on Thursday acquitted two close aides of the former Serbian leader Slobodan Milosevic, spurning the prosecution’s demand for life sentences for his once all-powerful secret police chief and the chief’s deputy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/world/sub-hague1/sub-hague1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/world/sub-hague1/sub-hague1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 707}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/world/sub-hague1/sub-hague1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 947, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/world/sub-hague1/sub-hague1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 947, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None,