 {'original': 'By Lisa Schlesinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schlesinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c544365-6236-5962-a4b7-13e7a0a34838', 'word_count': 1503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c544365-6236-5962-a4b7-13e7a0a34838'}, {'abstract': 'Marina Zenovich’s “Omit the Logic” delves into Richard Pryor’s life through those who knew him best.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/arts/television/richard-pryor-omit-the-logic-on-showtime.html', 'snippet': 'Marina Zenovich’s “Omit the Logic” delves into Richard Pryor’s life through those who knew him best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching the parade of comedy heavyweights and cultural heroes assembled for the biographical documentary “Richard Pryor: Omit the Logic,” my mind wandered to the roast of Pryor by the cast of his 1977 NBC television show. (The unedited version is on YouTube.) As you watch him being flayed by the largely unknown group that included future stars like Tim Reid, Robin Williams, Marsha Warfield, Sandra Bernhard and John Witherspoon — as usual, Pryor was ahead of the times — you can’t wait for him to get to the microphone and take revenge. When he does, he ad-libs a 13-minute rejoinder that’s as cruel and foul (and homophobic) as you might fear and as coruscatingly funny as you could hope.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/PRYOR/PRYOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/PRYOR/PRYOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 834}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/arts/