hem stay competitive?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting a Fair Share From Multinationals', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T01:09:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00dbe15b-e25d-5ed8-b9d0-7035440678f8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00dbe15b-e25d-5ed8-b9d0-7035440678f8'}, {'abstract': 'Corporate income taxes need to be reformed, not abolished.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/opinion/no-replacement-for-corporate-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'Corporate income taxes need to be reformed, not abolished.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In response to the recent Senate hearing on tax avoidance at Apple, some pundits have argued that the best solution is to abolish the corporate income tax altogether. Their contention is that multinational corporations — which hold trillions of dollars in mostly untaxed profits offshore — are so complex and so expert at avoidance that even trying to tax them is pointless. It would be better, they say, to quit trying and to raise needed tax revenue in other ways. This would be a completely wrong approach. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Replacement for Corporate Taxes', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Replacement for Corporate Taxes ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Shelters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T01:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/30a87462-1cc0-5758-a499-be7a421ec610', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30a87462-1cc0-5758-a499-be7a421ec610'}, {'abstract': 'A program financed by the Agency for International Development is dissolving because of mismanagement, insider dealings and a lack of federal oversight.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/business/southern-africa-development-fund-mired-in-debt.html', 'snippet': 'A program financed by the Agency for International Development is dissolving because of mismanagement, insider dealings and a lack of federal oversight.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The initiative began two decades ago, with the best of intentions, after apartheid fell and southern Africa’s future brightened. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/business/Fund1/Fund1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/business/Fund1/Fund1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/business/Fund1/Fund1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/business/Fund1/Fund1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1392, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Promising African Development Fund Collapses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Promising African Development Fund Collapses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Ron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Agency for International Development', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Andrew (1932- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T01:11:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Meier and Ron Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d8800d5-fe38-5f7f-a3dc-d7fa4a1e69b1', 'word_count': 1368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d8800d5-fe38-5f7f-a3dc-d7fa4a1e69b1'}, {'abstract': 'The government must speak and act against extremist violence if Myanmar wants to realize its democratic aspirations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/opinion/attacks-on-myanmars-muslims.html', 'snippet': 'The government must speak and act against extremist violence if Myanmar wants to realize its democratic aspirations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terrifying anti-Muslim violence surged this week in Myanmar, exposing deep ethnic and religious tensions that are undermining efforts to stabilize the country and move forward with political and economic reforms. Myanmar’s democratic aspirations can never be fully realized if Muslims, who make up 