 {'abstract': 'A biography of the band that, for a brief moment, held the future of rock ’n’ roll in its hands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/the-stone-roses-war-and-peace-by-simon-spence.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of the band that, for a brief moment, held the future of rock ’n’ roll in its hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some bands are famous for capturing the spirit of an era. Others are famous, or almost famous, for capturing the spirit of a fleeting moment — a bright, pivotal summer or even a single incandescent show — and then utterly squandering the whole opportunity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGORDINIER/02SUBGORDINIER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Wanna Be Adored', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Wanna Be Adored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordinier, Jeff', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stone Roses (Music Group)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spence, Simon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:26:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/673f4001-b2a9-5b88-b667-4c9d3c9aff54', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/673f4001-b2a9-5b88-b667-4c9d3c9aff54'}, {'abstract': 'Touré’s critical study of Prince, based on lectures the author delivered at Harvard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/toures-i-would-die-4-u-why-prince-became-an-icon.html', 'snippet': 'Touré’s critical study of Prince, based on lectures the author delivered at Harvard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cover of “Dirty Mind,” Prince’s ingenious/ingenuous 1980 exercise in self-reinvention, threw down a rose-scented gauntlet. It featured a black-and-white shot of a skinny young man who barely seemed out of his teens (he was 22), looking as much the racially and sexually ambiguous wild boy as a nice Minneapolis lad could. Sporting a thin gigolo mustache, a Joan Jett haircut and an open, oversize jacket that he could have swiped from a doorman at the Grand Hotel, Prince topped off his new image with a kerchief, bare chest and thong with a virile little tuft of hair peeking over the bikini line.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1626, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUB2HAMPTON/02SUB2HAMPTON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prince', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toure', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:27:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/68dbc02f-d8fc-5238-b76a-1a01fc7b8657', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68dbc02f-d8fc-5238-b76a-1a01fc7b8657'}, {'abstract': 'Self-plagiarism, grounds for expulsion, an exercise in intellectual complexity or all of the above?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/magazine/can-i-use-the-same-paper-for-multiple-college-courses.html', 'snippet': 'Self-plagiarism, grounds for expulsion, an exercise in intellectual complexity or all of the above?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was in college, I’d sometimes write a single paper that would satisfy assignments in more than one course. For instance, I once wrote a paper on how “The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock” expressed satire; I submitted it for assignments in both my poetry course as well as my completely separate satire course. I did not disclose this to either professor. When I share this with people, half call the practice cheating, and the other half call it genius. My niece told me it would certainly be grounds for expulsion at her college. In my mind, I was adding a level of intellectual complexity to my studies. Was this an ethical practice, or was I cheating? JOE, CONNECTICUT', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can I Use the Same Paper for Multiple College Courses?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decisions and (No) Revisions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plagiarism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:27:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fb3e7ae-1cfb-5525-b79f-6f9b3cc4c870', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fb3e7ae-1cfb-5525-b79f-6f9b3cc4c870'}, {'abstract': 'From theme parks to airports, the simple queue is under attack.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/magazine/want-to-save-civilization-get-in-line.html', 'snippet': 'From theme parks to airports, the simple queue is under attack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The next time you’re stuck behind 250 shoppers in a checkout line that snakes around the aisles in a Trader Joe’s, take a moment to ponder exactly what’s going on. Yes, you’re standing in a queue. Yes, it can be the pits. Except that, all things considered, it’s also pretty great.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'height': 83, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 