uth-r-wisse.html', 'snippet': 'Ruth R. Wisse recounts the long history of Jewish humor and brings it up to date.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the praise heaped on it by some analysts of Jewish humor, one might almost think the chosen people were chosen for the quality of their jokes. Freud’s “Jokes and Their Relation to the Unconscious,” which was published in 1905, drew almost exclusively on Jewish stories, because, he maintained, they were the funniest and the most interesting. Salcia Landmann, a scholar of Yiddish who died in 2002, argued that Jewish humor is “more acute, more profound and richer in expression than that of any other people.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 719}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 855, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1035, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02gottlieb-img/02gottlieb-img-t