tors of Superman,  sold their rights to the iconic character for next to nothing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We all know who Superman is. If you’re younger than 75, you don’t even remember a time when he didn’t exist. With the possible exception of Mickey Mouse, the indestructible and incorruptible Man of Steel is surely the most famous fictional character ever created in cartoon form.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 288}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02keepnews-img/02keepnews-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Superheroes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Superheroes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keepnews, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Superman (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegel, Jerry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shuster, Joe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ricca, Brad', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:33:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f821a34d-fb3b-542e-9dab-0aa0f3ddbfa4', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f821a34d-fb3b-542e-9dab-0aa0f3ddbfa4'}, {'abstract': 'New books go behind the scenes of two eponymous TV ventures: “The Mary Tyler Moore Show” and “The Flip Wilson Show.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/mary-and-lou-and-rhoda-and-ted-and-flip.html', 'snippet': 'New books go behind the scenes of two eponymous TV ventures: “The Mary Tyler Moore Show” and “The Flip Wilson Show.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long after “The Mary Tyler Moore Show” ended its run, and a New York station was airing back-to-back reruns around 1:30 a.m., I remember watching and hearing the familiar “Love Is All Around” theme echoing from both my upstairs and downstairs neighbors. As “Friends” and “Seinfeld” would later, in its afterlife “The Mary Tyler Moore Show” became a reassuring, unfailingly funny end to the day, as much a social phenomenon as a sitcom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 918, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 918}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 670, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1276, 'width': 834, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBJAMES/02SUBJAMES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Classic ’70s Television', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classic ’70s Television', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Mary Tyler Moore Show (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Flip', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Armstrong, Jennifer Keishin', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cook, Kevin', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caryn James', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09b6796e-109a-5a51-a56e-76a6443bd1c9', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09b6796e-109a-5a51-a56e-76a6443bd1c9'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Weintraub details baseball’s joyous reacclimatization after World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/the-victory-season-by-robert-weintraub.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Weintraub details baseball’s joyous reacclimatization after World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday, April 15, 1946, Jews observed Passover in Germany for the first time without fear since 1933. On the following Thursday, the League of Nations was dissolved. And on Sunday came the death of John Maynard Keynes. To the average American, however, the momentous week’s main event occurred on Tuesday, April 16 — when the unabashed Cardinals fan Harry Truman threw out the first pitch on opening day at Griffith Stadium in Washington, ending the presidential tradition’s four-year hiatus. (In 1942, faced with the Japanese invasion of the Philippines and the ensuing Bataan Death March, Franklin Roosevelt had deputized his vice president, Henry Wallace, to perform the ceremonial toss.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1482, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCARTER/02SUBCARTER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winning', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Winning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weintraub, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf4b1cdb-cffd-57fd-8f09-e9fd4aca2038', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf4b1cdb-cffd-57fd-8f09-e9fd4aca2038'}, {'abstract': 'The veteran sportswriter Allen Barra explores in depth the remarkable similarities between two great New York center fielders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/mickey-and-willie-by-allen-barra.html', 'snippet': 'The veteran sportswriter Allen Barra explores in depth the remarkable similarities between two great New York center fielders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time I played ball against Willie Mays, in 1965, it was impossible to take my eyes off him. At bat, in the outfield and on the bases, he did everything differently from — and better than — anybody else. Mays’s style of play could come only from an impressive intelligence and a thoroughly cultivated knowledge of the game.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 768}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SWOBODA/02SWOBODA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doubleheader', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doubleheader', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barra, Allen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mays, Willie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mantle, Mickey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:35:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Swoboda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swoboda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf577d5-86f4-5a47-9f72-8bef7981d33f', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf577d5-86f4-5a47-9f72-8bef7981d33f'}, {'abstract': 'The French Open win by John Isner is the first in his career from down two sets. It also snapped his streak of five consecutive fifth-set losses.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/down-two-sets-isner-rebounds-and-ends-fifth-set-struggles/', 'snippet': 'The French Open win by John Isner is the first in his career from down two sets. It also snapped his streak of five consecutive fifth-set losses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was the first time two American men had faced each other in the main draw of the French Open since 2001, but John Isner and Ryan Harrison compensated for the drought by playing deep into a fifth set, with Isner coming back from down two sets and prevailing, 5-7, 6-7 (7), 6-3, 6-1, 8-6, in 3 hours 50 minutes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Isner Rebounds and Ends Fifth-Set Struggles', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isner, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:36:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d639bafb-b6c5-5568-9d48-da79ccaa6fb6', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d639bafb-b6c5-5568-9d48-da79ccaa6fb6'}, {'abstract': 'Scott McClanahan writes of a world in West Virginia where existence is both tedious and shocking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/crapalachia-by-scott-mcclanahan.html', 'snippet': 'Scott McClanahan writes of a world in West Virginia where existence is both tedious and shocking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Grotesquerie is a fact of life for certain classes, the wallpaper for folks who dwell beneath a certain rung of the ladder. Those without health insurance, say. Or money for gas. Or dentists. My people were West Virginia factory workers and, as such, immersed in enough gothic absurdity to drown Flannery O’Connor. They were bawdy and hard-hewn and never surprised when tragedy befell their lives. They didn’t even call it tragedy, since tragedy was a thing that happened to rich folks with something to lose.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBGLOCK/02SUBGLOCK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Country Living', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Country Living', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McClanahan, Scott', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:36:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allison Glock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20f10694-902b-542f-ba27-4cb836f6f6ed', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20f10694-902b-542f-ba27-4cb836f6f6ed'}, {'abstract': 'The anthology “Yes Is the Answer” celebrates prog rock in all its tomfoolery and excess.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/yes-is-the-answer-and-other-prog-rock-tales.html', 'snippet': 'The anthology “Yes Is the Answer” celebrates prog rock in all its tomfoolery and excess.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oscar Wilde once said, “Experience is the name everyone gives to their mistakes.” But for men born about a century after Wilde, mistakes tended to have more colorful names, like “Yes,” “Genesis” or “Emerson, Lake and Palmer.” These were the hairy dragon-kings of prog rock (short for “progressive”), the bands that ruled the FM airwaves of the 1970s, providing black-light initiations for their adolescent male devotees. Even after all these years, prog remains one of the most intrinsically silly of rock fads: concept albums, ornate time signatures, keyboard solos, lyrical ruminations on the tendency of mountains to fall out of the sky.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '49', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBSHEFFIELD/02SUBSHEFFIELD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prog Rock', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prog Rock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weingarten, Marc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornell, Tyson', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:39:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Sheffield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheffield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae80bc24-cee7-524e-b6c9-db6dbc279928', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae80bc24-cee7-524e-b6c9-db6dbc279928'}, {'abstract': 'Side Street: The Rev. John C. Flynn, who was no stranger to a basketball court, died last year at 83. Now he is being honored in the neighborhood he served: A court has been named for him at a Bronx school.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/in-a-rooftop-gym-a-fitting-tribute-to-a-beloved-priest/', 'snippet': 'Side Street: The Rev. John C. Flynn, who was no stranger to a basketball court, died last year at 83. Now he is being honored in the neighborhood he served: A court has been named for him at a Bronx school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the final years of his life, when his memory was fading but his strength remained solid, the Rev. John C. Flynn paced the halls of a Bronx nursing home, talking with the lonely, smiling to all – and swinging an imaginary golf club. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/nyregion/01sidestreet-cityroom/01sidestreet-cityroom-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/01/nyregion/0