bo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-CUMMING/0602-bks-CUMMING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-CUMMING/0602-bks-CUMMING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-CUMMING/0602-bks-CUMMING-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spy vs. Spy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spy vs. Spy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matthews, Jason (1951- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Red Sparrow (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:40:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Cumming', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c764f02c-5cb3-58e0-b7fd-84510b6f9732', 'word_count': 716, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c764f02c-5cb3-58e0-b7fd-84510b6f9732'}, {'abstract': 'Scoundrels, artists and geniuses commingle in this ambitious history of Greenwich Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/the-village-by-john-strausbaugh.html', 'snippet': 'Scoundrels, artists and geniuses commingle in this ambitious history of Greenwich Village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hail, hail, the gang’s all here: a galaxy of scoundrels, artists and geniuses commingle in John Strausbaugh’s ambitious 600-plus-page history of Greenwich Village. Strausbaugh — who presided over this newspaper’s “Weekend Explorer” series on New York City and wrote what felt like the entirety of The New York Press in its 1990s heyday — turns a collection of stories and profiles into something less like a textbook than a party spinning happily out of control.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02SUBCALHOUN/02SUBCALHOUN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Downtown', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Downtown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strausbaugh, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:40:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ada Calhoun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calhoun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cea6377f-7566-502b-b00c-1b4ed22f7989', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cea6377f-7566-502b-b00c-1b4ed22f7989'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years after his death, J. R. R. Tolkien is on the hardcover fiction list again, this time with “The Fall of Arthur,” which makes its debut at No. 10.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/09/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Forty years after his death, J. R. R. Tolkien is on the hardcover fiction list again, this time with “The Fall of Arthur,” which makes its debut at No. 10.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LORD OF THE LIST: Forty years after his death, in 1973, J. R. R. Tolkien is on the hardcover fiction list again, this time with “The Fall of Arthur,” an unfinished epic poem that makes its debut at No. 10. Tolkien has had an active after\xadlife; reviewing the book in The Los Angeles Times last month, Elizabeth Hand noted that it lists nine works published during his lifetime and 24 published posthumously (at least six of which have been best sellers). Hand liked the book, with some reservations: “ ‘The Fall of Arthur’ is a fascinating work, though perhaps more for the Tolkien completist than the casual ‘Lord of the Rings’ fan,” she wrote. “It focuses on the bloody aftermath of Queen Guinevere’s affair with Lancelot, greatest of Arthur’s knights, and is surprisingly powerful in its terse depiction of the affair’s devastating consequences.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02tolkien/02tolkien-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02tolkien/02tolkien-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02tolkien/02tolkien-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02tolkien/02tolkien-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02tolkien/02tolkien-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19dab517-a07f-552c-a40c-96d168e75d63', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19dab517-a07f-552c-a40c-96d168e75d63'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE HANGING GARDEN,  by Patrick White. (Picador, paper, $15.) In this long fragment of a novel that White, the Australian Nobelist, left unfinished at his death 23 years ago, two refugee children are mistreated by a world that, by its lights, means no harm.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:42:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/11204187-a41d-5035-a51c-ef0ae249081e', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11204187-a41d-5035-a51c-ef0ae249081e'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE REVISED FUNDAMENTALS OF CAREGIVING,  by Jonathan Evison. (Algonquin, $14.95.) Ben, the 39-year-old narrator of Evison’s bittersweet novel, has lost his children in an accident and is losing his wife to divorce. At the end of his rope, he takes a job as full-time caregiver to Trevor, an angry, girl-crazed teenager suffering from Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Together they take a madcap road trip, from Washington State to Utah, to visit Trevor’s estranged father.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5bba716-6af7-5a66-baf5-28cffecd3eeb', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5bba716-6af7-5a66-baf5-28cffecd3eeb'}, {'abstract': 'Everything old can be new again, especially when you are cooking with ancient grains, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/new-tricks-for-old-grains/', 'snippet': 'Everything old can be new again, especially when you are cooking with ancient grains, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything old can be new again, especially when you are cooking with ancient grains, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health. Ancients grains like farro and sorghum have been dietary staples for hundreds of years, but many of today’s eaters are just discovering their hearty flavor and chewy