ml', 'snippet': 'They defeated Hitler, but they did so on behalf of the Soviet Union, whose own anti-Semitism led millions of its Jews to flee once they were able.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEST HOLLYWOOD, Calif. — When Rabbi Naftali Estulin walked into the synagogue, the men were waiting for him. They had come with canes and walkers and medals. Some wore yarmulkes. Several covered their heads with hats lettered “LA VETERAN ASSN WWII.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/01/us/RELIGION/RELIGION-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Victory Day, Rabbi Honors Red Army’s Jewish Veterans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Victory Day, Rabbi Honors Red Army’s Jewish Veterans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estulin, Naftali', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freedman, Samuel G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Army (Soviet Union)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:46:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae3ad6d5-3be9-5924-a909-5743d937e771', 'word_count': 1011, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae3ad6d5-3be9-5924-a909-5743d937e771'}, {'abstract': 'Four new picture books about rainy weather.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/water-in-the-park-by-emily-jenkins-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Four new picture books about rainy weather.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Rain, rain, go away.” Kids have always chanted the familiar refrain — gazing out the window wistfully, chin on fist — as if they had the power to make it happen. But these days, what’s a little precipitation when there are peevish birds to be flung and other indoor, on-screen distractions? In an era in which the directive “Go play” no longer implies “outside,” the idea that rain may affect a child’s fun is perhaps a quaint one. But four new picture books remind us that no matter how cosseted we are by technology, a downpour still holds the power to move us.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1968, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-JURIS-1/0602-bks-JURIS-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Splish Splash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Splish Splash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashman, Linda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, Mary Lyn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Geisert, Arthur', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jenkins, Emily', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:47:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carolyn Juris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carolyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Juris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8d24cd0-fcca-5fbe-9ec0-b3b9c8e00d79', 'word_count': 1023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8d24cd0-fcca-5fbe-9ec0-b3b9c8e00d79'}, {'abstract': 'A novel and a memoir about growing up with secrets in religious families.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/rapture-practice-and-openly-straight.html', 'snippet': 'A novel and a memoir about growing up with secrets in religious families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aaron Hartzler and Rafe Goldberg grow up mostly as polar opposites. Aaron comes from a fundamentalist Christian Kansas City family; Rafe is a child of modern-\xadhippie Boulder, Colo., (stereo)types. Aaron, the main character in the memoir “Rapture Practice,” is real; Rafe, the protagonist in Bill Konigsberg’s novel “Openly Straight,” is made up. But both wrestle, over several hundred pages, with their identities. And both share a secret: They’re liars.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out and About', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out and About', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chu, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Konigsberg, Bill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hartzler, Aaron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:47:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Chu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/af9a281f-cf97-5353-b78b-3ed1e4c861fc', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af9a281f-cf97-5353-b78b-3ed1e4c861fc'}, {'abstract': 'The French silversmith opens a pop-up, in preparation for a Beverly Hills store.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/garden/christofle-pop-up-opens-in-west-hollywood.html', 'snippet': 'The French silversmith opens a pop-up, in preparation for a Beverly Hills store.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shoppers sauntering along Melrose Place might mistake Christofle’s pop-up shop for just another beautifully merchandised lifestyle store in a section of West Hollywood, Calif., that includes Marc Jacobs and Isabel Marant. Studio Christofle, as it’s called, opened in early May and looks unexpectedly eclectic for the home of a venerable French silver company. The styling and merchandising of the 1,100-square-foot space is a result of two strategic collaborations. JF Chen antiques furnished it with pieces like a 19th century Chinese wedding armoire (now styled with Christofle candelabra, pitchers and vases) and Marco Zanini’s throne-line Roma chair for Memphis. Arcana: Books on the Arts supplied rare art, architecture, fashion and design books. “Christofle has different faces,” said Stéphane Parmentier, the company’s creative director, who organized the store. “We are traditional, but we are also youthful and practical.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 950, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 950, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/31/garden/31open/31open-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A West Hollywood Place-Setting for Christofle', 'kicker': 'Currents | Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A West Hollywood Place-Setting for Christofle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Christofle Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop-Up Stores and Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f682189-d546-5e64-9a0f-254231f3fe53', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f682189-d546-5e64-9a0f-254231f3fe53'}, {'abstract': 'This graphic novel presents the stories of three women who profoundly changed our understanding of the great apes: Dian Fossey, Biruté Galdikas and Jane Goodall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/primates-by-jim-ottaviani.html', 'snippet': 'This graphic novel presents the stories of three women who profoundly changed our understanding of the great apes: Dian Fossey, Biruté Galdikas and Jane Goodall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stories of scientists create new scientists. Alexander von Humboldt — the most famous naturalist of the early 19th century — chronicled his epic expeditions, between 1799 and 1804, in his “Personal Narrative of a Journey to the Equinoctial Regions of the New Continent.” When a nature-loving student at Cambridge named Charles Darwin read the book, it changed his life. He read passages aloud to his professors and learned Spanish so that he could follow in Humboldt’s footsteps. Humboldt’s “Personal Narrative,” Darwin later wrote, “stirred up in me a burning zeal to add even the most humble contribution to the noble structure of Natural Science.” At age 22, he embarked on his own voyage around the world, out of which he would develop his theory of evolution. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-ZIMMER/0602-bks-ZIMMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 533, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-ZIMMER/0602-bks-ZIMMER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 533, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-ZIMMER/0602-bks-ZIMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-ZIMMER/0602-bks-ZIMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-ZIMMER/0602-bks-ZIMMER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Apes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ottaviani, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fossey, Dian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodall, Jane', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wicks, Maris', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galdikas, Birute', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:47:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4b01735-8016-542d-82d2-6dcb2b5ba694', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4b01735-8016-542d-82d2-6dcb2b5ba694'}, {'abstract': 'In Emily Murdoch’s first novel, a girl is haunted by secrets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/if-you-find-me-by-emily-murdoch.html', 'snippet': 'In Emily Murdoch’s first novel, a girl is haunted by secrets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The secrets we hold as children, especially those born from familial dysfunction, can be particularly pernicious: too young to handle our experience or verbalize our feelings, and mistakenly convinced we’re at fault for events beyond our control, we develop sophisticated systems of self-\xaddefense that become second nature, deeply tied to our identity — until it’s time to let them go. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Secrets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:49:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Joiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d707da62-eb7a-5dd4-a82a-019eec2ab802', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d707da62-eb7a-5dd4-a82a-019eec2ab802'}, {'abstract': 'Today’s tourists might learn something from the traveler-memoirists of earlier generations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/literary-excursions.html', 'snippet': 'Today’s tourists might learn something from the traveler-memoirists of earlier generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a trip to Tulum last Christmas, I think I managed the true Epicurean ideal of hedonism, sipping gently of the tropical sun, bodysurfing more than I read, reading more than I ate, and eating more than I worried. The entire endeavor was calibrated to maximize pleasure and minimize hardship, and so I learned nothing. I relaxed. I kept myself entertained. I visited the local Mayan ruins and paid more consideration to the vigilant application of sun goop than to the historical artifacts on display. I had joined the neocolonialist community that, through sky miles and vacation days, has effectively turned the developing world into a Sandals resort. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1121, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1121}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1715, 'width': 918, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Wallace/0602-bks-Wallace-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Literary Excursions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Literary Excursions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T14:50:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Wallace', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wallace', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3fc60f4-7cb5-5a50-a3ba-7ba8ae404db8', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3fc60f4-7cb5-5a50-a3ba-7ba8ae404db8'}, {'abstract': 'New comics include “Relish,” Lucy Knisley’s memoir about various episodes in her life and what she was eating at the time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/relish-by-lucy-knisley-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New comics include “Relish,” Lucy Knisley’s memoir about various episodes in her life and what she was eating at the time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lucy Knisley has eaten well all her life, and everything she tastes is apparently a madeleine for some happy memory. She grew up with a mother who worked for David Bouley and an uncle who ran a gourmet shop; the cover of RELISH: My Life in the Kitchen (First Second, paper, $17.99) shows her lifting an olive to her open mouth, her eyes closed in elated anticipation. It’s a memoir with only the slightest hints of conflict or struggle, apart from an early scene in which young Lucy is attacked by a flock of geese. (“I have since eaten foie gras with great enjoyment and very little guilt,” she notes.) She maps out her emotional associations with her favorite foods — Venetian croissants, sautéed mushrooms, McDonald’s fries and so on — in a breezy clear-line style, lingering over treasured moments, like resting her hand against Richard Serra’s “Tilted Arc” during a catering gig. Between chapters, Knisley offers simple, neatly diagrammed recipes (chocolate chip cookies! pesto! sangria!). Her language, like her drawings, is precise and uncluttered: in one of the book’s liveliest sequences, 12-year-old Lucy and a friend wander through a little Mexican town, discovering treats like “sweet corn on a stick, with hot sauce and lime, which turned the corners of our mouths red.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-WOLK/0602-bks-WOLK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 217, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-WOLK/0602-bks-WOLK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 217}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-WOLK/0602-bks-WOLK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 1024, 'subType'