ey” is a French thriller that opens for a run at the AMC Empire 25 in Midtown Manhattan on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps it says something about Europe’s economic and unemployment troubles that a man who robs banks for a living is the good guy in “The Prey,” a French thriller that opens for a run at the AMC Empire 25 in Midtown Manhattan on Friday. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turning a Robber Into the Good Guy', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turning a Robber Into the Good Guy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T15:26:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c29b3e6d-a68a-5c15-93e0-aef9955579e5', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c29b3e6d-a68a-5c15-93e0-aef9955579e5'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Peter Lattman talks about the new memoir by the tennis star Jimmy Connors; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Alexandra Jacobs discusses Patricia Volk’s “Shocked”; Anthony Gottlieb on Ruth R. Wisse’s “No Joke: Making Jewish Humor”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/05/31/books/review/02books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Peter Lattman talks about the new memoir by the tennis star Jimmy Connors; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Alexandra Jacobs discusses Patricia Volk’s “Shocked”; Anthony Gottlieb on Ruth R. Wisse’s “No Joke: Making Jewish Humor”; ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Peter Lattman talks about the new memoir by the tennis star Jimmy Connors; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Alexandra Jacobs discusses Patricia Volk’s “Shocked”; Anthony Gottlieb on Ruth R. Wisse’s “No Joke: Making Jewish Humor”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the New York Times Book Review: Summer Reading', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T15:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/d32fa23a-0b05-5cf1-bed3-6b34700726c0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/d32fa23a-0b05-5cf1-bed3-6b34700726c0'}, {'abstract': 'The singer-songwriter, previously known as the frontwoman for the brother-sister duo Fiery Furnaces, has established herself as a solo artist to be reckoned with.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/listen-up-eleanor-friedbergers-70s-song-craft/', 'snippet': 'The singer-songwriter, previously known as the frontwoman for the brother-sister duo Fiery Furnaces, has established herself as a solo artist to be reckoned with.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think we’re drawn to the decade we were born in,” said the singer-songwriter Eleanor Friedberger, who was born in 1976. “I love looking at ’70s interiors, textiles or graphic design and thinking, If I could turn this picture into music, what would it sound like?” Her songs do sound the way a Barbara D’Arcy room or Milton Glaser album cover looks: warm, inviting and groovy. Though she’s best known as the frontwoman for the Fiery Furnaces, the Brooklyn indie rock band she founded with her brother Matthew more than a decade ago, the release of “Personal Record” (Merge), her second solo album next week, should solidify her identity as a stand-alone musician. Steeped in the analog sounds of Me Decade icons like Harry Nilsson, Emitt Rhodes and Caetano Veloso, the album is more mellifluous and emotionally resonant than the Furnaces’ eccentric work. Instead, it builds on the classic song craft of Friedberger’s 2011 solo debut, “Last Summer,” interweaving bossa nova beats and British folk balladry with summer-road-trip rock ‘n’ roll. It’s as relatable — and as much a career high-water mark — as its title suggests. “I don’t get to hide under a band blanket,” she says of flying solo. “But it also feels more authentically me.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listen Up | Eleanor Friedberger’s ’70s Song Craft', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now Listening: A Child Of the Seventies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiery Furnaces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T15:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c35e2e34-766c-5943-9c75-43cb016147f4', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c35e2e34-766c-5943-9c75-43cb016147f4'}, {'abstract': 'For serious ballet fans, there is no other place to be this week than the Metropolitan Opera House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/arts/dance/serious-stuff-with-bare-midriffs.html', 'snippet': 'For serious ballet fans, there is no other place to be this week than the Metropolitan Opera House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For serious ballet fans, there is no other place to be this week than the Metropolitan Opera House, where American Ballet Theater enters the third week of its spring season with its final performance of Alexei Ratmansky’s “Shostakovich Trilogy,” on Monday. The first section, “Symphony No. 9,” had its premiere during the company’s fall season last year, but the remaining two parts (“Chamber Symphony” and “Piano Concerto No. 1”) are brand new.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Serious Stuff, With Bare Midriffs', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Serious Stuff, With Bare Midriffs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T15:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae655c1f-8420-58a1-b5c3-e6171a164648', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae655c1f-8420-58a1-b5c3-e6171a164648'}, {'abstract': 'The fourth day of an Istanbul sit-in turned violent when the police moved in to disperse protesters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/05/31/world/europe/20130531TURKEY.html', 'snippet': 'The fourth day of an Istanbul sit-in turned violent when the police moved in to disperse protesters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fourth day of an Istanbul sit-in turned violent when the police moved in to disperse protesters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 