1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d0e8d57-77d3-5e3e-a8aa-2d6e06d9e81f', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d0e8d57-77d3-5e3e-a8aa-2d6e06d9e81f'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss Black Flag’s legacy and competing reunion tours.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/popcast-black-flag-revisited-twice/', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss Black Flag’s legacy and competing reunion tours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From 1979 to 1986 Black Flag was one of America’s greatest punk bands — maybe one of America’s greatest bands, full stop. But there were more than a dozen different Black Flags during that period, through frequent changes of lineup and at least one change of style. (Around 1983, Greg Ginn, the band’s lead guitarist, principal songwriter, record-label boss, musical director, and only permanent member of the group, radically slowed its tempos down.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1307, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/29/arts/blackflag2/blackflag2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: Black Flag Revisited, Twice', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Black Flag (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T16:46:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc3af57d-8aa9-5d82-a262-dc8c66466dac', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc3af57d-8aa9-5d82-a262-dc8c66466dac'}, {'abstract': 'No Canadian team has won the N.H.L. championship since 1993. How unlikely is this 20-year drought? And is there anything in the league’s economics that might explain it?', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/why-cant-canada-win-the-stanley-cup/', 'snippet': 'No Canadian team has won the N.H.L. championship since 1993. How unlikely is this 20-year drought? And is there anything in the league’s economics that might explain it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.H.L.’s conference finals will begin on Saturday, and fans in Boston, Chicago, Los Angeles and Pittsburgh can still dream of a Stanley Cup championship. But this year is an unhappy anniversary for America’s northerly neighbor. It is 20 years since the Montreal Canadiens defeated the Los Angeles Kings to win the 1993 Stanley Cup; no Canadian team has won the championship since.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Can’t Canada Win the Stanley Cup?', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Canadian Teams Can’t Seem to Win the Stanley Cup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T16:46:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4334b168-1668-543c-a565-25c3bbcacc50', 'word_count': 3703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4334b168-1668-543c-a565-25c3bbcacc50'}, {'abstract': '“Various Small Books” is a collection of pictures and essays showing how other artists have copied, parodied or otherwise paid homage to the work of Ed Ruscha.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/various-small-books-inspired-by-ed-ruscha-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“Various Small Books” is a collection of pictures and essays showing how other artists have copied, parodied or otherwise paid homage to the work of Ed Ruscha.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1970s, I purchased a half-dozen pocket-size paperback books that bore the name Ed Ruscha. I hadn’t heard of the California artist at the time — I just liked the look of his booklets. One was bound with ribbon; the rest were perfect binds. There were no cover pictures; only widely spaced titles typeset in a serif font that I also liked to use, called Stymie Bold. The cover designs were distinct yet understated, reminiscent of small-press poetry collections. Inside were photographic records of what each title promised — “Various Small Fires,” \u200a“Thirtyfour Parking Lots,” \u200a“Twentysix Gasoline Stations,” \u200a“Nine Swimming Pools,” \u200a“Crackers” (with text by Mason Williams on “How to Derive the Maximum Enjoyment From Crackers”) and “Babycakes” (with photographs of, yes, a baby and cakes). They were puzzling yet clearly ironic, prefiguring many books by today’s conceptual artists. The critical mass of decidedly mundane pictures gave them their allure. Marcel Duchamp’s “ready-mades” and Andy Warhol’s soup cans had already elevated everyday objects to the status of art, but Ruscha’s small books brought the ennobled vernacular back down to earth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1475, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/0602-bks-Heller4/0602-bks-Heller4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hidden in Plain Sight', 'kicker': 'Visuals', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T16:47:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cd0498e-72c1-576d-8ba1-d0aceb557c23', 'word_count': 1736, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cd0498e-72c1-576d-8ba1-d0aceb557c23'}, {'abstract': 'Twelve writers recall their most memorable experiences of summer reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/02/books/review/what-i-read-that-summer.html', 'snippet': 'Twelve writers recall their most memorable experiences of summer reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rites of summer are, by definition, fleeting: the summer romance, the summer job or vacation. Only the books seem to stick. Twelve writers recall their most memorable experiences of summer reading, proving perhaps that if you’re looking for an enduring summer romance, a good book might be your best bet.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02survey_span/02survey_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 125, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02survey_span/02survey_span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 125, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02survey_span/02survey_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02survey_span/02survey_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/review/02survey_span/02survey_span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What I Read That Summer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What I Read That Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T16:47:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a2789a5-1099-5470-be72-6b18387e18a6', 'word_count': 2966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a2789a5-1099-5470-be72-6b18387e18a6'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/the-gun-report-may-31-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can there be any doubt that we are becoming desensitized to gun violence? Look the first item in today’s report. A Chicago teenager who had moved to California—to escape gun violence!—was shot to death when he came home for summer break. The Chicago Tribune ran a story on the killing, but it merited just a few paragraphs at the top of a roundup of overnight shootings. It was awarded three paragraphs at a local news site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: May 31, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fresno County (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lakewood (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marine Corps Base Camp Lejeune (SC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Memphis (Tenn)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Jose (Calif)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T16:50:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe