ages/2013/05/19/fashion/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/19/fashion/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/19/fashion/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3/20130519_REGISTRY-slide-F7X3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reinventions on a Theme: Dinner Table Classics', 'kicker': 'Registry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-05-31T17:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARIANNE ROHRLICH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marianne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ROHRLICH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/5fb473e5-8ff4-5377-ae67-2f116a26d1c0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/5fb473e5-8ff4-5377-ae67-2f116a26d1c0'}, {'abstract': 'Volume one of the series from DC Comics is at No. 2 on the hardcover list.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/05/31/graphic-books-best-sellers-the-authority-saves-humanity/', 'snippet': 'Volume one of the series from DC Comics is at No. 2 on the hardcover list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At No. 2 on our hardcover graphic books best-seller list this week is Volume 1 of “The Authority,” from DC Comics. The 12 issues included in this edition were written by Warren Ellis and illustrated by Bryan Hitch. The series was originally bought in 1999, and captured some creative magic. The heroes (Jenny Sparks, who embodied the spirit of the 20th Century; Apollo and Midnighter, crime-fighting lovers who were analogues of Superman and Batman; Jack Hawksmoor, who could communicate with and control cities) were captivating, and the artwork was exquisite. The first year found the heroes fighting bigger and bigger threats as a team. The first four issues featured a nation of terrorists who were bred to have Superman-like powers and who were bent on destroying major world cities. The middle story arc featured an invasion from a parallel word. The final four issues featured the team confronting no less than the incomprehensible godlike creator of the solar system that wanted to cleanse the universe of humanity. (The heroes won!) Versions of these heroes continue to be featured in “Stormwatch,” a monthly series published by DC, but there are only sparks of the audaciousness that were once a hallmark of the series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/02authority/02authority-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 909, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/06/02/books/02authority/02authority-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 909}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/02authority/02authority-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/06/02/books/02authority/02authority-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1352, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_